window.DAILY_PAPERS = {"meta":{"updatedAt":"2026-11-28 09:50 (UTC+8)","totalDays":27,"totalPapers":197,"star5Total":46,"latestDate":"2026-11-25","schedule":"每日 08:30 (北京时间) GitHub Actions 自动抓取 arXiv","focusSummary":"图像/视频训练范式 · 少步蒸馏 · 强化学习 · 少步蒸馏强化 · 画质修复 · 打光光影 · 色彩迁移 · 表情人像"},"days":[{"date":"2026-11-25","count":28,"star5Count":4,"star4Count":8,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.34056","arxiv":"2611.34056v1","baseId":"2611.34056","title":"Understanding Diffusion Acceleration: Methods, Trade-Offs, and Open Questions","authors":["Xiaofeng Mao","Fu-yun Wang","Le Zhuo","Zhengkai Jiang","Shaohao Rui","Weijie Ma"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Diffusion and flow-based generative models support high-quality image, audio, and video synthesis. Sampling can require dozens to hundreds of denoiser evaluations, with each pass becoming more expensive as resolution, video length, or model size increases. This cost affects latency and energy use in interactive and edge applications. We examine four approaches to accelerating inference: distillation, consistency training, sampling methods, and denoiser design. We express training objectives, transition maps, and numerical updates in a common notation to compare changes to the learning target, the sampling trajectory, and the work performed within each denoiser pass. Published image and video benchmarks illustrate the quality--cost trade-offs of one- and few-step generation, the interaction between solvers and timestep schedules, the effect of feature caching, and the difference between first-frame latency and sustained streaming throughput. We close with open problems in visual-native architectures and post-compression distillation, solver-aware few-step training, and semantic trajectory alignment with factorized distribution matching.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34056v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34056v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34418","arxiv":"2611.34418v1","baseId":"2611.34418","title":"SPGSR: A Small Pixel-Space Generator for One-Step Super-Resolution","authors":["Yun Yue","Luming Liang","Yiqi Zhong"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"In one-step super-resolution (SR), vision-only models extract semantic cues directly from the low-resolution (LR) image, keeping those cues spatially aligned with the structures they help restore. Recent models achieve high restoration quality with generators of up to 1.4 billion parameters. Given how much structure the LR image already provides, could a much smaller generator achieve comparable quality? We introduce SPGSR, a vision-only framework that adds a learned RGB correction to the LR image. A frozen DINOv2 guides prediction, and a lightweight block refines the correction locally. SPGSR achieves higher PSNR/SSIM and lower LPIPS/DISTS than VOSR, a leading vision-only SR method, across three benchmarks. Its $31.8\\mathrm{M}$-parameter generator is just $6.5\\%$ the size of VOSR-0.5B's. SPGSR also achieves competitive quality against T2I-based SR methods. SPGSR's generator uses only $0.60\\%$ of the HR pixels used to train and distill VOSR's generator. For $512\\times512$ outputs, SPGSR achieves $2.80\\times$ and $3.35\\times$ GPU-pipeline speedups over VOSR-0.5B and VOSR-1.4B, respectively.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34418v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34418v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34940","arxiv":"2611.34940v1","baseId":"2611.34940","title":"LuxAvatar: Real-Time Relightable Head Avatars via Causal Neural Shading of 3D Intrinsic Proxies","authors":["Yipengjing Sun","Chenyang Wang","Boyao Zhou","Jiapeng Zhu","Ka Leong Cheng","Shengping Zhang"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影","表情与人像"],"tags":["打光与光影","表情与人像","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影 · 表情与人像】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"We present LuxAvatar, a feed-forward framework for real-time head avatar animation and relighting from a single portrait. To achieve both 3D controllability and complex facial light transport, we factorize avatar synthesis into an animatable 3D intrinsic Gaussian proxy and a causal autoregressive neural shader. This proxy disentangles canonical geometry and reflectance attributes, rapidly rasterizing deformed Gaussians into view- and motion-consistent PBR buffers. Conditioned on these buffers, our neural shader formulates forward rendering as a conditional video diffusion process, bypassing explicit PBR computation to synthesize realistic shading effects under target illumination. We achieve real-time streaming through heterogeneous distillation from a pretrained multi-step bidirectional relighting teacher to a two-step causal autoregressive neural shader, inheriting broad synthetic lighting priors while minimizing conditioning and decoding latency. To further specialize these priors to human heads, we construct a hybrid relighting dataset for domain-specific fine-tuning, capturing authentic facial dynamics and physically grounded light transport. Extensive experiments show that our framework enables 3D-controllable, real-time portrait animation and relighting, outperforming state-of-the-art methods in visual fidelity, motion control, and lighting accuracy.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34940v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34940v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34941","arxiv":"2611.34941v1","baseId":"2611.34941","title":"Improving and Expanding Video Editing Capabilities Without Videos","authors":["Ji Xie","Yanhao Jia","Xiangpeng Yang","Martin Q. Ma","Sihan Xu","Erik Cambria"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":["打光与光影","画质修复"],"tags":["强化学习","打光与光影","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 项目方向【打光与光影 · 画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Image editing models can follow a wide range of instructions, but video editing models still lag behind, limited in part by the difficulty of collecting paired source and edited videos. Is it possible to transfer advanced image editing capabilities to video editors without new paired videos? We introduce Image-to-Video Transfer (I2VT), which improves pretrained video editors and expands them to new editing tasks using an image editor. It enables efficient post-training with no architectural changes and no new videos, using only individual images. Our key idea is to let the video editor role-play as an image editor: it edits an image as a one-frame video, with the image editor guiding the video editor's predictions at each denoising step. Although the video editor sees only single images during training, at test time it applies the improved and expanded capabilities to whole video clips while preserving temporal consistency. To push the boundaries of video editing, we introduce OpenVE++, an extension of OpenVE-Bench that adds four new editing tasks useful for video production, including action changes, expression changes, body movements, and scene relighting. Our experiments across three different video editors show that I2VT improves existing editing capabilities and expands them to new tasks, and our models achieve state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance on both OpenVE-Bench and OpenVE++.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34941v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34941v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33861","arxiv":"2611.33861v1","baseId":"2611.33861","title":"OmniMeld: Enhancing Audio-Visual Understanding via Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation","authors":["Daili Hua","Yifan Dai","Bohan Zeng","Yuran Wang","Zihao Wu","Chengzhuo Tong"],"authorCount":11,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Audio-visual understanding requires perceptual grounding, temporal understanding, and semantic inference. Multi-teacher distillation can consolidate these capabilities, but direct probability matching does not explicitly capture teacher prediction changes across audio-visual conditions. We introduce OmniMeld, a multi-teacher on-policy distillation method that integrates domain expertise through modality-response-guided distribution matching. We train four teachers covering grounding and correspondence, temporal dynamics and event structure, causal and inferential reasoning, and contextual and holistic understanding. Each sample is routed to its corresponding teacher for supervision conditioned on student-generated answer prefixes. We derive transport costs from teacher prediction changes over shared candidate tokens across audio-visual conditions, using optimal transport to match student predictions to those of the teacher under complete audio-visual input. We also construct OmniMeld-20K for domain-specialized teacher training. The dataset spans four capability domains, with responses structured into evidence, reasoning, and answer sections and verified through consistency checks and media-based review. Experiments on audio-visual and general video understanding benchmarks demonstrate competitive teacher performance among similarly sized open-source models. The student improves over its base model across all evaluated benchmarks and surpasses the teachers' base model on several benchmarks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33861v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33861v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33895","arxiv":"2611.33895v1","baseId":"2611.33895","title":"TWIST: Teacher-Weighted Initial-Noise Spherical Transform for Quality-Preserving Diversity in Distilled Diffusion Models","authors":["Shayan Baghayi-Nejad","Jeongsol Kim","Jong Chul Ye"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Timestep-distilled diffusion models generate high-quality images in one or a few steps, but often suffer from reduced mode coverage and limited diversity. Recent methods improve diversity by optimizing the initial noise at inference time, requiring costly backpropagation through the denoiser. Moreover, their diversity objectives typically repel generated samples without accounting for how the corresponding teacher model distributes its outputs. We introduce Teacher-Weighted Initial-noise Spherical Transform (TWIST), a lightweight network that transforms the low-frequency components of initial noise to improve diversity without inference-time optimization. TWIST uses a teacher-guided determinantal point process (DPP) objective to align the feature-space diversity of student samples with that of the teacher. By amortizing noise optimization into a single network, TWIST eliminates inference-time backpropagation while preserving the efficiency of few-step generation. On SDXL-Turbo, TWIST matches the diversity of MoNO at less than 25% of its inference cost, while achieving higher recall and better PickScore, ImageReward, and CLIPScore under matched compute budgets. On SDXL-Lightning, it achieves comparable recall at lower cost without the ImageReward degradation observed with MoNO.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33895v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33895v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34101","arxiv":"2611.34101v1","baseId":"2611.34101","title":"AlignPre: Skill-Aligned Post-Training for Latent-Free Generative Precipitation Nowcasting","authors":["Demin Yu","Kenghong Lin","Xutao Li"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】","abstract":"Precipitation nowcasting requires accurate short-term predictions under rapidly evolving spatiotemporal dynamics. Generative diffusion models can produce detailed and competitive radar forecasts by modeling conditional distributions. However, most existing approaches rely on separately trained components, such as deterministic branches or latent compression modules. More fundamentally, distribution matching alone fails to explicitly optimize operational forecast skill. We propose AlignPre, a streamlined pixel-space nowcasting framework that bridges this gap via skill-aligned post-training (SAPT). AlignPre employs an end-to-end flow-matching forecaster with a hierarchical spatiotemporal architecture for distribution matching and direct radar-field synthesis, without latent compression. During post-training, SAPT exploits the analytic structure of forecast verification scores to construct a differentiable objective. By backpropagating gradients of this objective from radar fields to the generator, SAPT turns forecast verification into direct training feedback. Across three real-world benchmarks spanning different forecast horizons, AlignPre consistently surpasses state-of-the-art deterministic and generative baselines in forecast skill, demonstrating that aligning generative modeling with domain-specific verification provides an effective paradigm for meteorological forecasting.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34101v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34101v1","arxivCats":["cs.LG","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34141","arxiv":"2611.34141v1","baseId":"2611.34141","title":"Spe3DUp: Faithful Trajectory Distillation for Fast Image-to-3D Generation","authors":["Liangchao Xu","Jiaye Fu","Youtian Lin","Feihu Zhang","Yao Yao"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Recent 3D generative models can produce high-quality meshes with complex structures and fine geometric details, with broad applications in games, film, and digital design. However, high-quality generation typically relies on long multi-step sampling processes that repeatedly evaluate large networks, leading to long inference times. At the same time, reference-driven tasks such as game-asset reconstruction and product digitization demand close correspondence to the input image: generated geometry must faithfully reconstruct the specific reference object. Acceleration must therefore reduce sampling computation while preserving the model's ability to reconstruct that object. To enable fast and faithful 3D generation, we introduce Spe3DUp, a coarse-to-fine framework based on Faithful Trajectory Distillation (FTD). FTD directly supervises few-step students with the teacher's input-conditioned denoising trajectories to preserve its generation behavior. We apply FTD separately to coarse geometry generation and geometric refinement, compressing both sampling processes while maintaining correspondence to the reference input. We further accelerate the complete mesh-generation pipeline with efficient geometry decoding and parallel remeshing. Experiments show that Spe3DUp maintains geometric quality and input correspondence close to those of the teacher, accelerates the coarse and refinement DiTs by 10.0x and 19.9x, respectively, and achieves a 7.7x end-to-end speedup over the original teacher pipeline.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34141v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34141v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34226","arxiv":"2611.34226v1","baseId":"2611.34226","title":"LiveRelight: Sub-Second Global Illumination Editing for Gaussian Interiors","authors":["Noah Rothenberger","Dor Verbin","Serge Belongie","Bernhard Kerbl"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Relighting reconstructed 3D scenes remains challenging due to the complex nature of light transport: slow or approximate visual feedback restricts the editor's ability to fine-tune the results to satisfaction. We present a hybrid relighting approach that enables interactive, high-fidelity updates of direct and indirect indoor illumination without per-edit optimization. Our approach permits the editor to make a wide range of relighting edits at room-scale, with near-instant feedback. Our key insight is to precompute geometric light exchange rather than illumination responses, retaining an inexpensive transport solve that accommodates changes in emission and surface reflectance. We evaluate direct illumination on reconstructed 2D Gaussians using visibility-aware sampling of one-sided emitters, while propagating diffuse interreflection through sparse radiosity on a triangle proxy. A sparse projection fitted through Gaussian alpha compositing transfers the proxy solution to the reconstruction. Our formulation further supports fast fixture transformations and emitter insertion by recomputing light source injection. Across eight relighting conditions of our synthetic test suite, our sub-second edits improve relighting fidelity over prior path-traced editing by an average of 5\\,dB PSNR, while reducing editing latency by up to three orders of magnitude. Qualitative results further demonstrate realistic, interactive relighting of real-world captures.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34226v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34226v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34446","arxiv":"2611.34446v1","baseId":"2611.34446","title":"PreserveReward: Reliable Consistency Reward Modeling for Image Editing via Dual-Teacher On-Policy Self-Distillation","authors":["Xueheng Li","Yong Liu","Wen Xue","Shi-Yu Tian","Zhuofan Xia","Chengjun Xie"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】","abstract":"Instruction-guided image editing has a wide range of applications, yet existing models may inadvertently alter content that should remain unchanged. Reliable consistency evaluation is therefore essential for assessing content preservation and providing reward signals for Reinforcement Learning (RL). However, existing reward models may overlook subtle visual changes or fail to distinguish necessary modifications from unintended alterations, limiting the reliability of consistency assessment. To address this challenge, we propose PreserveReward, a consistency reward model for image editing that integrates visual On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD) to enhance fine-grained visual perception and consistency assessment. PreserveReward learns from reference preservation scores through a regression-based scoring loss and employs two complementary teacher views for self-distillation. The local teacher uses matched crops to identify subtle visual changes, while the joint teacher combines local details with global context to determine whether these changes align with the editing intent. To improve the reliability of distillation supervision, we calibrate teacher score distributions via minimum-KL projection under reference-score error constraints and adaptively select teacher process supervision based on gradient alignment with the scoring objective, mitigating scoring bias and optimization conflicts. Extensive experiments across multiple benchmarks demonstrate that PreserveReward achieves strong alignment with human preference on non-edited content preservation and generalizes well across diverse editing scenarios.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34446v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34446v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34795","arxiv":"2611.34795v1","baseId":"2611.34795","title":"CIRKDV2: Cross-Image Relational Knowledge Distillation with Contextual Modeling for Efficient Semantic Segmentation","authors":["Chuanguang Yang","Yu Wang","Chengqing Yu","Xinqiang Yu","Weilun Feng","Yuqi Li"],"authorCount":15,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Existing Knowledge Distillation (KD) methods for semantic segmentation often guide the student to mimic the teacher's structured information generated from an individual image. However, they ignore the global semantic relations across various images that are valuable for KD. This paper proposes Cross-Image Relational KD V2 (CIRKDV2), which focuses on transferring structured pixel-to-pixel, pixel-to-region, and channel-to-channel relations modeled from global images in the dataset. A large teacher network often constructs a well-structured feature space in terms of global contextual dependencies. CIRKDV2 makes the student mimic the better structured semantic relations of the teacher, thus improving the feature representational capability. Beyond relational distillation, we further propose contextual feature distillation by using context aggregation to augment student pixel embeddings to match the semantic level with the teacher. This avoids the semantic gap problem between the teacher and student occurred in traditional feature distillation studies. Experimental results on Cityscapes, ADE20K, Pascal VOC, and COCO-Stuff-164K demonstrate that CIRKDV2 outperforms the state-of-the-art semantic segmentation distillation methods by large margins across various network architectures. Our code and pre-trained models are publicly available at https://github.com/winycg/CIRKD.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34795v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34795v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34093","arxiv":"2611.34093v1","baseId":"2611.34093","title":"Exploring the Design Factors of Pixel-space Diffusion","authors":["Mang Ning","Le Zhang","Albert Ali Salah","Itir Onal Ertugrul","Jintao Zhang"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":52,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"In this paper, we introduce EiT by exploring the key design factors of pixel-space diffusion from three perspectives: model architecture, representation, and frequency domain. Inspired by Denoising Autoencoders (DAE) which perform denoising through a bottleneck, we construct a U-shaped pixel diffusion transformer that performs explicit compression along the channel dimension. In terms of representation, we find that both pixel and latent diffusion transformers exhibit a three-stage representation dynamics across layers and timesteps: denoising/local feature expansion, basis lift-up, and posterior projection. Moreover, earlier denoising and a longer basis lift-up stage are associated with better generation, which EiT naturally achieves through its channel bottleneck and increased network depth. From the frequency perspective, we establish the equivalence between pixel-space and frequency-space diffusion when keeping the network operating in pixel space. On ImageNet $256\\times256$ and $512\\times512$, EiT consistently outperforms JiT (Just image Transformers) in generation quality. Specifically, our EiT achieves better FID than JiT: 1.61 vs. 1.85 at $256\\times256$, and 1.73 vs. 1.99 at $512\\times512$. Remarkably, EiT can be seamlessly applied to video pixel diffusion by simply extending the patch size to temporal dimension and outperforms video latent diffusion in FVD evaluated on Kinetics-600 (resolution 256), under comparable total network parameters. Code is available at https://github.com/forever208/EiT","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34093v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34093v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33636","arxiv":"2611.33636v1","baseId":"2611.33636","title":"What Is Redundant at High Resolution? Efficient Attention and Feed-Forward Design for All-in-One Image Restoration","authors":["Dongheon Lee","DongJae Lee","Sewhan Chun","Chanseul Cho","Youngwook Kim","Youngmin Ro"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Multi-scale architectures are widely adopted in all-in-one image restoration (AIO-IR) to capture larger-scale context at low-resolution stages and refine details at high-resolution (HR) stages. However, processing spatially large feature maps at HR stages incurs substantial computational costs and memory traffic, making efficient HR processing challenging. To address this challenge, we propose High-resolution Global-Local Restoration (HiGLR), an efficient multi-scale AIO-IR network grounded in an analysis of spatial redundancy in self-attention and convolutional feed-forward networks (ConvFFNs). Specifically, this analysis identifies three key redundancies: per-query readout at the finest-scale stage, key routing across neighboring queries at intermediate stages, and spatial mixing in the expanded feature spaces of ConvFFNs. Accordingly, HiGLR replaces costly self-attention at the finest-scale stage with a learned input-independent kernel. At intermediate stages, we propose a Block-Routed Hybrid Attention, which combines coarse-scale dense attention with fine-scale block-sparse attention over value residuals relative to coarse summaries, and shares routing indices across neighboring queries. We further introduce a Dual-Space FFN that performs spatial mixing in a compact space and pointwise nonlinear transformation in an expanded space, enabling efficient kernel fusion. Combining these designs, HiGLR achieves state-of-the-art performance while delivering up to a $4.24\\times$ inference speedup over existing methods.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33636v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33636v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33809","arxiv":"2611.33809v1","baseId":"2611.33809","title":"Probabilistic Monocular Biomechanical Reconstruction via Guided Flow Matching","authors":["R. James Cotton","Fabian Sinz"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Reconstructing 3D joint kinematics from a single video is ill-posed due to depth and stature ambiguity, limb occlusion, and missing uncertainty estimates. We introduce the Biomechanical Flow Model (BFM), a continuous-time flow-matching prior over subject anthropometry, anatomical joint-angle trajectories, and virtual markers, trained on multi-camera clinical recordings from 565 participants and steered toward monocular 2D keypoints at test time without paired video training. In a head-to-head comparison on the same clinical trials against a state-of-the-art deterministic trajectory optimizer fitting the identical skeleton, BFM achieves comparable joint-angle accuracy across walking and timed-up-and-go (TUG). Across held-out control, prosthetic, and neurologic participants, BFM reaches 3.91 deg and 4.97 deg mean absolute joint-angle error on walking and TUG while providing credible intervals that bridge temporal and whole-limb occlusions. Where keypoints are hidden, the intervals widen with the error and keep their coverage near nominal. Supplying individual anthropometric calibration removes most monocular pelvis depth error, whereas testing on out-of-distribution pediatric crouch gait produces biased, overconfident extension predictions, highlighting the need for pathologically diverse movement priors.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33809v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33809v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34860","arxiv":"2611.34860v1","baseId":"2611.34860","title":"DocDUR-Net: Degradation-Aware Unified Document Image Restoration","authors":["Haowei Xu","Peirong Zhang","Xuhan Zheng","Lianwen Jin"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Captured document images often suffer from diverse degradations caused by uncontrolled imaging conditions, which reduce readability and obscure fine text structures. Single-degradation restoration methods are usually trained and deployed for individual degradation types, and therefore require model selection before restoration. Unified restoration models alleviate the need to maintain multiple restoration networks by sharing one network across tasks, but representative unified document image restoration methods still depend on external task selection. This dependency limits automatic document image restoration. We propose DocDUR-Net, a degradation-aware unified restoration network that derives restoration guidance directly from the input image. It introduces a Degradation Perception Module (DPM) to extract degradation representations and predict degradation weights, and Degradation Prior-guided Adaptive Feature Fusion (DP-AFF) to combine learnable degradation priors with degradation features extracted from the input. The resulting guidance is injected into a shared restoration backbone, enabling adaptive restoration without external task selection. DocDUR-Net also supports a manual mode with explicit task selection. Experiments on document image restoration benchmarks show that DocDUR-Net achieves the best average PSNR of 29.405 dB and SSIM of 0.960 among unified methods while remaining competitive with single-degradation methods.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34860v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34860v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34912","arxiv":"2611.34912v1","baseId":"2611.34912","title":"LiveVAE: Causal Window Attention Makes Efficient Streaming Video VAE","authors":["Jiachen An","Sijie Zhao","Le Li","Hongyu Liu","Zhiyuan Li","Tianyu Yang"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"While video diffusion models have grown faster and increasingly streamable, the video VAE remains a primary latency bottleneck across interactive generation and editing pipelines. Existing VAEs and their acceleration methods rely mainly on heavy convolutions or full frame attention, and most focus only on decoding, leaving encoding costly for streaming conditional generation and editing. This raises a natural question: Do video VAE encoding and decoding actually require such global spatiotemporal modeling? By analyzing the effective receptive fields of pretrained video VAEs, we find that both encoding and decoding a patch rely almost entirely on its spatial and temporal neighbors. To exploit this strong locality, we introduce LiveVAE, a streaming encoder and decoder built on causal window attention, which cuts per frame compute while keeping the temporal KV cache bounded for streaming. Decoupled encoder/decoder distillation followed by joint finetuning preserves the pretrained latent space, making LiveVAE a drop-in replacement for the original VAE. On MiniMax-H3, LiveVAE achieves 5.2-6.2x faster encoding and 3.0-3.3x faster decoding than the official VAE with higher PSNR. Paired with our dedicated inference infrastructure, LiveVAE pushes the speedup to 13.4x with almost no quality loss. Code and models will be released.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34912v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34912v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33719","arxiv":"2611.33719v1","baseId":"2611.33719","title":"Understanding Mode Collapse in Diffusion Models under Classifier-Free Guidance","authors":["Daniel Pfrommer","Chenyang Yuan","Frank Permenter","Ali Jadbabaie"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"The use of classifier-free guidance in diffusion and flow models has proven critical to their empirical success, as it allows for the controllable generation of lower-temperature and higher-fidelity samples. Despite its widespread use, classifier-free guidance remains both empirically and theoretically poorly understood, owing to the difficulty of solving the associated transport equations for arbitrary normalizing flows. In this paper, we observe that the effects of classifier-free guidance arise from a mismatch between the representation capabilities of the conditional and unconditional distributions, wherein the conditional distribution exhibits higher complexity and fidelity. We propose an analytical model to capture this observation: the conditional distribution is a Gaussian mixture whose means lie on an ellipsoid, and the unconditional distribution is a single Gaussian approximating the mixture. The flows arising from this mixture are closed under scaling, enabling us to derive analytic closed-form solutions for the guided distribution at all noise scales and guidance levels. Our analytic solution shows that classifier-free guidance induces (a) a shift along a mean-axis direction and (b) a persistent skew toward, and concentration around, a singular point corresponding to the conditional sample starting from zero noise. We validate our theoretical predictions on pretrained, large-scale image diffusion models.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33719v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33719v1","arxivCats":["cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33874","arxiv":"2611.33874v1","baseId":"2611.33874","title":"X-Matte: VAE-Free Pixel-Space Flow Matching for Single-Step Image Matting","authors":["Zanyi Wang","Jiaang Li","Dengyang Jiang","Ziru Wang","Chengzu Li","Davit Abrahamyan"],"authorCount":9,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"Pre-trained generative models offer rich visual priors for image matting, but their adaptation often relies on variational autoencoders (VAEs), whose spatial compression can degrade fine alpha details and introduce additional inference overhead. To address these limitations, we propose X-Matte, a VAE-free pixel-space flow matching framework for single-step image matting. By eliminating latent compression, X-Matte formulates the RGB-to-alpha transition as a continuous flow directly in pixel space. We adopt trajectory-wide x-prediction with transition-aware alpha supervision, enabling the model to learn from intermediate states while directly predicting the target alpha matte in a single forward pass. This design avoids both VAE encoding and decoding and iterative sampling. Extensive experiments demonstrate performance competitive with state-of-the-art trimap-free matting methods on P3M benchmarks, together with promising zero-shot generalization to RWP-636 and AIM-500. Moreover, X-Matte achieves up to a 9.4x inference speedup over a constructed single-step latent counterpart at high resolution, demonstrating the effectiveness of VAE-free pixel-space modeling for accurate and efficient image matting.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33874v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33874v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34032","arxiv":"2611.34032v1","baseId":"2611.34032","title":"Counter-DECEIVR: Counterfactual Explanations for Deepfake Detectors using Detector-Guided Rectified Flow Inversion","authors":["Mariano V. Ntrougkas","Vasileios Mezaris","Ioannis Patras"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"Deep-learning-generated media are increasingly passed off as real, driving an arms race between image generators and deepfake detectors. Trust in the latter is limited by their often black-box nature; and, while region-based explanations can sometimes be extracted, these become less useful as generation quality improves. Counterfactual examples, which show the minimal change that flips a detector's decision, reveal what evidence drives a verdict, helping analysts decide whether to trust it. This work introduces Counter-DECEIVR (DEtector-guided reCtifiEd flow InVeRsion for counterfactuals), a novel training-free framework that generates counterfactual examples. Counter-DECEIVR steers latents in the velocity field of a rectified flow model using gradients from off-the-shelf deepfake detectors, without training a generator or adapting the detector; unlike prior methods, it requires no per-instance or per-detector fitting and no diffusion training on the target data. It also leverages the detector's explanation maps to concentrate editing to salient regions, turning low-level feature importance into localised counterfactual edits. A single parameter controls the strength of the edits. Across two deepfake detectors and two widely used forensics datasets, Counter-DECEIVR outperforms prior counterfactual generation methods, including training-based ones. Code will be made available upon acceptance.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34032v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34032v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34046","arxiv":"2611.34046v1","baseId":"2611.34046","title":"End-to-End Image-Goal Navigation for Agile Quadrotors via Recurrent State Distillation","authors":["Simone Nascivera","Ismail Geles","Davide Scaramuzza"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Image-goal navigation asks a robot to reach the viewpoint of a target photograph without a map or goal pose. In three dimensions, this requires not only recognizing the goal but also estimating 3D displacement and generating continuous control actions to approach and settle at the target view. This is difficult because the same visual difference between current and goal image can require very different actions depending on the robot's velocity, yet monocular images alone reveal neither metric scale nor body motion. As a result, existing image-goal policies are limited to planar action spaces and rely on a state estimator for flight control. Policies that map directly from images to low-level control commands have been demonstrated for agile flight, but not toward goal images in unseen environments. We present the first image-goal navigation policy that directly commands thrust and angular rates for agile quadrotor flight in unseen 3D environments, without relying on state estimation (e.g., SLAM, VO, or SfM) or a separate velocity controller. We train a recurrent vision-based policy through a four-stage pipeline that combines geometric and motion supervision with privileged-teacher distillation and end-to-end reinforcement learning fine-tuning. On 1000 held-out flights in unseen environments, the policy achieves an 82.3% success rate with a mean terminal position error of 0.10m across successful flights. Deployed zero-shot on a real quadrotor, the policy reaches an 84% success rate over 25 trials in an unseen indoor environment.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34046v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34046v1","arxivCats":["cs.RO","cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34907","arxiv":"2611.34907v1","baseId":"2611.34907","title":"Agentic LongLive: Asynchronous Agentic Memory for Long Video Generation","authors":["Shaoteng Liu","Shuai Yang","Wei Huang","Luozhou Wang","Song Han","Yukang Chen"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Real-time autoregressive video generators stream minutes of video and accept new prompts on the fly, but struggle to keep people, objects, and places consistent over a long interactive story. Agent systems provide such memory for offline multi-shot generation, yet they read the whole script in advance, plan each shot before generating it, and regenerate failed shots, which a live stream does not allow. We present Agentic LongLive, an asynchronous agentic memory for long video generation. A memory agent on separate GPUs reads only the prompts revealed so far, never the generated video, and keeps a text state and evolving images for every person, object, and scene. From this entity memory it composes a global image and a per-shot memory image, which an auxiliary GPU prefills into the generator's attention sink before the next shot starts, and writes entity cards for the prompts that name them, so the video never waits for memory and video errors are not written back. A small language model, trained with reinforcement learning and an image reward calibrated using downstream video outcomes, decides what to remember before the next prompt is known. On LongStory-Online, a new benchmark of 25 stories (857 shots) revealed one shot at a time, Agentic LongLive improves persistent-state accuracy of LongLive-2.0, its base generator, by 26.3 points, protagonist identity by 44.9, and revisited-place consistency by 40.4, at about 1% extra generation time on H100. The full system streams faster than real time on one 8xB200 node with the generator in BF16 (34.3 FPS), keeps image quality stable over 14-minute stories, and improves identity on two MiniMax-H3 generators, one of which, LongLive-H3, also runs in real time with it. To our knowledge, it is the first real-time video generator with text and image memory kept by an LLM agent.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34907v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34907v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34929","arxiv":"2611.34929v1","baseId":"2611.34929","title":"High-Fidelity Image Matting with Pixel-Space Diffusion Transformers","authors":["Baihan Yang","Xin Lin","Yufan Wei","Baiang Li","Yuheng Liu","Juanxi Tian"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"Diffusion models have shown strong potential for image matting by transferring generative priors learned from large-scale pretraining. However, existing approaches largely rely on latent diffusion models, whose compressed representations and VAE-based reconstruction introduce a mismatch with the high-frequency precision required for matting. We present PixelMatte, a framework that adapts pretrained pixel-space diffusion transformers for high-fidelity image matting. We show that strong image-conditioned matting capabilities can be acquired even from a pure text-to-image model without paired image-to-image pretraining. Our approach reuses the backbone's native patch embedding to jointly process the input image, trimap, and noisy alpha matte as spatially aligned tokens, enabling direct pixel-space prediction without additional condition encoders or adapters. With pixel-accurate synthetic supervision and real-image fine-tuning, PixelMatte predicts alpha mattes in a single forward pass. Two lightweight calibration modules, LayerShift and High-Pass Refiner, further improve regional opacity accuracy and boundary fidelity. Experiments across four benchmarks and two distinct pixel diffusion backbones demonstrate substantial improvements over existing methods, reducing SAD and gradient errors by 24-44% relative to RenderMatte on P3M-500-NP and AM-2K, while achieving competitive performance on AIM-500 and RenderMatte-2K. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of pixel-space generative priors for precision-sensitive image matting. Project page: https://white-yc.github.io/pixelmatte/.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34929v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34929v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34064","arxiv":"2611.34064v1","baseId":"2611.34064","title":"SPARC: Geometry-Guided Attention Pruning for Long-Horizon Camera-Controlled Video Generation","authors":["Zesong Yang","Weikai Chen","Liyuan Cui","Yuan Li","Jiaxin Huang","Lutao Jiang"],"authorCount":14,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"We present SPARC, a geometry-guided attention pruning framework that turns the native self-attention of pretrained video diffusion transformers into a unified mechanism for camera control and persistent scene memory. Our key insight is that attention connectivity can simultaneously serve as geometric conditioning for camera control and spatial addressing for historical memory retrieval. Motivated by the asymmetric contextual requirements of reconstructing observed content and synthesizing novel regions, SPARC restricts observed tokens to geometrically corresponding historical tokens and local spatiotemporal context, while preserving dense attention for unobserved regions. Correspondence-aware attention biasing and order-invariant historical positioning further enable stable retrieval across long temporal gaps. Without introducing additional learnable control or memory modules, SPARC achieves precise camera adherence and faithful scene revisitation while substantially reducing attention computation. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of aggressive attention pruning in improving camera adherence, revisit consistency, and visual fidelity, with an inference speedup of over 50x when combined with four-step distillation. Project page is available at https://zju3dv.github.io/SPARC.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34064v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34064v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34944","arxiv":"2611.34944v1","baseId":"2611.34944","title":"AnyCam: Anything Can Represent Camera for Video Generation","authors":["Weijie Lyu","Zhixin Shu","Yuntian Ke","Ming-Hsuan Yang"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Camera-controlled video generation re-renders a video along a target camera trajectory, yet moving the camera around an object, the most natural way to change the viewpoint of an object-centric scene, remains difficult. Existing methods condition either on camera parameters, which are scale-ambiguous in monocular video, or on explicit geometry such as warped point clouds or depth, which breaks down under large viewpoint changes. We present AnyCam, a method that represents the camera with an anchor object in the scene: we reconstruct it in 3D and render it along the target trajectory, a camera signal that is scale-aware, view-complete and easy to specify relative to the object. A video diffusion model transfers the content and dynamics of the source video to the new viewpoint. Its H-Concat layout aligns source and target frames in time, so the model can be distilled into AnyCam-AR, a streaming few-step generator for on-the-fly camera control. Both models are trained on paired videos with pixel-aligned anchor renderings from multi-camera videos and static-scene captures. Experiments on diverse datasets and trajectories show that AnyCam performs favorably against camera-controlled video generation and 3D reconstruction methods in camera control and visual quality.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34944v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34944v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34021","arxiv":"2611.34021v1","baseId":"2611.34021","title":"Rethinking Unsupervised Cross-Modal Homography Estimation:Spatially Aware Representations and Geometric Consistency Learning","authors":["Zhuoran Yang","Junchen Yu","Runmin Zhang","Linshan Li","Chenghao Zhang","Hui-liang Shen"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Unsupervised cross-modal homography estimation is challenging because appearance differences obscure shared geometric structures. Separating modality transfer from homography estimation helps reduce these differences, but accurate alignment still requires effective geometric representations and consistency constraints on the two subtasks. We propose GeoCo (Geometry-Aware and Consistency-Constrained Homography Estimation), a framework that addresses these two requirements without cross-modal transformation labels. Its homography estimator, RVHomo, adapts hierarchical features from a frozen vision foundation model (VFM) and fuses them with local CNN features to capture structural context and spatial detail. Two geometric consistency constraints encourage modality transfer to preserve geometry and homography predictions to remain consistent under synthetic geometric transformations. Through two-stage progressive distillation, we further obtain GeoCo-D, which uses the lightweight RVHomo-D estimator. An intermediate model first learns to perform cross-modal matching without modality transfer, then transfers prediction and feature knowledge to the student, eliminating online VFM extraction. Experiments on GoogleMap, OPT-SAR, FSD, and VIS-IR show that GeoCo reduces mean average corner error by 17.4-46.7% relative to the best-performing baseline on each dataset. GeoCo-D also outperforms all compared baselines while using 98.5% fewer parameters and 70.4% fewer FLOPs than the complete GeoCo teacher.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34021v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34021v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.34782","arxiv":"2611.34782v1","baseId":"2611.34782","title":"Generation as Reverse Encoding","authors":["Cheng Shi","Sibei Yang","Hanzhong Guo","Yipeng Qin","Pietro Lio","Yizhou Yu"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Pretrained visual representations accelerate generative learning by abstracting away appearance and spatial detail. Yet the same abstraction makes the return to pixels far harder, shifting much of the burden onto a larger decoder. We ask why the representation-to-pixel path should be learned independently when the encoder already provides a learned pixel-to-semantics path (the semantic encoding path). We introduce RevFlow, based on the principle of generation as reverse encoding. RevFlow uses intermediate representations of a frozen encoder as successive generative targets, first generating deep semantic representations, then shallow representations that retain appearance and spatial detail, before decoding to pixels. To learn these transitions without training a separate generator for each stage, we develop HyC-DiT, a unified generative model with a standard DiT backbone shared across stages and stage-specific condition injection. We further show that native matching in semantic representation space enables few-step generation. RevFlow accelerates both learning and sampling. On ImageNet $256 \\times 256$, RevFlow reaches 1.96 FID at 690M and 1.74 FID at 1.55B after only 5 training epochs. It also achieves 30.25 dB reconstruction PSNR with an 89M decoder. After a short native-matching adaptation phase, RevFlow-XL achieves 2.85 FID using a single sampling step in each stage. Our code repository is at https://github.com/ChengShiest/RevFlow","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34782v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34782v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33886","arxiv":"2611.33886v1","baseId":"2611.33886","title":"DeltaRAE: Derivative Tokenization for Long Video Generation","authors":["Mingzhuo Yu","Shengcao Cao","Chengzhi Mao","Yong Cheng","Di Jiang","Lijun Yu"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":42,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Latent video generators are trained on clips of a few seconds, and much of this limit lies in their tokenizers, which spend as many tokens on a static frame as on a moving one and need future frames that do not exist during generation. We introduce DeltaRAE, a causal representation autoencoder for video. Its derivative tokenization encodes periodic anchor frames in full and all remaining frames with a few tokens each, and its causal encoder and decoder read a frozen pretrained image encoder. On UCF-101 and Kinetics-600, a standard diffusion transformer trained on DeltaRAE latents is competitive with state-of-the-art video tokenizers. DeltaRAE halves the tokenizer GFLOPs on 16-frame clips relative to keeping the same number of patch tokens for every frame, and it represents a minute of video with 18.8x fewer tokens. The tokenizer trains end to end on one-minute videos, and in video prediction a generator on its latents produces a full minute of video. The same generator collapses within 15 seconds in most videos when the tokenizer has the same token budget but no pretrained encoder.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33886v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33886v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33917","arxiv":"2611.33917v1","baseId":"2611.33917","title":"Event-WAM: Learning to Act with Event-Derived Futures","authors":["Jongseong Bae","Hyunwoo Kim","Yeongin Lee","Eubin So","Ha Young Kim"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-25","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"World Action Models (WAMs) leverage future visual modeling for robotic action learning, with recent studies exploring alternative or complementary objectives beyond conventional RGB prediction. We introduce Event-WAM, which incorporates event-derived visual changes as auxiliary future supervision alongside RGB. Event-WAM constructs temporally structured event representations from RGB demonstrations and encodes them using a frozen pretrained RGB video VAE, without training an event-specific VAE. Joint RGB--Event latent flow matching provides appearance- and change-oriented supervision without requiring event observations or explicit future generation at inference. Across simulated benchmarks, Event-WAM maintains comparable performance under standard conditions, while robustness gains under perturbations are benchmark-dependent, including improvements on LIBERO-Plus. Real-world evaluations further demonstrate improved manipulation success under both standard and unseen perturbation conditions. Further analyses show that policy improvements are not consistently accompanied by higher future prediction fidelity, suggesting the potential of change-oriented supervision for action learning.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33917v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33917v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-24","count":26,"star5Count":7,"star4Count":5,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.31977","arxiv":"2611.31977v1","baseId":"2611.31977","title":"Distill to Loop: Turning Pretrained Diffusion Transformers into Looped Models with Adjustable Depth","authors":["Chenru Wang","Shengwei An"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】","abstract":"Looped transformers reuse one block of shared weights along the depth of the network. Recent looped diffusion transformers are trained from scratch, which repeats the full pretraining cost although strong pretrained non-looped diffusion transformers exist. We propose Distill to Loop (Dooop), which distills a pretrained non-looped diffusion transformer into a looped student whose blocks are initialized from the teacher's. We cast distillation as a fixed-point iteration toward the teacher, in which the output after every loop is trained toward the teacher, and each loop is one step of an ordinary differential equation in depth. As a result, one model runs any number of loops at test time without retraining, and the higher-order ODE solvers of diffusion model sampling can be applied along the depth dimension for better results. Meanwhile, because a looped model produces an output after every loop, we also construct loop guidance between the outputs of different loop counts, which improves the generated samples at little extra compute. On SD3-medium, a looped student with about 37% of the teacher's parameters matches the teacher on average over GenEval, ImageReward, PickScore and HPSv2.1 with two to four loops, and with two loops it uses about 58% of the teacher's compute. And on a four-step SD3-medium, loop guidance raises the average of the four metrics from 88.2 to 99.2.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31977v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31977v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32135","arxiv":"2611.32135v1","baseId":"2611.32135","title":"Interpretable Orthogonal Latent Diffusion with Scale-Selective Uncertainty for Medical Image Super-Resolution","authors":["Onkar Jadhav","Tim French"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Diffusion models are increasingly used for medical image reconstruction and super-resolution, but their samples may contain plausible fine-scale hallucinations that are difficult to identify from the reconstructed image alone. We propose Med-PODiff, a patch-wise proper orthogonal decomposition (POD) diffusion framework for brain MRI super-resolution that performs generative reconstruction in compact and interpretable local coefficient spaces. Unlike pixel-space or learned-latent diffusion models, Med-PODiff uses the POD structure to separate low-rank anatomical variation from fine-detail residual variation, enabling predictive uncertainty to be decomposed into total, coarse, and fine components. Building on this decomposition, we define a pixel-wise hallucination-risk score, $H_p$, that emphasizes regions where fine-detail uncertainty dominates coarse structural uncertainty. On brain MRI, Med-PODiff outperforms representative diffusion baselines in reconstruction quality while producing well-calibrated predictive intervals, with total uncertainty detecting high-error regions at AUROC 0.954. More importantly, the decomposed uncertainty is scale-selective: coarse and fine uncertainty preferentially predict errors at the corresponding scales, with $H_p$ localizing fine error at AUROC $0.906$. Controlled injections and retrospective Cartesian $k$-space undersampling further support fine-detail localization and degradation robustness. The discarded POD tail is only $3.8\\%$ of the retained fine-error magnitude, and Med-PODiff improves downstream segmentation consistency while requiring substantially less inference time than pixel-space diffusion.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32135v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32135v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32406","arxiv":"2611.32406v1","baseId":"2611.32406","title":"BEACON: Learning Observation-Conditioned Starting Points for Guided Conditional Flow Matching in Microscopy","authors":["Anirban Ray","Florian Jug"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Fluorescence microscopy drives much of modern life science, but accessible imaging setups often yield images degraded by out-of-focus light, limited resolution, and strong signal-dependent noise. Computational restoration can recover high-quality images from such data, enabling cheaper and faster imaging at lower light dose, provided biologically meaningful structures are preserved. Guided conditional flow matching has recently shown strong results for microscopy dehazing and computational super-resolution by learning distributions of plausible restorations. However, it pairs each clean target with an independent standard Gaussian sample, so the starting point of every training path does not leverage the degraded observation. This arbitrary noise--target coupling can produce conflicting velocity targets and makes the conditional velocity field harder to learn. We present BEACON, a $BE$st-of-$K$ $A$daptive learned coupling for guided $CON$ditional flow matching, which learns an observation-conditioned base distribution. During training, a lightweight conditional noise model proposes $K$ starting points for each degraded input, and the candidate with the lowest flow-matching loss supervises both networks, while a KL term keeps the proposal close to the Gaussian prior. At inference, a single draw from the learned proposal is transported along the velocity field, requiring neither candidate selection nor the target. We evaluate BEACON on ten datasets spanning microscopy dehazing and computational super-resolution against 13 baseline families. BEACON achieves the best median perception--distortion rank for every pairing of a fidelity metric (PSNR, MicroMS-SSIM) with a perceptual metric (LPIPS, FID). Code and pretrained models will be released under a permissive license.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32406v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32406v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32562","arxiv":"2611.32562v1","baseId":"2611.32562","title":"Sol$^{3}$: Accelerating Video Diffusion for Free via Hierarchical Sparse Attention Routing","authors":["Guoxin Fan","Weilun Feng","Chuanguang Yang","Mingqiang Wu","Xiaokun Liu","Jitao Lu"],"authorCount":11,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Sol-Attn (Sparsifying online attention) combines sparse exact computation with low-cost representative approximation to accelerate training-free inference in diffusion video models. However, intra-block attention can focus on only a few tokens. While preserving these key interactions exactly, the block-level decision incurs the cost of evaluating many low-response members. We propose Sol$^{3}$, a training-free hierarchical sparse attention method that further refines how exact computations are allocated. First, we introduce Gaussian-guided hierarchical routing, which determines the scope of exact computation step by step, from blocks to tokens, using statistical thresholds at each level. Only a subset of members within Mixed blocks that require refinement is selected for exact computation, while representative terms approximate the remaining members. We further design heterogeneous routing statistics that combine block-center correlations with variations in Key and Value within blocks to identify blocks that are difficult to approximate. Among these Mixed blocks, we uniformly compare raw token relevance to select members requiring exact computation. Experiments on video generation, audio-video co-generation, and world simulation tasks demonstrate that Sol$^{3}$ achieves 86% attention sparsity and delivers a $3.07\\times$ attention speedup while preserving quality. Our code is available at https://github.com/FofGofx/sol3.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32562v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32562v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32632","arxiv":"2611.32632v1","baseId":"2611.32632","title":"HazeFlux: Few-Step Generative Priors for Real-World Image Dehazing","authors":["Yunong Liu","Danna Xue","Javier Vazquez-Corral"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Real-world single-image dehazing suffers from severe domain shifts, as paired real data is scarce and models trained on synthetic haze generalize poorly to real-world haze. While pre-trained generative editing models offer powerful image priors to bridge this generalization gap, applying them to image dehazing poses two major challenges: standard generative models prioritize distribution alignment over strict pixel-level fidelity, and operating in compressed latent spaces inherently discards fine-grained structural details. In this paper, we propose a novel two-stage framework that adapts the generative prior of a few-step flow-matching backbone to real-world dehazing. In the first stage, we fine-tune the latent backbone using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), supervised not only on a flow-matching objective, but also on pixel-level supervision, thus enabling direct hazy-to-clear translation. In the second stage, we introduce structure-preserving skip connections directly to the VAE decoder, bypassing latent compression artifacts to recover precise spatial edges. Experiments on four realworld benchmarks demonstrate that our approach significantly outperforms existing dehazing methods in real conditions.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32632v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32632v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33371","arxiv":"2611.33371v1","baseId":"2611.33371","title":"MotifFlow: Training-Free, Self-Contained Style Transfer from Color to Motif in Rectified Flow Transformers","authors":["Chun Xie","Itaru Kitahara"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["训练范式","色彩迁移"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【色彩迁移】","abstract":"Expressive style transfer must reproduce a painting's palette, brushwork, and characteristic motifs while retaining the content image's structure. Attention injection is effective in U-Net-based diffusion models, but adapting it to FLUX often transfers color without capturing the style's distinctive brushwork. Our analysis across backbones links this gap to how injected features are integrated across neighboring spatial locations. We introduce MotifFlow, a training-free, self-contained framework that combines style injection with style-alignment guidance in FLUX. Injection supplies style features during generation but does not explicitly encourage the model's native attention output to align with them. We therefore use the discrepancy between native and injected attention outputs to guide latent-space updates, encouraging the evolving image to develop the style's distinctive brushwork and motifs. Style encoding, guidance, and synthesis all use the same frozen backbone, without auxiliary image encoders or trained adapters. A single strength parameter controls the transition from color transfer to pronounced brushwork and motifs, with additional support for regional stylization and style mixing. Experiments on 1,000 content-style pairs show that MotifFlow better matches the local textures of the style image than baseline methods and produces more consistent stylization than injection alone. The same design extends to FLUX.2.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33371v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33371v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33437","arxiv":"2611.33437v1","baseId":"2611.33437","title":"ViCARD: Visual Credit Assignment with Reward-Gated Distillation for Multimodal Reasoning","authors":["Zijian Huang","Jiasi Chen"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏强化","少步蒸馏","强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏强化","少步蒸馏","强化学习"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏强化 · 少步蒸馏 · 强化学习】","abstract":"Visual reasoning requires models to identify relevant visual evidence and use it to derive correct answers. Outcomebased reinforcement learning (RL) faces two key challenges in this regime: it yields no learning signal when perceptual errors cause all sampled responses to fail, and it broadcasts a uniform advantage across every token within a response. Conversely, while visual self-distillation offers dense guidance from evidence-centered image crops, student learning from token distributions alone often fails to elicit correct final answers. Motivated by an audit of training rollouts that exposes these complementary failure modes, we introduce ViCARD (Visual Credit Assignment with RewardGated Distillation), which integrates per-token visual supervision with verified correctness rewards. For each rollout group, we leverage a teacher with its input image cropped to the relevant visual area, and use this to provide per-token supervision to the student while also valuing correctness. If all sampled rollouts fail, ViCARD samples an additional crop-teacher answer and activates on-policy distillation only if that answer is correct. Together, these mechanisms provide fine-grained visual credit within informative reward groups and a learning signal for otherwise uninformative all-fail groups. Extensive comparisons across model scales demonstrate that ViCARD improves fine-grained visual reasoning performance over RL and distillation baselines, while retaining broad visual reasoning capabilities.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33437v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33437v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33016","arxiv":"2611.33016v1","baseId":"2611.33016","title":"RePix-VFI: Re-rendering Observed Pixels for Video Frame Interpolation with Generative Priors from One-Step Diffusion","authors":["Dayeon Kim","Jeonghyeok Do","Munchurl Kim"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Multi-frame video frame interpolation (VFI) aims to reconstruct dense video from sparse keyframes, requiring accurate intermediate motion and faithful appearance. As temporal gaps grow, flow-based methods can retain observed textures but misplace them under ambiguous motion, whereas iterative latent-diffusion models can synthesize plausible content but incur substantial sampling costs and may alter details during reconstruction with a variational autoencoder (VAE). We present RePix-VFI, named for Re-rendering observed Pixels, a multi-frame VFI framework that couples draft-conditioned one-step video diffusion with reconstruction from observed keyframes. A pretrained video diffusion model refines a flow-rendered draft of the whole window in a single forward pass. For each target frame, our generative readback matches its two bounding keyframes to the generated prediction using an optical-flow estimator. The resulting correspondences guide re-rendering of original keyframe RGB pixels outside the VAE. A lightweight appearance fusion head combines these renderings with generated appearance. Experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate significant improvements in reconstruction and perceptual quality. On SNU-FILM at $\\times$8 and $\\times$16, RePix-VFI achieves the highest PSNR and the lowest LPIPS, FloLPIPS, and tOF among the compared methods, exceeding the best evaluated diffusion-based baseline by 1.71 and 1.48dB in PSNR, respectively. It also runs 32--42$\\times$ faster than the state-of-the-art multi-frame VFI diffusion model.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33016v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33016v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32463","arxiv":"2611.32463v1","baseId":"2611.32463","title":"Describe, Don't Ask: Caption-Driven On-Policy Distillation for Fine-Grained Visual Perception","authors":["Haozhan Shen","Liangbo He","Jun Lan","Huijia Zhu","Weiqiang Wang","Tiancheng Zhao"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Vision-language models (VLMs) often recognize fine-grained details more accurately in a relevant crop than in the full image. Multimodal on-policy distillation (OPD) methods exploit this by transferring the advantage of a crop-view teacher to a full-image student. However, their supervision relies on region-specific questions curated through an elaborate synthesis pipeline, which brings three weaknesses: (1) every region needs a question--answer pair written exclusively for it, locking training into costly QA curation that is hard to scale to unlabeled images; (2) all rollouts of a region answer the same question, so the teacher supervises only the details it asks about, leaving other visual elements in the region unsupervised; and (3) every question presupposes that its target exists, so the student becomes prone to answering even when the target is absent. In this paper, we study how to turn unlabeled images into broad visual supervision through multi-aspect self-description and privileged-view distillation, and propose CaptionOPD, which replaces tailored questions with a fixed set of image-agnostic caption prompts, ranging from general descriptions to focused captions of parts, colors, counts, text, and layout. The rollouts of a region receive different prompts, so the teacher supervises various aspects of the region. Moreover, because caption prompts mask the model to describe what is visible instead of querying a specific target, they avoid presupposing what the region contains. Using the model itself as the crop-view teacher, CaptionOPD requires no questions, no answers, and no larger model annotations: only images and region boxes. Experiments show that, trained on the same images and regions, CaptionOPD outperforms prior OPD methods across multiple fine-grained and general perception benchmarks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32463v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32463v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32467","arxiv":"2611.32467v1","baseId":"2611.32467","title":"TDD: Target-Discrepancy Distillation for Underwater Image Enhancement","authors":["Zefan Feng","Zhaocheng Yu","Laibin Chang","Kui Jiang","Junjun Jiang","Xiaopeng Fan"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Underwater degradation changes image appearance and can compromise the semantic cues used to guide enhancement. Identifying scene content alone does not explicitly specify the appearance to recover or the input-dependent changes associated with that target. We propose target-discrepancy knowledge distillation (TDD) for underwater image enhancement, which organizes guidance around these two aspects of the same restoration problem. For each degraded-reference training pair, reference features from an underwater vision-language teacher provide target-appearance supervision, while paired feature residuals describe the observation's discrepancy relative to the same target. Both representations are jointly distilled into a lightweight guidance network that predicts them from the degraded image alone; neither the teacher nor reference images are required at inference. To align their use with their supervisory roles, we introduce progressive knowledge fusion within a multi-scale enhancement network. The estimated discrepancy first conditions spatial feature updates, after which target-appearance guidance refines the updated features and channel interactions consolidate their responses. This correction-before-refinement design lets target-guided refinement build on discrepancy-conditioned features. Experiments on six underwater benchmarks demonstrate competitive performance across full-reference and no-reference metrics. Ablations and feature visualizations analyze the contributions of the knowledge source, joint distillation, and progressive fusion.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32467v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32467v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32472","arxiv":"2611.32472v1","baseId":"2611.32472","title":"STAR: Counterfactual Dual-World Rollouts and Spatial Target Adaptive Reconstruction for Visual On-Policy Distillation","authors":["Yi Zou","Zhuang Yan","Yudong Zhang","Xu Wang","Yang Wang"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Vision-language models often rely on global context and language priors while overlooking answer-determining local details. Privileged visual on-policy self-distillation supervises full-image student rollouts using a frozen teacher that views high-resolution evidence crops. Yet our cross-scale diagnostics show that models retain over half of their full-image accuracy when local evidence is completely masked, indicating that single-image correctness does not guarantee reliance on local evidence, while the need for visual correction also varies across tokens with their spatial attention states. We propose STAR, a visual on-policy self-distillation framework combining independent counterfactual dual-world rollouts with spatial-attention-guided target reconstruction. Local image editing constructs paired worlds with identical questions and backgrounds but distinct answer-determining facts, where a shared student samples trajectories independently to expose contextual shortcuts under image-consistent prefixes. Within each world, the teacher contrasts real and mean-color crops to determine the visual correction direction, while the divergence between student image attention and a teacher-centroid spatial reference adaptively controls token-wise target log-probability reconstruction. Without auxiliary attention losses or test-time tool overhead, STAR improves Qwen3.5 across scales by up to 8.04 percentage points in average accuracy over initialization across six fine-grained benchmarks, reaches OPSD's final accuracy $4.7\\times$ faster, and generalizes to out-of-domain reasoning tasks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32472v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32472v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.CL"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33316","arxiv":"2611.33316v1","baseId":"2611.33316","title":"What Does RL Post-Training Teach Text-to-Image Models? A Skill-Wise Analysis of Flow-GRPO","authors":["Cheng Zhang","Jiesong Liu"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】","abstract":"Reinforcement learning (RL) post-training has become a standard way to improve text-to-image flow models: Flow-GRPO lifts the GenEval score of SD3.5-M from 0.63 to 0.95, a gain read as the model having learned compositional generation. We ask how much of that gain the base model's own samples already contained, by measuring pass@k over shared noise seeds, one skill at a time, before and after RL, on prompts never used in training. The answer differs by skill. On four of GenEval's six skills, where 32 base samples already solve almost every prompt, the gain is sharpening: one post-trained sample is worth 4 to 10 base samples, and pass@32 barely moves. On spatial relations it is unlocking: pass@32 rises from 0.69 to 1.00, 128 base samples do not match one post-trained sample, and prompts the base model never solved in 32 samples are solved 93% of the time, as often as those it solved. The benchmark average, 31 base samples per post-trained sample, describes no skill. Relations carry 35% of the gain; of the other 65%, selecting among 8 base samples with the judge behind the reward recovers nine tenths. We propose an account of when each occurs: within a prompt, a group-relative update re-weights only the outcomes its samples contain, and a flow policy cannot widen the noise that produces them; across prompts, shared parameters let successes on some prompts repair a deficit common to many. The same anatomy applies to a text-rendering checkpoint. The account's predictions are tested with a checkpoint trained without KL, within-prompt diversity, and controlled training that holds object classes out, where sharpening comes first and transfers, and unlocking follows, on practised objects before unseen ones.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33316v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33316v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.31984","arxiv":"2611.31984v1","baseId":"2611.31984","title":"WaveSR: Physics-Inspired Wave Evolution Attention for Efficient Image Super-Resolution","authors":["Xiaoyan Lei","Biao Luo","Wenlong Zhang","Tingwen Huang","Chunhua Yang","Weihua Gui"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Recently, physics-inspired neural networks have gained widespread attention due to their interpretability. The closed-form, frequency-time decoupled solution of the wave equation offers linear complexity and allows different frequency components to evolve independently, making it a potential alternative solution for efficient image super-resolution. Therefore, a physics-inspired wave evolution attention framework is proposed, namely WaveSR. The WaveSR framework involves two key modules, i.e., wave evolution attention (WEA) and Frequency-Adaptive Feed-Forward Network (FA-FFN). The WEA module achieves feature interactions through the analytical solution of the 2D wave equation in the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) domain, enabling independent evolution of different frequency components with learnable wave speed \\(c\\) and damping coefficient \\(α\\). The FA-FFN module realizes adaptive frequency feature selection through learnable spectral modulation, effectively enhancing feature representation capability. Subsequently, we employ a two-stage training strategy with a hybrid objective that combines a spatial-domain pixel loss and a frequency-domain loss to improve reconstruction quality. Our method provides a preliminary exploration of physics-inspired neural networks for efficient image super-resolution.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31984v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31984v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32394","arxiv":"2611.32394v1","baseId":"2611.32394","title":"Adaptive and Detection-Aware Super-Resolution for Small Object Detection","authors":["Cezica-Elena Luncanu","Stelian Spînu"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Image super-resolution aims to reconstruct a high-resolution image from a low-resolution input and is relevant for surveillance, satellite imagery, medical imaging, and object detection. This paper investigates SRCNN-based single-image super-resolution for a scale factor of x4, starting from a classical baseline and progressively introducing residual learning, Charbonnier loss, and adaptive loss functions. The proposed approach includes a three-region adaptive loss, where image areas are divided into smooth, texture, and edge regions using gradient information. The models are evaluated using PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS, and EdgeMAE in order to measure both reconstruction quality and structural detail preservation. In addition to reconstruction evaluation, the paper analyzes the impact of super-resolution on object detection. A YOLOv8 detector is applied to images processed through bicubic interpolation and SRCNN-based variants, using images from the COCO validation dataset. The results show that improved reconstruction quality does not always lead to better detection performance. However, for small objects, a detection-aware variant combined with residual scaling improves the F1 score compared to the bicubic baseline. These results highlight the importance of task-aware evaluation when super-resolution is used in computer vision pipelines.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32394v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32394v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33455","arxiv":"2611.33455v1","baseId":"2611.33455","title":"Recovering What Matters, Transferring Only What Is Reliable: Toward Precise Reference-Based Super-Resolution","authors":["Sihan Ji","Wenxin Chen","Yue Shi","Jiezhang Cao"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Reference-based super-resolution (RefSR) aims to recover missing details from high-quality references. However, existing methods face two fundamental challenges. First, perceptual plausibility does not guarantee content fidelity. Existing methods often produce visually convincing details without faithfully recovering reference-specific information, such as text, codes, and identity cues. Second, faithful reference reading does not guarantee reliable information transfer. Increasing reference utilization also exposes the restoration process to misleading content, as global semantic relevance does not necessarily imply local correspondence consistency. To address these challenges, we propose PreciseRef, a reference-aware generative framework for faithful and reliable RefSR. At its core, Asymmetric Twin-Attention (ATA) allows target and reference images to participate in a unified attention space while the reference keeps its own shape. By letting their image tokens interact directly through joint attention, ATA facilitates fine-grained reference information retrieval without explicit image alignment. To ensure reliable information transfer, we introduce Dual-Scale Reference Gating (DRG), which combines Semantic Relevance Gating (SRG) to filter globally irrelevant reference context and Bidirectional Consistency Gating (BCG) to suppress unreliable local correspondences. Furthermore, we establish VeriRef, a benchmark evaluating exact reference-dependent content recovery and robustness to mismatched references. Extensive experiments show that our method achieves 85.6% character accuracy and 76.3% exact code decoding, compared with 17.4% and 0% for existing methods, while effectively suppressing erroneous reference transfer. It also achieves state-of-the-art perceptual quality on CUFED5 and WR-SR.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33455v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33455v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32151","arxiv":"2611.32151v1","baseId":"2611.32151","title":"Let The Pixels Through: Rethinking Long Skip Connections for Scalable Pixel Diffusion","authors":["Jiachen Lei","Jiahong Wu","XiangXiang Chu"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"Training pixel diffusion models remains less straightforward, owing to the ad hoc training recipes and architecture designs, along with the sensitivity to hyper-parameter choices. We show that strong denoisers can be built by refining existing pixel-native designs from the literature. Specifically, we revisit the long skip connection in U-Net and find that the optimization benefit commonly attributed to it is not necessary for its denoising benefit. We further find that the conventional linear combination of features from the skip and main backbone is inadequate for regimes of various noise levels. Motivated by these observations, we introduce two core modifications. First, we adopt asymmetric long skips, which propagate fine-grained information from input embeddings to decoder layers. Second, we introduce adaGLU, a SwiGLU-like MLP block, in decoder layers to adaptively filter information from the embeddings before being added to main-branch features. The above designs give a simple pixel diffusion transformer architecture. When trained from scratch, it scales smoothly from small-scale baselines up to 1.6B parameters, achieving 1.62 and 1.70 FID on ImageNet-256 and -512, respectively. Besides, it natively supports multimodal inputs, such as text and images, yielding competitive performance in text-to-image generation. We release our codes at https://github.com/AMAP-ML/PiT","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32151v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32151v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.31895","arxiv":"2611.31895v1","baseId":"2611.31895","title":"Loupe: Reinforcing Knowledge-Intensive Video Generation Agents","authors":["Kai Zeng","Ruichuan An","Zhen Fang","Xi Fang","Han Lyu","Moran Li"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Knowledge-intensive video generation remains challenging because prompts often omit the facts, appearances, and temporal relationships required for accurate generation. While search-based workflows can retrieve missing evidence, manually designed strategies limit adaptive planning, and conventional training suffers from costly video-level feedback and coarse trajectory-level credit assignment. In this paper, we present Loupe, a video generation agent that retrieves and verifies textual and visual evidence to construct evidence-grounded temporal video prompts with reference images. We further introduce Rubric-Based Credit (RBC), which leverages prompt-specific factual, visual, and temporal requirements to evaluate intermediate search and planning decisions before video rendering. Building on RBC, RBC-SFT reweights assistant-token learning by step quality, while RBC-GRPO incorporates step-level credits alongside a final planning reward, without rendering videos during RL. To address the lack of dedicated search-oriented evaluation, we construct Search-VBench, which contains 150 held-out prompts across seven categories. Experiments show that Loupe improves all four evaluation dimensions across three video generators, raising the overall score of MiniMax-H3 from 32.80 to 70.67 and outperforming an untrained search workflow. Ablation experiments show that full RBC training outperforms standard SFT followed by trajectory-level GRPO by 5.27 points. Human evaluations and results on Sci-VBench and KIVI-Bench further demonstrate improved generation quality, scientific grounding, factual precision, and helpfulness. Our project page is available at https://zengkaiya.github.io/Loupe/.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31895v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31895v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32445","arxiv":"2611.32445v1","baseId":"2611.32445","title":"A Mesh to Remember: Persistent Appearance for Interactive Video Worlds","authors":["Joonghyuk Shin","Alex Trevithick","Katarína Tóthová","Liyuan Zhu","Shengyu Huang","Jaesik Park"],"authorCount":10,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Video world models synthesize photorealistic observations in response to user actions but struggle to remain consistent over long interactions. Coding agents and text-to-3D models instead generate explicit 3D worlds whose geometry and state persist by construction, but whose appearance falls far short of photorealism. Recent methods combine the strengths of both by rendering such worlds into coarse proxies that guide a video model. However, since these proxies only specify where things are, the renderer must invent what they look like as the world is explored, and anything it forgets is reinvented when a surface reappears. Our key insight is that a mesh identifies which surface each pixel shows, across frames and views, even as objects move, allowing it to serve as an address for what the renderer generates. We propose M2R (Mesh to Remember), a few-step causal video renderer that stores its own features on the surfaces they depict, in each object's frame, and retrieves them by rasterizing the mesh whenever those surfaces come back into view. Appearance thus travels with objects wherever they move, persists across revisits, and is shared by every agent that observes it, while memory grows with the surfaces seen instead of the number of frames. Trained only on synthetic data, M2R transfers zero-shot to real indoor scans and to worlds authored by coding agents. It outperforms state-of-the-art methods in consistency across static, dynamic, and multi-agent benchmarks, more than doubling object permanence and nearly doubling cross-agent consistency, while rendering 720p video in real time (40 FPS).","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32445v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32445v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33141","arxiv":"2611.33141v1","baseId":"2611.33141","title":"iWAM: Efficient Action Prediction from World Models without Explicit Future Prediction","authors":["Vansh Kapoor","Max Sobol Mark","Sreyas Venkataraman","Max Simchowitz","Aviral Kumar"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"World-action models (WAMs) build policies on pretrained video-generation models, on the premise that predicting how a scene evolves helps determine how to act. However, generating future scenes is expensive for real-time control. This motivates efficient proxies for future imagination, but how to train such proxies without sacrificing action-prediction quality remains unclear. An effective proxy need not generate a high-resolution frame; it only needs to provide enough information to distinguish between possible futures. This leads to the design principle behind iWAM: rather than denoising an entire future frame, can we condition the action policy on intermediate features from a single video-model evaluation? We show that the answer is yes. Exploiting the reversible dynamics of flow-matching models, we invert each training future into the corresponding latent noise vector. However, a single velocity evaluation at this latent does not necessarily yield informative features for action prediction. We therefore learn a bidirectional flow map that amortizes future-generation computation, enabling a single model evaluation to provide effective conditioning features. At inference, we sample a latent noise vector from the prior and condition the policy using just one video-backbone forward pass. Across simulated benchmarks and real robots, iWAM recovers the generalization benefits of WAMs while substantially reducing its inference cost.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33141v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33141v1","arxivCats":["cs.RO","cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33268","arxiv":"2611.33268v1","baseId":"2611.33268","title":"JetDiffusion: Pushing the Speed Limits of Video Generation with NVFP4 Sparse Attention","authors":["Yulun Wu","Zihan Min","Minshen Zhang","Kaiqin Kong","Ji Xie","Will Lin"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Attention over long sequences is a major computational bottleneck in video generation, which can be alleviated through sparsity and low-bit quantization. We propose JetDiffusion, a video generation system built on trainable Video Sparse Quantized Attention (VSQA), with custom inference kernels that combine native NVFP4 computation and kernel fusion. At 90% sparsity, VSQA accelerates attention by 8-29x compared with FlashAttention on different hardwares. To further accelerate video generation, we combine VSQA with NVFP4 linear quantization and step distillation. Although each technique can individually preserve generation quality, jointly adapting to all three remains challenging, particularly under aggressive NVFP4 quantization. Through empirical studies, we develop an effective staged training recipe that enables stable adaptation to quantization, sparsity, and few-step denoising. On Wan2.1-14B, the resulting model achieves 273x end-to-end video generation speedup on the RTX 5090 while maintaining comparable video quality.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33268v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33268v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32172","arxiv":"2611.32172v2","baseId":"2611.32172","title":"MediaStormBench-CameraLog: Can Multimodal Models Identify a Camera's Log Curve?","authors":["Ming Ma","Wenhao Hu","Qichao Wang","Yuhao Wang","Junpeng Zhao","Qiyi Wang"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-25","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["色彩迁移"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移】","abstract":"MediaStormBench is a multimodal benchmark for real-world film and television production, with each subset drawn from MediaStorm's day-to-day work. We release its first subset, MediaStormBench-CameraLog (CameraLogBench), on a common foundational task in footage ingest and initial color grading: identifying the Log curve of a clip without metadata. Cameras record Log footage by compressing a wide range of scene light into code values with a vendor-defined logarithmic curve, and color can be restored correctly only once that curve is known. The curve, however, is recorded only in file metadata, easily lost in transcoding and team hand-offs. A colorist then has to try candidate curves one by one against a waveform scope, and a wrong choice shifts the tones and colors of the whole clip. Recovering the curve from the image alone is challenging, because vendor curves cluster tightly in code value and are coupled with unknown exposure. CameraLogBench is built on professionally shot footage and tests whether multimodal models can identify the curve, with a probe that removes the correct option to expose guessing from priors. Because every candidate curve is strictly defined by a published vendor formula, we derive four controls from one implementation of these formulas: graded negatives, synthetic frames of identical scenes, in-context specification digests, and a training-free formula baseline, which together decouple semantic priors from photometric measurement. Across 11 models, real-footage accuracy barely exceeds always guessing the most frequent curve; when the correct option is removed, models almost never abstain, revealing heavy reliance on scene and brand priors. Supplying the vendor specification sharply improves recognition on calibrated charts but hardly helps on natural images without a photometric reference, exposing a gap between reading a specification and measuring pixels.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32172v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32172v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32299","arxiv":"2611.32299v1","baseId":"2611.32299","title":"Towards a Multimodal Aesthetic Agent for Photographic Reference Grading","authors":["Kunjie Lin","Haitao Zhang","Bosheng Chen","Lanhui Xu","Gaofeng Pan","Yuxin Lin"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["色彩迁移"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移】","abstract":"Reference-guided photographic color grading helps users realize their aesthetic intent by selectively drawing on the color and tonal characteristics of a reference photograph. Doing so requires understanding which characteristics users wish to adopt and translating these preferences into controllable edits. However, image-only reference methods align overall appearance without instruction-grounded selection, while pixel-space generative editors typically produce flattened images without editable parameters, limiting subsequent refinement. To address these challenges, we introduce JarvisAura, a multimodal aesthetic agent that formulates photographic color grading as structured decision-making in an editable parameter space. JarvisAura offers three key advantages: (1) an aesthetic agent paradigm that integrates visual attribute perception, strategy-level reasoning, and parameterized execution for interpretable and controllable photographic color grading; (2) a unified reasoning-to-execution pipeline that connects aesthetic planning with backend rendering and editable parameter refinement; and (3) a three-stage training strategy comprising Parameter-Grounded Continual Pre-Training for parameter-appearance grounding, Reference-Grounded Supervised Fine-Tuning for reference-guided reasoning and parameter decisions, and Reference-Aligned Group Relative Policy Optimization for rendered-outcome refinement with multidimensional rewards and dynamic data derivation. On Ref-Bench, JarvisAura achieves the best scores among methods with results across reported automatic metrics and a 74% human-rated task-success rate, exceeding GPT-Image-2.5 by 9 percentage points.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32299v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32299v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33157","arxiv":"2611.33157v1","baseId":"2611.33157","title":"PointX: Multi-View Consistent Intrinsics by Querying Surface Points","authors":["Miguel Farinha","Sam Sartor","Iliyan Georgiev","Giuseppe Vecchio","Ronald Clark","Michael Fischer"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"The appearance of a scene is determined by the albedo, roughness, metallicity and normal of every point on its surface, and by the light that reaches it, its irradiance. Recovering these intrinsics from images is ambiguous, and methods that predict them per pixel give the same surface point a different decomposition in every view. We introduce PointX, a simple feed-forward model that predicts intrinsics per 3D surface point instead. A multi-view transformer encodes a set of unposed images, and for any queried surface point, PointX pools the point's tokens across the views in which it appears and decodes the point's intrinsics once. Each point of a 3D reconstruction of the scene, such as a point cloud or a mesh, thus receives a single prediction, which is exactly consistent across views by construction and enables view-consistent relighting and editing. The same model also predicts per-view maps, from one image or many, by querying the point seen at every pixel. These maps agree across views up to the precision of the estimated geometry and correspondences. PointX is significantly more consistent than existing methods, while remaining competitive in accuracy from a single image and improving as views are added. Project page: https://pointx-paper.github.io/","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33157v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33157v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32793","arxiv":"2611.32793v1","baseId":"2611.32793","title":"Back to Raw Motion: Latent-Free Diffusion Transformers for Text-to-Motion and Exact Conditional Generation","authors":["Yun Chen","Jeonghyeok Do","Munchurl Kim"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":42,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"As generative models tackle larger and more complex datasets, learned codecs enable efficient generation through compact latent representations, but may lose details and cannot guarantee exact preservation of given conditions. Generating directly in the original data space avoids errors introduced by the codec. Recent advances in image generation demonstrate that pixel diffusion can produce high-quality samples at practical computational costs. Inspired by these advances, we conduct a preliminary study of pixel diffusion, demonstrating its potential for adaptation to motion generation. Building on these findings, we present RawMo, a framework that brings text-to-motion generation to raw motion space. Our Motion Diffusion Transformer (MiT) models local skeletal structures and whole-body dynamics, while refining motion across frames to improve temporal consistency. Working directly in raw motion space further allows our high-noise text-motion alignment loss (HiNCE) to improve text consistency without intermediate motion decoding. RawMo supports multiple motion-conditioning tasks with a single conditional model while preserving given motion conditions exactly. Experiments on HumanML3D and KIT-ML show the best text alignment among compared methods and competitive motion quality, with ablations confirming the benefits of MiT and HiNCE.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32793v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32793v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.33388","arxiv":"2611.33388v1","baseId":"2611.33388","title":"Measuring and Predicting Image-Token Repair Boundaries in Vision-Language Models","authors":["Vishal Bhutani","Prasang Gupta","Sumanth Chundru","Kevin Paul"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Degraded images make vision-language models answer wrongly. Using synthetic paired blur, noise, and low light, we trace which clean image states can still repair those answers from the vision encoder through the language model. In four encoder models, 12 to 23 ranked tokens recover half of the full-patch effect, whereas random orders require four to fifteen times as many; encoder-free Gemma needs 22.2 versus 53.1. Full-block repair expires at a model-specific language-model crossing. The matched reverse-direction intervention is algebraically identical to text-token restoration, so its gap from image restoration measures a redundancy band rather than an independent predictor. A clean-image gray-ablation crossing independently predicts held-out repair with 1.53-layer error, against 3.65 for shared depth. Gemma MMBench has the only large attention-to-repair point gap; Gemma GQA does not. Restormer provides a non-oracle consistency check on two models.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.33388v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.33388v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.CL"]},{"id":"daily_2611.32407","arxiv":"2611.32407v1","baseId":"2611.32407","title":"Every Edit Its Own Budget: Per-Instruction Token Freezing for Training-Free Acceleration of Multi-Edit Image Editing","authors":["Jyotirmoy Nath","Neeraj Kumar","Brejesh Lall"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-24","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":38,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"Accelerating instruction-based image editing when one instruction requests several edits is challenging. Many training-free accelerators for flow-matching image editors skip tokens whose prediction has stopped changing, but decide this with a single threshold for the whole image. With several edits, such a threshold, tuned for speed, freezes small and slowly settling edits first, and requested edits disappear. We propose TIDE (Training-free Instruction-aware Drift-based Editing), which gives every instruction part its own compute budget. First, TIDE finds the region of each part in the editor's own attention by unbalanced optimal transport, without masks or external models. Second, it freezes a token once its drift is small relative to its own part and moves it along the straight path to its endpoint estimate. Third, a sparse engine computes only the remaining tokens. We prove that part-agnostic rules cannot guarantee per-part error budgets, characterize the optimal per-part threshold policy up to one tie class per part, and derive when our rule attains it. On FLUX.2 Klein, one global threshold breaks at least one edit's budget in 89% of MICE-Bench images, and TIDE drops up to 77% fewer edits at matched or higher speed (48% on MICE-Bench), a gap human raters confirm. On the many-step Klein Base, TIDE is the fastest compared method on six benchmarks (4.81-4.89x) and needs 5.3x fewer TFLOPs than vanilla, the largest saving we measure. With the same settings, it is also the fastest compared method on Qwen-Image-Edit-2511, a second model family (3.46-3.52x), and drops the fewest edits on all six benchmarks. Code will be made publicly available.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.32407v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.32407v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-23","count":16,"star5Count":7,"star4Count":5,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.30328","arxiv":"2611.30328v1","baseId":"2611.30328","title":"Draft Forcing: Target-guided Speculative Drafting and Learned Verifier for Autoregressive Video Generation","authors":["Chenkai Xu","Yuji Ren","Zihui Liu","Yijie Jin","Yuhao Shen","Xin Wang"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Autoregressive video diffusion models enable streaming synthesis chunk by chunk, yet invoking the large model for every chunk remains costly. While chunk-level speculative decoding can offload chunks to a lightweight draft model, existing approaches suffer from two limitations: (1) an off-the-shelf draft diverges from the target's trajectory, compounding errors in history; and (2) they either omit verification or rely on costly post-decode aesthetic scores rather than verifying consistency against the target. We address both with Draft Forcing, a framework coupling target-guided drafting with an on-policy trained verifier. Specifically, to align the draft for high acceptance, we opt for feature-level drafting, conditioning it on multi-layer target hidden states persisted from committed chunks, whose outputs are pushed to match target trajectories with multi-aspect losses. For verification, we propose an on-policy trained feature-space verifier predicting CDF-calibrated frame-wise relative RMSE against the target. It is trained on speculative rollouts as contexts anneal from random to inference-aligned mixtures, and gates proposals without VAE decoding. On the 14B Self-Forcing target, Draft Forcing achieves $1.63\\times$ DiT and $1.57\\times$ end-to-end speedups on VBench with minimal quality loss, and up to $1.93\\times$ DiT ($1.82\\times$ end-to-end) when combined with orthogonal KV compression. It generalizes to accelerate a streaming variant of MiniMax-H3, generating over $50\\%$ draft frames with competitive quality.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30328v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30328v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30472","arxiv":"2611.30472v1","baseId":"2611.30472","title":"Target-Part Preference Optimization for Part-Aware 3D Generation","authors":["Zishan Qin","Rui Li","Zeyu Zhang","Sevvandi Kandanaarachchi","Lina Yao","Dong Gong"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习 · 训练范式】","abstract":"Part-aware 3D generation requires not only globally plausible objects but also complete, well-separated semantic parts, yet high-quality part decompositions remain too scarce to support large-scale supervised fine-tuning. We study whether preference fine-tuning based on Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) can adapt a pretrained part-aware generator from limited part-annotated data without architectural changes. We rank candidates generated with different random seeds against ground-truth (GT) geometric metrics, and select the (object, target-part) examples with the largest across-seed quality gap. Each selected example stores a seed pair whose two flow trajectories share one clean GT compositional latent and differ only in Gaussian noise. We derive a reference-relative target-block preference loss from FlowDPO that compares the two trajectories against a frozen reference model. GT flow-matching losses on the target block and complete part set provide absolute supervision, while only the diffusion transformer (DiT) is updated. Human-ranked controls test an alternative ordering source, and synthetic cropping provides controlled loser targets that are incomplete by construction. Among the three training-set scales, the medium-scale setting gives the strongest result on held-out Objaverse and outperforms both the pretrained generator and the reported direct supervised fine-tuning baseline on the multi-part aggregate. Further analysis shows sensitivity to pair ordering, target-part coverage, and the balance between pairwise and GT supervision. Cross-dataset experiments show less consistent gains under distribution shift.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30472v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30472v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.31164","arxiv":"2611.31164v1","baseId":"2611.31164","title":"Analyzing and Improving FlowMap Distillation of Autoregressive Video Models","authors":["Yong-Hyun Park","Guande He","Joon Hyeok Kim","Hansheng Chen","Kaifan Yu","Chaoqi Luo"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"FlowMap distillation offers a natural route to few-step generative modeling by directly learning a teacher's denoising trajectories. Yet for autoregressive (AR) video generation, existing few-step models still rely on distribution matching distillation (DMD) to remain competitive, sacrificing sample diversity and requiring a large bidirectional teacher. In this work, we analyze what limits standalone AR FlowMap distillation and introduce two complementary guidance mechanisms to close this gap: (1) anti-drifting guidance, which helps the AR teacher correct drifted student rollouts by training an auxiliary model to mimic student drift and using it as a negative guidance branch; and (2) motion-aware guidance, which makes motion easier for a FlowMap student to learn by locating motion patches and selectively applying skip-layer guidance. Empirically, our approach applies to $π$-Flow, MeanFlow, and consistency distillation with 1.3B AR teachers, improving generation quality and reducing drift over 30 s rollouts. Notably, our 4-step $π$-Flow students match DMD students distilled from 14B teachers on VBench while preserving the sample diversity of their AR teachers, demonstrating that pure FlowMap distillation is effective for few-step autoregressive video generation.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31164v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31164v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.31471","arxiv":"2611.31471v1","baseId":"2611.31471","title":"PromptSMC: Enabling Particle Exploration in Flow Matching via LLM-Based Prompt Rejuvenation","authors":["Wenyi Lian","Zheng Zhao","Thomas B. Schön"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习 · 训练范式】","abstract":"This paper is concerned with prompt-image alignment at inference time through exploration of generative trajectories and dynamical prompt rejuvenation. We leverage sequential Monte Carlo (SMC), to offer a gradient-free and principled framework for this purpose through reward-guided trajectory selection. More specifically, we introduce LLM-based prompt rejuvenation, allowing for efficient particle exploration in SMC samplers along the diffusion time in flow matching models. The proposed method, PromptSMC, retains the selected latent states and uses a pretrained LLM to dynamically revise the prompts of duplicated particles under semantic-preservation constraints. These revised prompts induce alternative conditional velocity fields, allowing particles to explore different continuations from a shared intermediate state. The method further uses a reward model to evaluate each particle's alignment with the original prompt, and the resulting scores guide particles through resampling. PromptSMC thus integrates prompt-based exploration with reward-guided sampling, achieving faithful prompt-image alignment without additional noise injection, model training, or reward gradients. Our experiments and ablation studies across multiple benchmarks and backbones demonstrate the effectiveness of the method for text-to-image flow matching. Code is available at https://github.com/shermanlian/PromptSMC.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31471v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31471v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.31690","arxiv":"2611.31690v1","baseId":"2611.31690","title":"GRUN: Generalized Retinex Unfolding Network for Image Restoration","authors":["Rihan Zhang","Fengyang Xiao","Yuhan Chen","Chunming He","Sina Farsiu"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影","画质修复"],"tags":["打光与光影","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影 · 画质修复】","abstract":"Image restoration remains a fundamental challenge in low-level vision, requiring methods that handle diverse degradations while preserving scene structures and details. We revisit classical Retinex theory and propose a Generalized Retinex formulation that extends the illumination-reflectance decomposition into a Retinex-inspired content-environment factorization prior for image restoration. The key idea is to restore images using two learned factors: a content-dominant response (generalized reflectance) capturing scene properties and an environment-side response (generalized illumination) encoding task-dependent variations associated with the observed degradations. Based on this formulation, we develop GRUN (Generalized Retinex Unfolding Network), a MAP-guided deep unfolding framework that alternates reflectance restoration, illumination estimation, and progressive degradation inference across K stages with degradation-conditioned proximal operators. We further introduce a Degradation-aware Coupling Regularizer (DCR) that combines mutual information minimization, gradient decorrelation, and scale anchoring to promote complementary content-environment factor representations. Experiments on seven image restoration tasks, including denoising, dehazing, deraining, low-light enhancement, deblurring, underwater enhancement, and backlit enhancement, demonstrate competitive results on multiple benchmarks using the same architecture with task-specific weights. Moreover, the proposed Generalized Retinex design can be integrated into existing restoration methods, demonstrating its cross-architecture applicability. Code will be released.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31690v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31690v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30114","arxiv":"2611.30114v1","baseId":"2611.30114","title":"Thermal-Guided Low-Light UAV Image Enhancement: A Reliability-Aware Guidance Network and a Unified Benchmark","authors":["Zhicheng Zhao","Yu Wang","Jiangong Xu","Lixiang Xu","Chenglong Li","Jin Tang"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":96,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影","画质修复"],"tags":["打光与光影","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影 · 画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Thermal-guided low-light image enhancement (LLIE) is promising for nighttime unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) perception because thermal imagery remains informative under severe visible degradation. However, the degraded visible image serves as both the alignment reference and the enhancement target, making thermal guidance uncertain in its spatial correspondence and local utility under low illumination, sensor differences, and UAV motion. Misaligned or unsuitable thermal cues may introduce artifacts, while spatially varying degradation requires region-specific restoration rather than uniform enhancement. To address these challenges, we propose a Reliability-Aware Multi-Strategy Guidance Network (RMGNet) for thermal-guided UAV LLIE under weak optical-thermal alignment. Its Reliability-Aware Guidance Calibration Module (RAGCM) performs coarse-to-fine displacement estimation and jointly evaluates the reliability of aligned thermal guidance and the usability of degraded visible evidence. Based on calibrated guidance, degradation, and reliability cues, the Multi-Strategy Enhancement Routing Module (MSERM) generates candidates for detail preservation, highlight-protected relighting, and thermal compensation, then spatially routes their contributions. We further construct the UAV weakly aligned Optical-Thermal dataset (UWOT), the largest UAV-oriented benchmark to date for weakly aligned optical-thermal low-light enhancement, containing 5,000 triplets. Extensive experiments show that RMGNet outperforms representative single-modal and multimodal state-of-the-art methods in enhancement quality while retaining competitive computational efficiency and greater robustness to practical cross-modal misalignment. The dataset and code will be publicly available at https://github.com/mmic-lcl/Datasets-and-benchmark-code.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30114v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30114v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30986","arxiv":"2611.30986v2","baseId":"2611.30986","title":"Reinforcing Diffusion Models by Instantaneous Trajectory Distribution Matching","authors":["Yihong Luo"],"authorCount":1,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":92,"coreTags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习 · 训练范式】","abstract":"Forward diffusion reinforcement learning (RL)---enabling deterministic ODE sampling without trajectory backpropagation---has grown increasingly popular for efficiently aligning diffusion models. However, we reveal that existing methods essentially rely on signed reward-weighted regression (RWR), regressing the trainable policy velocity onto rollouts from a behavior distribution. Unbiased baseline subtraction implicitly assumes that the policy velocity equals the behavior marginal velocity---an idealized assumption violated under classifier-free guidance (CFG) rollouts, offline/off-policy updates, and imperfect online self-consistency. This behavior--policy mismatch breaks baseline invariance and injects baseline-induced gradient drift, destabilizing training. To resolve this issue, we propose Instantaneous Trajectory Distribution Matching (iTDM), taking the infinitesimal limit of Trajectory Distribution Matching toward a time-local reward-guided target via the posterior-score identity to yield a sample-level gradient anchored at the behavior velocity without learning an additional reward-tilted target score. Tracking this velocity with an online auxiliary denoiser guarantees exact baseline invariance at the auxiliary optimum, effectively suppressing baseline drift across both online and offline RL. Extensive experiments across compositional generation, text rendering, and preference alignment show that iTDM achieves state-of-the-art in-domain and out-of-domain performance across group-wise online/offline RL and pairwise offline RL. Notably, on the challenging GenEval2 benchmark, iTDM converges nearly 3\\times faster than competing methods while scaling smoothly to 0.87 without collapse. Furthermore, iTDM scales effectively to the 5.6B FLUX.3 model, achieving better performance across both in-domain and unseen metrics. Code is available at https://github.com/Luo-Yihong/iTDM","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30986v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30986v2","arxivCats":["cs.LG","cs.AI","cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30728","arxiv":"2611.30728v1","baseId":"2611.30728","title":"Same Seed, Different Picture? Deciding Early at Low Resolution for Seed-Faithful Few-Step Flow Sampling","authors":["Huiran Duan","Weilun Feng","Yuqi Li","Yingli Tian"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":60,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Few-step samplers make large text-to-image flow models fast, but from the same noise they no longer generate the image of the standard 50-step sampler. Subjects, attributes and layout change, not just fine detail. We trace this loss of seed fidelity to the start of sampling. The coarse part of the initial noise decides coarse structure first, while the noise level is still above 0.9. Few-step samplers cross this range in their first steps, which account for 25-52% of their distance to the 50-step reference. Because this decision is coarse, a half-resolution run from the same coarse noise can make nearly the same decision at a third of the cost. We therefore propose Decide-then-Render (DtR), a training-free sampler that decides the composition in a few low-resolution steps, rebuilds the full-resolution state with the seed's own noise and renders the details. On 4 large models, DtR runs 11-25$\\times$ faster than the reference sampler. At each speed-up, it is closer to the reference and rated higher by ImageReward than every compared sampler, including few-step schedules calibrated per budget and published multi-resolution samplers. On FLUX.1-dev, it remains the most faithful sampler down to about $8\\times$, ahead of caching accelerators. A user study on this model found DtR's samples closer to the reference in 86% of pairwise trials. DtR transfers to a held-out model and to video, and distilled models can serve as its renderers. Project link: https://duanhuiran.github.io/DtR","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30728v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30728v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.31184","arxiv":"2611.31184v1","baseId":"2611.31184","title":"On-Policy Codec Distillation for Efficient All-in-One Video Generation","authors":["Boyang Wang","Kevin Blackburn-Matzen","Oindrila Saha","Matheus Gadelha","Zezhou Cheng"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":60,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Modern visual generative models operate on tokens, and every visual asset they receive, whether an identity, a scribble, a depth map, or a motion video, is tokenized and appended to the input token sequence. Consequently, conditioning quickly dominates the computation cost. Yet most of these tokens carry redundant spatiotemporal information. Our key insight is that identifying what is genuinely new is exactly the problem that classical video codecs have long solved. Thus, we first introduce a codec-based token pruning method that discards redundant tokens. Then, we propose an on-policy self-distillation (OPSD) strategy to train a DiT adapter capable of learning the codec pruning pattern well without quality degradation. Together, they form On-Policy Codec Distillation (CodecOPSD). We scale CodecOPSD to 16 condition modalities on our own video diffusion model, where it prunes 66% of the condition tokens on average with almost no quality drop. Therefore, denoising becomes 2x faster, and the cache memory shrinks by up to 73% in KV cache mode. CodecOPSD also extends to other state-of-the-art pretrained image and video generative models, where its benefit grows further with more conditions, longer frames, and higher resolution.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31184v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31184v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30040","arxiv":"2611.30040v1","baseId":"2611.30040","title":"Teaching How to Reason, Beyond What to Answer: Evidence-Faithful On-Policy Distillation for streaming video understanding","authors":["Mengnan Liu","Sanping Zhou","Kun Xia","Jiaxuan Lin","Gang Hua","Le Wang"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Moment Retrieval (MR) aims to localize the temporal segment in a video that corresponds to a given textual description. However, a textual query often provides only a sparse semantic description of the target event, whereas the video contains rich and fine-grained visual variations. This mismatch creates an inherent gap between textual semantics and visual content, making a single textual input insufficient for precise cross-modal alignment. As a result, when multiple temporally adjacent or semantically similar segments exist, the model may struggle to distinguish the target moment from confusing candidates. To address this challenge, we propose a Multi-Perspective Auxiliary Representation (MPAR) framework, which aims to bridge this alignment gap by enriching a single textual input with complementary visual cues from multiple perspectives. Specifically, MPAR consists of three key components: (1) Auxiliary Representation Initialization (ARI) constructs multiple learnable representation slots from the original query, preserving the query intent while preparing flexible carriers for subsequent visual cue injection; (2) Visual-Induced Semantic Structuring (VISS) injects target-specific visual attributes into each representation while preserving semantic coherence with the original query; and (3) Multi-Perspective Semantic Aligner (MPSA) aligns each auxiliary representation with corresponding video regions to enhance discriminative localization. By constructing multi-perspective auxiliary representations and guiding the model to attend to relevant visual regions across these representations, our model achieves more accurate moment localization. Extensive experiments on MR benchmarks demonstrate that our MPAR outperforms state-of-the-art methods, validating the effectiveness of MPAR.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30040v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30040v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30154","arxiv":"2611.30154v1","baseId":"2611.30154","title":"Preserve to Compress: Beyond Low-Dimensional Latents in One-Step Diffusion Codec","authors":["Chuqin Zhou","Xiaoyue Ling","Yuhang Wei","Yunuo Chen","Guo Lu"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】","abstract":"Pretrained latent diffusion models bring powerful generative priors to ultra-low-bitrate image compression, but their latent spaces reflect a reconstruction--generation tradeoff. Low-dimensional latents reduce modeling cost but can discard image details, while higher-dimensional latents improve reconstruction yet make diffusion optimization harder under a fixed computational budget. Generative codecs that adopt these pretrained spaces also inherit this compromise, even though compression requires fidelity to a specific source image. In particular, low-dimensional latent interfaces can discard source details before coding and restrict how much decoded information reaches the image decoder. We propose Preserving Information for Compression and Synthesis (PICS), a one-step generative image compression framework that adapts these interfaces to preserve source information. Our Information-Preserving Adaptors (IPAs) expose richer features for coding and retain decoded information throughout reconstruction, while deferred decoder adaptation stabilizes training and helps the image decoder exploit the expanded representations. Experiments on Kodak, DIV2K, and CLIC 2020 show average BD-rate savings of 52.64% and 39.61% over StableCodec at matched LPIPS and DISTS, respectively.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30154v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30154v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30351","arxiv":"2611.30351v1","baseId":"2611.30351","title":"NNS-GS: Non-Local Neural Scattering for Relightable Gaussian Splatting","authors":["Shiyu Liang","Yang Gao","Lili Wang","Aimin Hao","Beibei Wang"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Creating relightable 3D assets of translucent objects is challenging because changing the illumination at one surface location can affect the appearance elsewhere through subsurface scattering. Existing Gaussian-based scattering models typically predict scattering from local features, making this dependence difficult to capture. We present Non-Local Neural Scattering for Gaussian Splatting (NNS-GS), which learns non-local scattering together with geometry-guided local reflection. The scattering module learns how illumination from light-visible surface regions in light space contributes to the shading point in view space, conditioned on regional diffuse responses and spatial relationships. The reflection module supplies these diffuse responses and uses additional geometric guidance to better capture specular highlights and sharp shadow boundaries. To reduce component mixing and support controllable scattering appearance, anchor-based progressive training establishes the diffuse-scattering base before introducing specular reflection and supervises the components separately. Under calibrated point lighting, NNS-GS improves mean PSNR over existing methods by 2.47 dB and 0.81 dB on the synthetic and real translucent scenes, respectively. Additional synthetic comparisons further show that NNS-GS better matches the reference when adjusting scattering attenuation.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30351v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30351v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30945","arxiv":"2611.30945v1","baseId":"2611.30945","title":"Diffutoon-2: Toon Shading with Video Diffusion Models","authors":["Yuze Sun","Zhongjie Duan","Yingda Chen","Jingjing Chen"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["色彩迁移","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Toon shading renders objects with a flat, stylized appearance. Diffusion models open a new route to this task by transforming photorealistic videos into an anime style. However, existing approaches often rely on curated source and style training pairs or additional visual references. Diffutoon avoids these requirements through structure guidance and colorization, but builds on an image diffusion backbone with short-window motion modules and repeated overlapping-window denoising. We present Diffutoon-2, a video diffusion framework that learns toon shading through animation reconstruction. Two complementary modules independently reconstruct animation clips from outlines and blurred colors, with the video backbone frozen. At inference, the modules jointly guide generation using the same types of conditions extracted from photorealistic footage, requiring neither cross-domain training pairs nor external style references. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Diffutoon-2 outperforms both general-purpose and domain-specific baselines, across quantitative metrics and human evaluation.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30945v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30945v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.31466","arxiv":"2611.31466v1","baseId":"2611.31466","title":"AdProducer: Towards Agentic Advertising Video Generation","authors":["Kunyang Li","Chaoyang Wang","Mubarak Shah","Yuzhang Shang"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Recent advances in video generation have made automated advertising production increasingly feasible. However, an effective advertisement must faithfully represent the product and communicate its selling points through compelling visual storytelling, which requires more than visual synthesis: it calls for deeper product understanding and strategic decisions about how to present product value. Crucially, product research and creative design are closely intertwined, as the information gathered about a product determines which features can be faithfully demonstrated and how they should be presented. In this paper, we introduce AdProducer, the first end-to-end agentic framework for advertising video creation, transforming concise creative briefs into finished advertisements. Our multimodal agent autonomously researches products through web and image search and translates its findings into selling-point-driven storyboards that guide a frozen video generator. To achieve this, we develop a dedicated data construction pipeline that yields two training datasets, AdProducer-SFT and AdProducer-RL, and train the agent through supervised fine-tuning followed by reinforcement learning with joint optimization of storyboard- and video-level rewards. We further introduce AdCreation-Bench, comprising 305 advertising briefs across eight domains, with two complementary metrics: PG-Score for product correctness and creative execution, and Ad-Score for overall advertising effectiveness assessed by an independent judge. Experiments demonstrate that AdProducer improves the PG-Score of MiniMax H3 from 17.3 to 51.3 and generalizes to Veo 3.1 without additional training, improving its score from 28.0 to 35.9, with consistent gains in Ad-Score, VideoScore2, and human evaluations. Code, models, and data are released.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31466v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.31466v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30345","arxiv":"2611.30345v1","baseId":"2611.30345","title":"SceneCast: Learning Indoor Scene Synthesis from Curated Synthetic Videos with LLM Reward","authors":["Ziyang Zong","Canming Xia","Haoran Xu","Guang Tan"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"3D indoor scene synthesis requires generating objects to form a complete and coherent scene. Existing approaches rely heavily on manually annotated datasets, leading to high costs and limited generalization. We present SceneCast, an annotation-free framework that trains on large-scale, unlabeled videos, addressing two key challenges: (1) Videos provide only partial views of a scene, requiring the model to extrapolate unseen 3D regions, which is inherently error-prone; (2) Raw videos available online are uncurated, suffering from uncontrolled conditions and variable data quality. First, we design a Transformer-based model that predicts objects sequentially and is fine-tuned with reinforcement learning (RL), where an LLM acts as a spatial common-sense evaluator. The LLM provides reward signals through chain-of-thought reasoning and code-executable geometric checks to refine object configurations. Second, we introduce \\trim, a data curation approach that generates training videos with controllable video generation models. Crucially, it retains only high-quality prefix clips via multi-criteria quality assessment with an entropy-based weighting strategy. Evaluated on curated synthetic video sets, our annotation-free approach outperforms state-of-the-art supervised methods in scene quality.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30345v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30345v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.30057","arxiv":"2611.30057v1","baseId":"2611.30057","title":"FireDesmoke: Fire Smoke Removal via Real Smoke Transfer and Semantic Rectification","authors":["Chih-Chung Hsu","Rong-Lin Jian"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-23","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Fire smoke removal must restore dense and medium smoke regions while preserving the clear (smoke-free) content. Existing methods either leave the smoke or alter the visible content, and generative models may hallucinate people or remove flames. Real fire smoke is spatially local: the degradation and its corruption of semantic features are both concentrated in the smoke regions. We propose FireDesmoke, built on this locality: Real Smoke Transfer calibrates smoke fields on real-fire recordings and transfers their spatial layout onto clean images to synthesize training pairs, and Semantic Rectification uses pixel features to rectify the tokens of a frozen foundation encoder where smoke corrupts them. With matched training budgets, replacing the transferred pairs with SmokeBench pairs lowers dense-smoke PSNR by 0.74 dB, and removing Semantic Rectification leaves clear regions 1.31 dB below the input. On what is, to the best of our knowledge, the first paired dataset of real fire smoke for image restoration, with 1,500 test frames from a held-out viewpoint, FireDesmoke improves dense- and medium-smoke PSNR by 3.73 and 2.63 dB while keeping clear regions at the input level (+0.41 dB); no other compared method gains at least 1 dB in dense smoke without lowering clear-region PSNR, and no hallucinated person is found in its outputs. It achieves state-of-the-art overall results on this benchmark and, trained on SmokeBench, the highest PSNR with the second-best SSIM and LPIPS.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.30057v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.30057v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-22","count":8,"star5Count":2,"star4Count":2,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.29389","arxiv":"2611.29389v1","baseId":"2611.29389","title":"PINE: Point, Instance and Normal Estimation in One-Step Diffusion Model","authors":["Zeyu Ren","Jie Ni","Rui Qian","Jiahao Wei","Hao Tang","Yongtai Liu"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】","abstract":"Understanding a scene requires both geometry and semantics: geometry recovers its spatial structure, and semantics separates it into objects. Current models obtain the two from separate networks or separate passes. We present PINE, a one-step Omni Diffusion Transformer that predicts the point map, the surface normals, and the instance map of an image in one forward pass. PINE treats each map as a token stream, and a stream mask decides which streams interact. A camera-aware ray encoding positions every token by its camera ray rather than its pixel. Grounding attention adds the affinity of pretrained DINOv3 features to the attention logits. Trained on only 139K synthetic images for geometry, PINE achieves state-of-the-art point maps whether or not intrinsics are given and the best average rank on surface normals. It also exceeds SAM on large objects with less than 1 percent of its training images, and it predicts all three maps 5.7x faster than MoGe-3 with SAM.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29389v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29389v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29618","arxiv":"2611.29618v1","baseId":"2611.29618","title":"Mass Forcing: On-Policy Mass-Aware Distillation for Autoregressive Video Generation","authors":["Yiyu Li","Fei Wei","Yong Wang","Zaiquan Yang","Gerhard Petrus Hancke","Xiangxiang Chu"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Distribution Matching Distillation (DMD) has emerged as a promising paradigm for real-time video synthesis, distilling pretrained diffusion models into few-step autoregressive generators. However, its reverse-KL objective is mode-seeking and can collapse the student's conditional video distribution onto a narrow subset of modes, leading to reduced sample diversity, diminished temporal dynamics, and color oversaturation. Existing methods mitigate this issue mainly via coverage-oriented student initialization, but we observe that such coverage is transient: across three initializations, the fraction of dynamic videos drops sharply during subsequent DMD training, indicating that the mode-seeking objective, not the initialization, drives this degradation. We propose Mass Forcing, a probability-mass-aware framework that moves on-policy DMD supervision from whole videos to individual states. Each update branches several continuations from a shared student-generated prefix, making the student's probability mass at that state measurable against the teacher. First, Mass Forcing estimates relative teacher-to-student density ratios among these continuations through latent-space score integration and uses them to rebalance conditional DMD updates, amplifying rollouts underrepresented relative to the teacher. Second, since mismatched mode probabilities do not necessarily induce strong local score corrections, it aligns generated rollout sets with a reweighted reference distribution defined by the same mass estimates, providing cross-rollout supervision. Experiments on MovieGen and VBench show that Mass Forcing improves video fidelity and sustains temporal dynamics throughout training, achieving the highest Total scores among compared methods and a Dynamic Degree of 83% after 1,000 generator updates, versus 12-32% for the baselines, with no additional inference-time overhead.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29618v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29618v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29431","arxiv":"2611.29431v1","baseId":"2611.29431","title":"HorizonFlow: Few-Step Video Distillation in 600 Iterations","authors":["Xianglong Guo","Haobo Xu","Zanyi Wang","Zhen Lei","Chang Yu"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Distribution matching distillation (DMD) enables fast video generation by distilling a multi-step teacher into a few-step student. However, DMD-based methods often suffer from unnatural motion and costly training. We trace these limitations to the DMD gradient, which jointly optimizes appearance, layout, and motion without distinguishing their learning dynamics. Its static component, which carries appearance and layout, has a much higher signal-to-noise ratio than its motion component. As a result, per-frame quality converges within a few hundred iterations, whereas motion lags far behind. Beyond being noisy, the gradient also lacks two signals. (1) Video VAEs encode several frames into one latent frame (four in Wan2.1), and the gradient provides little guidance for spreading motion over these frames, causing periodic stutter. (2) DMD does not constrain the final sampling step to preserve the layout set by earlier steps, so objects merge and drift once the video moves. We propose HorizonFlow, which decouples the correction of motion from that of layout. It keeps the DMD loss and only shifts its regression target, with one training-only correction in the motion component and one in the orthogonal layout component. Chunk Evenness addresses (1) by spreading the motion of each latent chunk evenly over its frames, and Semantic Commitment addresses (2) by anchoring the coarse layout to the teacher's completion of the student's last step. In only 600 iterations, HorizonFlow distills Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B into a 4-step model using 8.4k training samples, 4.6-304$\\times$ fewer than prior work. On VBench, HorizonFlow outperforms all released few-step Wan2.1-T2V-1.3B models. The same recipe transfers to Cosmos-Predict2.5-2B, where our student outperforms NVIDIA's distilled model. Code: https://github.com/XIANGLONGGUO/HorizonFlow-repo","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29431v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29431v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29728","arxiv":"2611.29728v1","baseId":"2611.29728","title":"Reasoning Beyond Imitation: Observation Conditioned Distillation for Video LLMs","authors":["Junzhou Chen","Jindong Wang","Gang Zhou"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Reasoning distillation offers a promising approach to transfer temporal reasoning capabilities of powerful Video-LLMs to open-source student models. However, teacher and student Video-LLMs may construct different temporal observations from the same video due to the adoption of distinct video sampling pipelines. A reasoning trajectory valid under the teacher's observations may rely on evidence inaccessible to the student and thus cannot be directly executed based on the student's observations. We formalize this as a conditional target mismatch, thereby revealing the Reasoning Acquisition-Execution Gap. To bridge this gap, we propose an acquisition-to-execution framework that first distills teacher trajectories into a student reasoning prior and then further optimizes the execution capability of this prior across multiple observations. We further introduce Execution-Aware Reference Regularization, which retains the existing prior when it is executable under current observations, while allowing a greater degree of adaptive adjustment when it is not. Experiments across multiple Video-LLM backbones and six video reasoning benchmarks show that explicitly optimizing observation-conditioned execution consistently outperforms supervised distillation and standard RL-based optimization, with gains extending to held-out out-of-domain benchmarks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29728v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29728v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29257","arxiv":"2611.29257v1","baseId":"2611.29257","title":"CFANet: Learning Conflict-Free Weather Adapters for All-Weather Image Restoration","authors":["Minglong Xue","Wenchao Yan","Guoan Guan","Palaiahnakote Shivakumara","Yirui Wu"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"All-weather image restoration aims to recover clean images from diverse weather degradations such as rain, haze, and snow. Most existing all-in-one methods handle different weather types with a unified network or rely on single-label weather modeling. However, real-world adverse weather is often compositional, where multiple known weather primitives may coexist in a single image. To this end, we propose Conflict-Free Adapter Network (CFANet) for open-compositional all-weather image restoration. CFANet first employs a Weather Mixture Router (WMR) to predict non-exclusive composition weights for rain, haze, and snow from shallow features. Guided by these weights, a Shared-Private Weather Adapter (SPWA) dynamically composes a common adapter with weather-private adapters inside each Conflict-Free Adapter Block, preserving shared restoration capability while providing weather-specific residual adaptation. Moreover, a lightweight Natural Color Adapter (NCA) performs constrained color calibration to improve color fidelity and visual naturalness. Extensive experiments demonstrate that the proposed method achieves competitive adverse weather image restoration performance and benefits downstream visual tasks. Our code is available at https://github.com/wenchao-tech/CFANet-main","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29257v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29257v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29388","arxiv":"2611.29388v1","baseId":"2611.29388","title":"SpectralGS: Spectral Gaussian Splatting for Unsupervised Spectral Super-Resolution","authors":["Qiaoying Qu","Bin Pan","Chen Jia","Haiyang Xu","Xia Xu","Zhenwei Shi"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Unsupervised spectral super-resolution aims to recover spectral information from RGB images without paired hyperspectral data. However, the limited spectral information in RGB observations makes learning an effective spatial-spectral representation challenging, requiring the incorporation of structured spectral modeling with sensor imaging. In this paper, we propose SpectralGS, a spectral Gaussian splatting framework comprising two components: Shared Spectral Gaussian Representation and Spectral Response Projection Rendering. The representation module constructs a continuous spatial-spectral representation via shared spectral and local spatial Gaussian primitives, exploiting the low-rank spectral structure of hyperspectral images. The rendering module utilizes the spectral response function (SRF) to apply integral projection to the continuous representation, blending it to generate an RGB image. Consistency between the rendered and observed RGB images provides training supervision. Our main contribution is a Gaussian splatting framework that integrates structured continuous spatial-spectral representation with imaging response-based rendering, enabling unsupervised spectral reconstruction. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method outperforms current state-of-the-art methods.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29388v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29388v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29232","arxiv":"2611.29232v1","baseId":"2611.29232","title":"Why Adaptive Optimizers Struggle with Large Convolution Kernels","authors":["Tianyu Ding","Jiachen Jiang","Jinxin Zhou","Yong Ma","Zhihui Zhu","Luming Liang"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Large convolution kernels enlarge the receptive fields of ConvNets at a modest computational cost, but they are notoriously difficult to train. Existing large-kernel networks therefore rely on costly training techniques, such as parallel small-kernel branches with batch normalization. In this paper, we trace this difficulty to adaptive optimizers such as Adam, which normalize the update of every weight separately. Since input features rarely average to zero, all weights of a kernel share a common gradient, and these optimizers move the mean of a $k\\times k$ kernel $k$ times as far as a single parameter. We find this mean amplification in widely used ConvNets and vision transformers, and show that it can force the maximal learning rate to fall as $1/k^2$. We further prove that the basis of the discrete cosine transform (DCT) isolates the mean and is the only orthonormal basis that diagonalizes every stationary gradient statistic with mirrored boundaries. We therefore train large kernels through their DCT coefficients and convert them back after training. The resulting method, DCT-Rep, requires only a few lines of code and adds no parameters, no inference cost and negligible training overhead. Extensive experiments on image classification, object detection, semantic segmentation and image restoration show that DCT-Rep consistently improves large-kernel ConvNets and vision transformers, by $+0.79\\%$ top-1 accuracy for a large-kernel ConvNeXt-T and $+0.43\\%$ for ViT-S/16 on ImageNet-1K, $+1.4$ box AP on COCO, $+1.2$ mIoU on ADE20K and up to $+0.4$ dB on image deblurring, where the gain grows with the kernel size.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29232v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29232v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29138","arxiv":"2611.29138v1","baseId":"2611.29138","title":"Supply the Global, Supervise the Local: Combining Representation Conditioning and Alignment in Diffusion Transformers","authors":["Chaojie Ji","Sergio Quiroga-Sandoval","Ruxin Wang","Zheju Zheng","Yankai Cao"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-22","updated":"2026-11-22","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":38,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"A pretrained image representation helps train a diffusion transformer in several roles, including as an alignment target, which the model's hidden states learn to match, as in representation alignment (REPA), and as a condition, an extra input describing the image to be generated. Combined, they would give the model an input and a target, but whether they conflict is unknown. To test this, we let the condition carry only part of REPA's target: we split the representation of each image patch into an image-level (global) part, shared by all patches of the image, and a within-image (local) part, the patch's difference from the image-level part, and use one token carrying the image-level part as the condition. Then, the two roles overlap on the image-level part, where they could conflict, while only REPA supervises the within-image part, where we can see whether the token helps REPA. We find that the token and REPA together teach both the image-level part and the within-image part, the main source of REPA's FID improvement, far more than the sum of what each teaches alone, so they cooperate rather than conflict. These findings give one design rule for combining REPA with an image-level condition, supply the global, supervise the local, which we implement as Global-Condition REPA (GC-REPA): REPA with the token as an extra input; the token carries no information beyond REPA's target and is drawn at sampling from a separately trained token prior. GC-REPA greatly accelerates convergence: on ImageNet 256*256, SiT-XL/2 + GC-REPA reaches REPA's 4M- and SiT-XL/2's 7M-iteration FIDs within 200K and 100K iterations (20 and 70 fewer).","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29138v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29138v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-21","count":10,"star5Count":2,"star4Count":2,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.28792","arxiv":"2611.28792v1","baseId":"2611.28792","title":"ArgsWorld: Unifying 3DGS Scene Reconstruction, Generation and Extrapolation with Autoregressive Diffusion Transformer","authors":["Weiming Chen","Shuaiyan Liu","Xinyu Shao","Zhewen Chen","Tian Guan","Yonghong He"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Autoregressive diffusion world models deliver strong video generation performance, yet they typically output only RGB frames rather than explicit 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scene representations, creating a gap between learned visual world dynamics and usable 3D assets for downstream robotics, simulation and rendering. To bridge this gap, we introduce ArgsWorld, an autoregressive diffusion model that also produces an explicit 3DGS scene. For each generation chunk, the diffusion transformer jointly processes clean historical image latents and noisy latents for new views. It keeps the historical image latents fixed and refreshes the scene features so that the Gaussian representation can be revised as new regions come into view. Our 3D Self-Forcing Distillation trains the model on its own generated histories using a mixture of single-view and multi-view data. Distribution matching supervises direct image latents and latents of Gaussian renderings at both generation and additional cameras. ArgsWorld unifies 3D scene reconstruction from posed images, scene generation from image or text prompts, and scene extrapolation across camera segments with disconnected endpoints. Experimental results demonstrate that ArgsWorld achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple 3D scene modeling tasks. Project page: https://m4a1tastegood.github.io/ArgsWorld/","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28792v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28792v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28809","arxiv":"2611.28809v1","baseId":"2611.28809","title":"RadAG: Reward-Adversarial Attention Guidance for Efficient Inference-Time Alignment of Diffusion Models","authors":["Hyeongchan Jeon","Kwanyoung Kim"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习 · 训练范式】","abstract":"Diffusion and flow-based models have achieved remarkable success in generation, yet their samples often remain suboptimal in fidelity and text alignment. Two lines of inference-time methods address this: extrapolation guidance, such as classifier-free guidance (CFG) and its variants, and reward guidance, which aligns samples with reward models of human preference. Extrapolation guidance needs only forward passes, but it builds its reference by heuristic rules unrelated to the reward and therefore cannot steer sampling toward a given reward. Gradient-based reward guidance steers sampling directly toward a higher reward, but backpropagates through the entire generator at every step. This trade-off raises the question of whether extrapolation guidance can steer sampling toward a higher reward without a full backward pass. To answer this, we introduce Reward-Adversarial Attention Guidance (RadAG), which constructs the reference by perturbing the attention logits of a single block against their reward gradient and extrapolates away from it. We prove that this update increases the reward of the clean estimate to first order, while its backward pass stops at the perturbed block instead of traversing the entire generator. On FLUX.2-klein-4B and 9B with PickScore and aesthetic rewards, RadAG achieves the highest target reward in every setting with 30-65% less time than gradient-based and candidate-based baselines.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28809v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28809v1","arxivCats":["cs.LG","cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28764","arxiv":"2611.28764v1","baseId":"2611.28764","title":"Contrastive Gradient Guidance for Test-Time Multi-Preference Alignment of Diffusion Models","authors":["Po-Yi Lu","Jui-Chao Lu","Paul Kuo-Ming Huang","Shih-Hsin Wang","Chun-Liang Li","Hsuan-Tien Lin"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】","abstract":"Aligning pre-trained diffusion models with multiple context-dependent preferences requires adapting generation at test time rather than relying on offline fine-tuning, which requires hyperparameter tuning and a new training run for each preference trade-off. Existing approaches compress diverse preferences into a single scalar reward objective for fine-tuning or test-time guidance rather than directly reusing existing generative experts released for different purposes and obtained through different fine-tuning methods. In this work, we propose Contrastive Gradient Guidance (CGG), a test-time compositional generative framework that reformulates multi-preference alignment as a composition of distributions. CGG represents each preference as a contrastive preference pair of favored experts and unfavored anti-experts, whose score difference forms a contrastive gradient guidance signal that combines denoising directions (gradients) from off-the-shelf diffusion models during sampling. Experiments show that CGG reuses pre-trained and fine-tuned models as favored experts or anti-experts to control output style and safety, and that it can be combined with existing sampling-based reward guidance. With a single fixed scale per pair, CGG composes five preferences at once and gives a more balanced trade-off across text-image preference, concept control, photographic fidelity, and safety than offline fine-tuning, weight-space merging, and reward steering.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28764v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28764v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28717","arxiv":"2611.28717v1","baseId":"2611.28717","title":"ReCoRain: Reliability-Coupled Content and Noise Conditioning for Event-Guided Video Deraining","authors":["Runzhe Li","Zhaocheng Yu","Binbin Song","Kui Jiang","Junjun Jiang","Xianming Liu"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":54,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Event-guided video deraining depends on reliable cross-frame correspondences, yet events generated by scene motion, rain, and sensor noise can provide ambiguous matching evidence. In diffusion-based restoration, an incorrect match can transfer unrelated content and correlate initial noise between unrelated scene locations. We propose ReCoRain, an event-guided residual video diffusion framework that couples temporal content transfer and noise correlation through a common assessment of correspondence reliability. This assessment combines RGB appearance, interval-event evidence, and forward-backward motion consistency. The content path aggregates neighboring features according to alignment reliability, while the noise path uses composed backward motion fields and their associated reliability estimates to regulate initial-noise sharing across the latent sequence. We use the same conditional noise construction during residual flow-matching training and inference, incorporating temporal noise coordination into the learned restoration process. Temporal supervision matches motion-compensated inter-frame differences in restored and clean videos under the same teacher-estimated flow and visibility mask, accommodating legitimate appearance changes. ReCoRain achieves 32.09 dB PSNR on NTURain-Syn and 28.73 dB on RainSynLight25. Enabling reliability gates in both paths reduces warping error by 33.0% relative to disabling both gates.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28717v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28717v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28836","arxiv":"2611.28836v1","baseId":"2611.28836","title":"Omni-H3-Image: Constrained Foundation Priors Usher in a New Era of All-in-One Blind Image Restoration","authors":["Zihao He","Songhua Liu"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Off-the-shelf unified image models such as 33B MiniMax-H3-Image, FLUX.1-Kontext, and Qwen-Image-Edit-2511 synthesize crisp textures yet collapse to 11.33-16.74 dB PSNR across 429 degraded public restoration scenes due to spatial drift, chromatic hallucination, and semantic fabrication. We present Omni-H3-Image (Omni-H3), an approximate three-stage constrained posterior projection framework over unmodified MiniMax-H3-Image and Qwen3-VL-32B using only a 0.92M-parameter auxiliary weather pre-conditioner (<0.003% of backbone weights). Omni-H3 unifies blind physics-VLM sensing with exact-32 px lattice anchoring, joint affine-Wiener-Butterworth projection in opponent YCbCr space, and closed-loop Z=4 text verification with sub-Nyquist abstention. Across 925 blind tasks against 11 external SOTA baselines -- five public benchmarks (N=647), Dev-160, and a held-out locked_test suite (N=118) -- Omni-H3 recovers +4.73 dB degraded-scene PSNR over Base H3 (21.47 vs. 16.74 dB; +6.27-10.14 dB over other omni backbones) while winning overall #1 DISTS (0.207), #1 NIQE (4.45), and #1 Edge-F1 (0.807) on all 429 degraded public scenes (MUSIQ 66.8 vs. 40-44 for discriminative SOTA), 76.59 dB on CBSD68 Clean, -91.6% Urban100 chroma drift, #1 2K Dev Non-Clean DISTS (0.202), and #1 locked_test 2K DISTS (0.173) and 132-ROI SubCER, cutting pre-annotated 132-ROI SubCER from 0.456 to 0.319 under non-Qwen RapidOCR (PP-OCRv4) (with recognizer-dependent tradeoffs under TrOCR-large-str).","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28836v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28836v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28762","arxiv":"2611.28762v1","baseId":"2611.28762","title":"Frozen by the Mean: Early Temporal Guidance Unfreezes Motion in Video Diffusion Models","authors":["Xuanhua Yin","Boyu Wei","Chengkun Yue","Shunqi Mao","Chuanzhi Xu","Wei Guo"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Video diffusion models can render convincing frames while failing to produce the requested action. Increasing classifier-free guidance (CFG) can strengthen motion, but also saturates and shifts colors. Scalar CFG assigns the same coefficient to the averaged and frame-varying components of its prediction difference, limiting independent control of temporal variation. THAW, Temporal Harmonic Amplification during Warm-up, retains stock CFG and adds a separately weighted residual during an initial denoising window. Its dose specifies the added norm relative to the current CFG difference, giving the extra guidance a consistent relative budget across sampling steps. THAW is training-free and reuses CFG's two model predictions with an unchanged network evaluation count. It improves cost-matched motion over scalar and scheduled CFG on five video models, with positive point estimates against existing guidance methods on three of them. On VBench, THAW raises dynamic degree by about 14 to 15 points over stock CFG while increasing total scores on a different set of three models.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28762v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28762v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28942","arxiv":"2611.28942v1","baseId":"2611.28942","title":"XShot: Cross-Shot Video Object Insertion from a Reference Video","authors":["Bangxun Tang","Chen Chen"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"We present XShot, a framework for cross-shot video object insertion, which adds objects to a shot by watching another shot of the same scene. Video productions show a scene through many shots, and objects added to one shot must appear consistently in the others. Most video editors take the appearance of new objects from reference images and their location from a text prompt, a mask or an edited keyframe, leaving this consistency to the user. XShot reads both from a reference video of another shot, which need not be aligned with the target, and needs no mask, box or camera pose. We build on a video Diffusion Transformer and formulate the task as in-context generation over the reference video, a few images of the objects and the target video. A geometry-aware cross-shot attention estimates the epipolar geometry between the two shots from the model's own correspondences and uses it to guide where the target reads the reference and the exemplars. We train XShot with an erase-and-restore data engine: objects are erased from one shot of a film or a multi-camera recording and restored from another shot of the scene. Experiments show that XShot places objects more accurately and renders them more faithfully than open-source and commercial video editors, while leaving the rest of the shot intact.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28942v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28942v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28670","arxiv":"2611.28670v1","baseId":"2611.28670","title":"Learning Continuous Scene Depth Representations via Cross-Modal Gaussian Rendering","authors":["Zhiwei Zhong","Lingyu Zhu","Baoliang Chen","Peilin Chen","Shiqi Wang"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Depth super-resolution with color guidance is a fundamental problem in geometric scene understanding that aims to reconstruct high-resolution depth using structural cues from color images. In practical RGB-D systems, the resolution gap between depth and color images often varies, requiring flexible upsampling factors. However, many existing methods are designed for predefined integer scales. Although implicit methods support arbitrary-scale reconstruction, they typically rely on computationally demanding per-pixel neural decoding and may struggle to recover sharp depth boundaries and fine structures. To this end, we propose a cross-modal Gaussian rendering framework that learns continuous depth representations for arbitrary-scale depth super-resolution. Specifically, we first construct hierarchical Gaussian representations for progressive depth reconstruction, with explicit spatial support defined by learnable Gaussian primitives. Building on this representation, we design a Structure-Aware Gaussian Aggregation module that combines spatial weights with cross-modal feature affinities to reduce aggregation across depth discontinuities. We then develop a Scale-Adaptive Gaussian Rendering module that adapts Gaussian spatial kernels to the target pixel footprint. To further facilitate cross-modal learning, we propose a training strategy that gradually introduces higher-frequency guidance information during training. Extensive experiments on benchmark datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method at both in-distribution and out-of-distribution upsampling scales.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28670v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28670v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28736","arxiv":"2611.28736v1","baseId":"2611.28736","title":"Dynamic Spectrum Re-parameterized Convolution for Cost-free Image Enhancement","authors":["Zhiyan Zhou","Hailong Yan","Hanlin Liu","Yongrui Zhang","Xiangtao Zhang","Le Zhang"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Image enhancement spans degradations with distinct spectral characteristics. Standard convolutions use fixed kernels, whereas dynamic convolutions gain adaptability at additional inference cost. We propose Dynamic Spectrum Re-parameterized Convolution (DSRC), a plug-and-play module that learns task-adaptive spectral responses during training while retaining standard convolution at inference. DSRC decomposes convolution kernels into frequency bands, applies learnable high/low-pass modulation, and incorporates lightweight residual branches. Owing to the linearity of these operations, all training-time components are analytically fused into a single kernel for inference. Experiments on low-light enhancement, underwater enhancement, and image super-resolution show consistent PSNR gains and generally improved or preserved SSIM and LPIPS across twelve backbone-task configurations, with unchanged deployed parameter counts and theoretical FLOPs, and no additional inference operators.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28736v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28736v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.29064","arxiv":"2611.29064v1","baseId":"2611.29064","title":"Single-Transformer Latent Diffusion","authors":["Hao-Yu Hsu","Shenlong Wang","Alexander G. Schwing"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-21","updated":"2026-11-21","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":38,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"Latent generative models typically rely on three separate networks: an encoder that tokenizes pixels, a generator that models the latent distribution, and a decoder that maps latents back to pixels. UNITE recently merged the first two into a single generative encoder trained in one stage, but its decoder remains a separate network. We ask whether this last network can be folded in too, so that one diffusion transformer encodes, generates, and decodes. Simply letting the shared backbone decode does not help. What makes it work is to cast all three modes as position-aligned sequence maps over one fully shared backbone, distinguished only by a mode token and the timestep. The resulting model, UNIFIED, is simple: one network trained from scratch in a single stage with only reconstruction and flow-matching losses, without adversarial losses or pretrained encoders (e.g., DINO). On ImageNet-1K 256x256 at matched parameters, UNIFIED improves gFID over UNITE at three sizes (~60M, ~115M, and ~215M) and reaches 2.08 with 215M parameters, the lowest among compared models under 250M parameters. On text-to-image generation, UNIFIED-B (218M) reaches a GenEval score of 0.87 at 512x512, exceeding single-stage pixel diffusion models with around one billion parameters. Full unification of latent diffusion is thus not merely feasible but beneficial.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.29064v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.29064v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-20","count":9,"star5Count":5,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.26963","arxiv":"2611.26963v1","baseId":"2611.26963","title":"Causal Diffusion for Extreme Super-Resolution in Interactive Games","authors":["Christina Zhang","Jenna Kang","Colin Groth","Akshay Jindal","Stavros Diolatzis","Qi Sun"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Existing game super-resolution methods typically operate at moderate upscaling factors, struggling to recover fine visual details from extremely low-resolution inputs (e.g., 120p). At such resolutions, super-resolution shifts from reconstructing existing information toward synthesizing missing visual details. While video diffusion models offer powerful generative priors for this challenging task, their dependence on future frames limits their applicability to interactive games. We present CIDER, a causal diffusion framework for extreme game super-resolution that synthesizes high-frequency details from current and past low-resolution frames, guided by G-buffers (depth and motion vectors) to preserve scene structure and temporal consistency. We collect data from two AAA games (Cyberpunk 2077 and Black Myth: Wukong). To reduce inference load, we develop a lightweight latent projection encoder tailored to ultra-low-resolution inputs, fine-tune a decoder (TAE) on our high-resolution outputs, and distill the diffusion model to reach interactive frame rates. Our experiments demonstrate high-fidelity 8$\\times$ upscaling from 120p to 960p at around 13 FPS, outperforming state-of-the-art game upscalers in visual quality and showing potential for 16$\\times$ magnification.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26963v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26963v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.27266","arxiv":"2611.27266v1","baseId":"2611.27266","title":"PP-CFG: Polarity-Preserving Guidance for Controllable Few-Step Image and Video Generation","authors":["Junyi He","Xu Peng","Chengming Xu","Sixiao Zheng","Donghao Luo","Chao Wen"],"authorCount":10,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Few-step diffusion distillation reduces sampling cost but often weakens adjustable classifier-free guidance (CFG) and runtime negative prompting. We introduce PP-CFG, an architecture that keeps positive and negative text conditions separate and combines their attention responses with opposite signs within a shared visual backbone. Its Scale-Time Polarity Mixer refines each condition using the guidance scale and denoising timestep, allowing prompt influence to vary throughout generation. The student retains a continuous scale input and selectable negative prompts with one visual backbone pass per step. We evaluate PP-CFG on Wan2.1 for video generation with distribution matching distillation (DMD), and on Qwen-Image with both latent consistency model (LCM) distillation and DMD. PP-CFG achieves 81.46 VBench in four steps and lower video teacher-student distances across guidance scales than step-distilled DMD. On NegElement-1K, our benchmark of 1,000 matched image and video prompt cases, it achieves higher target-removal rates than the corresponding teachers. These experiments support the method's generality across attention architectures, generation modalities, and distillation objectives while retaining competitive generation quality.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27266v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27266v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28265","arxiv":"2611.28265v1","baseId":"2611.28265","title":"DeFlowCritic: Dense Latent Reward Alignment for Text-to-Image Flow Matching Models","authors":["Zeeshan Khan","Xin Yu","Shizhe Chen","Cordelia Schmid"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习 · 训练范式】","abstract":"Online reinforcement learning (RL) has emerged as a powerful paradigm for aligning text-to-image flow matching models with complex user intent. However, existing methods typically rely on sparse rewards computed only from final decoded images. This delays credit assignment across the denoising trajectory and makes training computationally expensive. Moreover, simply combining multiple rewards does not reliably produce a Pareto trade-off, where gains in compositional accuracy often degrade aesthetic quality. In this work, we propose DeFlowCritic, an efficient online RL framework based on Dense Latent Reward Alignment. Unlike prior work, our method provides dense supervision on intermediate latents, enabling early-stage guidance for the denoising trajectory. To achieve this, we introduce an internal critic that evaluates noisy latents using features extracted directly from the diffusion model, a representation inherently better suited for latent-space reward estimation than standard image encoders. We construct a high-quality reward-modeling dataset with continuous scores for critic training, capturing compositional correctness on high-quality reference images. The resulting dense reward enables efficient online RL training and inference-time scaling. Experiments show that our method achieves a superior balance between prompt faithfulness and visual quality on GenEval2 and T2I-CompBench++ benchmarks. The approach also generalizes across text-to-image flow matching models, including SD3.5-Medium, SD3.5-Large, and FLUX.1-dev.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28265v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28265v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28402","arxiv":"2611.28402v1","baseId":"2611.28402","title":"Plan Big, Paint Small: Semantic-Aware Computation Allocation for Efficient Autoregressive Video Generation","authors":["Yixuan Luo","Yanjing Li"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Autoregressive (AR) video diffusion streams a video chunk by chunk, but large AR models are too slow for real-time use. We ask where in an AR rollout a large model is actually needed. Pairing Krea-Realtime-14B with Self-Forcing-1.3B, which share their latent space and denoising schedule, we find that the order of the two models matters: when the 14B model generates only the first chunk and the 1.3B model continues, the rollout keeps 95\\% of the 14B model's semantic advantage, whereas the reverse allocation keeps 16\\% at about ten times the cost. The large model decides what the video shows; the small model only has to carry it forward. We turn this into BigSmall, a training-free planner--renderer sampler. The 14B model plans the opening chunk and sketches the chunks in which content changes, which preserves motion, and the 1.3B model renders the rest; a faster variant, BigSmall, plans only the opening chunk. A cross-model KV cache that is approximate, windowed and lazy lets the planner leave and re-enter the rollout at low cost. On VBench, BigSmall is $3.02\\times$ faster than the 14B model at an unchanged semantic score and motion, and $1.55\\times$ faster than SDVG, which lets the small model draft first; BigSmall reaches $3.77\\times$ at the same semantic score, trading away 10\\% of the motion. On minute-long videos (LoCoT2V-Bench), where the 14B model degrades when it reads its own long history, BigSmall is $3.14\\times$ faster than the 14B model and scores higher ($+1.78$).`","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28402v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28402v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28274","arxiv":"2611.28274v1","baseId":"2611.28274","title":"Neural Fidelity Calibration for Informative Sim-to-Real Adaptation","authors":["Youwei Yu","Lantao Liu"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":92,"coreTags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习 · 训练范式】","abstract":"Deep reinforcement learning (RL) can transfer agile robot skills from simulation to the real world, but closing the sim-to-real gap by domain randomization requires expert physics knowledge, and simulator calibration from real data typically targets a few hand-picked parameters. Even then, simulators miss real-world effects, and environments reconstructed from onboard perception inherit sensing errors that limit policy improvement. We propose Neural Fidelity Calibration (NFC), which uses conditional flow matching to calibrate simulator physical parameters and residual fidelity domains online during deployment. Residual fidelity captures sim-to-real dynamics discrepancies and perception-induced environment uncertainty. NFC operates in a latent space so that realistic environments can be sampled from the inferred posterior for real-to-sim policy fine-tuning. The framework is informative: the pretrained policy is fine-tuned only when an anomaly is detected, sequential NFC seeds its proposal from the simulation-pretrained estimator, and a hallucination policy modulates posterior uncertainty following optimism in the face of uncertainty. NFC attains the most accurate posteriors among established simulation-based inference methods on five robots with up to 39 physical parameters and a 128x128 residual terrain. On a wheeled robot with a broken wheel axle deployed on snow, mud, and wet grass, NFC raises success on rough terrain from 26% to 70% and reduces orientation jerk by 88%. Code and videos will be released upon publication.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28274v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28274v1","arxivCats":["cs.RO","cs.LG","eess.SY"]},{"id":"daily_2611.27285","arxiv":"2611.27285v1","baseId":"2611.27285","title":"SIR-Agent: Skill-Augmented Image Restoration Agent with Recursive Self-Improvement","authors":["Yijian Wang","Qingsen Yan","Weiyu Zhou","Mingde Zhang","Axi Niu","Haokui Zhang"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Agents for Image Restoration (IR) aim to leverage Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) to autonomously perceive image degradation and invoke IR tools. However, existing IR agents typically rely on fine-tuning MLLM parameters to adapt to new IR tasks, leading to catastrophic forgetting of IR knowledge. To address this limitation, we propose a skill-augmented IR agent, dubbed SIR-Agent, that maintains IR knowledge in a scalable skill library and uses Recursive Self-Improvement (RSI) to facilitate efficient skill-library evolution. Specifically, we design a Chain Feedback-driven Skill Revision (CFSR) to evolve the skill library. The CFSR analyzes restoration chains to identify and revise weak operations in existing skills. The compatibility of a revised skill with known tasks decides if it replaces the original skill or is appended independently. Furthermore, to advance CFSR's skill-library evolution, we present an RSI-driven skill meta-learning mechanism, which identifies CFSR's inefficient operations and autonomously refines its revision strategy. Extensive experiments across 14 IR benchmarks, covering 6 single-degradation and 8 mixed-degradation scenarios, demonstrate SIR-Agent's superior IR performance. Further evaluation on 7 unseen tasks validates SIR-Agent's generalization to new IR tasks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27285v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27285v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.27074","arxiv":"2611.27074v1","baseId":"2611.27074","title":"Hindsight Forcing: Learning Better History from the Future in Autoregressive Video Diffusion","authors":["Haonan An","Yihang Tao","Yu Guo","Renlong Chen","Jiasheng Zhang","Yuguang Fang"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Few-step autoregressive video diffusion enables real-time streaming generation, with each generated chunk serving as historical context for subsequent generation. To avoid the memory explosion of backpropagation through time, previous self-rollout methods typically truncate cross-chunk gradients or detach historical latents. As a result, earlier denoising computations receive no downstream feedback through the generated history on how to synthesize frames that facilitate subsequent generation, leading to visual drift and structural collapse as rollouts extend. To address this limitation, we propose Hindsight Forcing, which allows historical chunks to learn directly from future outcomes by decoupling forward generation from backward credit assignment via a latent straight-through bridge. In the forward pass, each chunk completes a full multi-step rollout to update the KV cache. In the backward pass, the bridge routes gradients from future losses back to a single selected denoising step per historical chunk, training both the context writer and historical denoiser on shared parameters without retaining the full trajectory graph. Remarkably, Hindsight Forcing requires no additional model parameters, architecture modifications, or separate trajectory replay. Extensive experiments on VBench demonstrate that Hindsight Forcing outperforms existing baselines in overall visual quality and dynamic motion, while ablation studies validate the benefit of supervising historical denoising. Code is available at https://github.com/haonanAN309/Hindsight-Forcing.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27074v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27074v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.27268","arxiv":"2611.27268v1","baseId":"2611.27268","title":"Budgeted Safety Verification for Streaming Video Generation","authors":["Yuxin Cao","Ziqi Ding","Jiayu Zhang","Xianglin Yang","Wei Song","Jin Song Dong"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"A streamed video generator releases each chunk the moment that it is produced, so a safety decision that waits for the finished clip arrives after the harm has been seen. We measure a guardrail by exposure, the harmful frames a viewer is shown, and ask where a fixed moderation budget should be spent. The accurate detector reads decoded frames with a video language model and is too expensive for every chunk, so our guard reads hidden states the generator has computed already and spends a cheap score on deciding when to call it. On 599 streams from a distilled causal generator the undefended model shows 46.25 harmful frames of 81. The video model on every chunk leaves 0.91 frames at 80.1 scored per benign stream, and the same model triggered by our guard leaves 2.35 at 5.3. Spending that budget at random leaves 37.20 frames, so the gain is in the ordering, and a frame detector in the same role gives up almost six, so the judging stage must read the chunk as a clip. The ordering is worth more as the stream lengthens, and the guard carries to a second system. An independent guardrail model agrees with our oracle at $κ=0.74$, and a blinded audit by 50 participants puts its precision at 96.1% and recall at 82.8%. Warning. This paper contains examples of generated content that some readers may find disturbing or offensive in nature.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27268v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27268v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.27870","arxiv":"2611.27870v1","baseId":"2611.27870","title":"Beyond Visual Fidelity: Comparing Categorical and Continuous World Models","authors":["Sarah Parisot","Melanie Fernandez Pradier","David Bignell","Sergio Valcarcel Macua","Katja Hofmann"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-20","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":38,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】","abstract":"Visual world models can now generate compelling interactive worlds, but visual realism does not guarantee faithful dynamics. We systematically compare how categorical and continuous prediction shape learned dynamics under both next-token and iterative whole-frame generation. These choices are often bundled in approaches such as autoregressive token models and whole-frame flow matching. We design controlled 2D and 3D environments and evaluate dynamics validity, calibration, and visual quality. In 2D with lossless visual encoding, categorical models achieve higher rollout validity with less training compute, particularly for stochastic entities. Among categorical models, greedy decoding produces the highest rollout validity, while sampling from a next-token model yields the best-calibrated stochastic predictions. Failures in continuous models, such as disappearing game characters, echo those seen in more complex environments. Here, known dynamics let us measure their impact. In 3D, categorical models also achieve stronger long-horizon performance with less training compute. Even models with lossy tokenization remain competitive on semantic accuracy and FVD, despite lower next-frame RGB accuracy. Together, these findings make a strong case for categorical prediction as a compute-efficient route to learning world dynamics.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.27870v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.27870v1","arxivCats":["cs.LG","cs.AI"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-19","count":12,"star5Count":4,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.25847","arxiv":"2611.25847v1","baseId":"2611.25847","title":"TrajCal: Trajectory-Calibrated Time Schedules for Few-Step Flow Sampling","authors":["Junchi Zhang","Zihan Gu","Jiangcan Jia","Lulu Zhao","Wenbo Su","Hua Zhang"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Flow matching enables high-quality image and video generation, but its iterative sampling process requires repeated evaluations of large neural networks. Existing few-step methods can distort the output and reduce its agreement with a many-step reference. We propose TrajCal, a training-free calibration framework for few-step sampling. Based on scatter search, a well-established metaheuristic for difficult optimization problems, TrajCal starts with diverse seed schedules, deforms and combines these candidates, and selects one fixed schedule by final output fidelity for reuse on new inputs. For both image and video generation, TrajCal achieves higher reference fidelity than all compared baselines at low evaluation budgets while maintaining competitive visual quality. The search is effective even with randomly generated seeds, while trajectory-based seeds further improve fidelity. For cross-style generalization, photography-calibrated schedules also improve fidelity on unseen image styles.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.25847v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.25847v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.26178","arxiv":"2611.26178v2","baseId":"2611.26178","title":"FlexVSR: Continuous-Scale Super-Resolution for Streaming Video Generation","authors":["Ji Wang","Haonan An","Jiasheng Zhang","Renlong Chen"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-23","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Real-time streaming video generation has gained rapid momentum in live content creation and interactive media. Deploying such systems in practice requires flexible scaling factors to accommodate diverse source resolutions, target displays, and computational budgets. However, extending fixed-scale generative video super-resolution (VSR) to varying target scales poses two challenges: downsampling from a larger fixed scale incurs redundant cost, while re-optimizing the diffusion transformer (DiT) is costly and alters the shared generative prior. To address both, we propose FlexVSR, a decoupled framework for continuous-scale streaming generative VSR with supervision at only a few scales. Our key insight is that naively resampling fixed-scale latents onto a new target grid induces scale-dependent input mismatch, which can be effectively addressed through parameter-efficient, scale-aware adaptation of the latent interface without updating the DiT. To this end, FlexVSR introduces ReLSA (Refined Latent Scale Adaptation), a lightweight latent adapter that constructs and refines target-grid latents before they enter the DiT, enabling a shared frozen VSR prior to generalize across continuous scales with only 0.274M trainable parameters and an adaptation cost of 7.3 H100 GPU-hours, while introducing no additional temporal dependencies and preserving the native causal streaming protocol. Extensive experiments demonstrate competitive or superior reconstruction and temporal quality, lower latency than most native-scale streaming baselines and offline methods, and effective generalization to unseen continuous scales, filling the continuous-scale gap in real-time streaming generative VSR.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26178v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26178v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.26179","arxiv":"2611.26179v1","baseId":"2611.26179","title":"RegionAgent: Two-Stage Preference Optimization for Regional Image Restoration","authors":["Weiyu Zhou","Jiacong Tang","Tao Hu","Axi Niu","Jinqiu Sun","Peng Wu"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["强化学习","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Images under non-uniform coupled degradations require diverse restoration capabilities for different degraded regions. Existing methods employ globally uniform operations, potentially overlooking regional differences and causing incomplete restoration or even damage to image content. Moreover, a degraded region may require successive restoration actions, and earlier actions may alter residual degradation characteristics or introduce artifacts, potentially hindering subsequent restoration. To address these challenges, we propose RegionAgent, an adaptive restoration framework that trains a Vision-Language Model (VLM) through two-stage preference learning to generate a region-specific restoration action at each iteration. Specifically, we first train the VLM to select an action suited to the current region through step-level Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), using preferences derived from immediate restoration feedback to supervise only the differing component in each action pair. To account for the effects of current actions on subsequent restoration, we then apply trajectory-level DPO, using preferences derived from complete trajectory scores to supervise the sole differing action in each trajectory pair. Furthermore, as conventional image-pair datasets do not directly provide preferences over regional restoration decisions, we construct a regional restoration preference dataset comprising 10K action pairs and 20K trajectory pairs for two-stage VLM training. Experiments across multiple datasets demonstrate strong restoration performance under both mixed and single degradations. Ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of two-stage preference learning in improving restoration quality.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26179v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26179v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.26540","arxiv":"2611.26540v1","baseId":"2611.26540","title":"AURA: AUdio-dRiven streaming Avatar","authors":["Nathaniel Cohen","Nicolas Dufour","Amélie Royer","Alasdair Newson","Patrick Pérez"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":["画质修复","表情与人像"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","画质修复","表情与人像","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 项目方向【画质修复 · 表情与人像】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Current audio-driven video diffusion models can synthesize realistic talking-head videos but generally require dozens of diffusion denoising steps over multi-second video chunks, making them unsuitable for streaming applications such as voice assistants and virtual avatars, where low-latency and online causal generation are required properties. AURA generates video chunk by chunk with a rolling KV cache, requiring only the audio of the current chunk to start generating, rather than a multi-second audio look-ahead like bidirectional models. It comes in two sizes: AURA 1.3B streams 576p video in real time on a single GPU (20.7 fps for a 16 Hz playback rate), making it suited to interactive avatar applications, and AURA 5B streams 720p video at 9.7 fps. Our two-stage distillation pipeline first aligns the causal student with the teacher, by training it to match the teacher's trajectories with fewer diffusion steps and a chunk-wise sliding window causal attention mask, then applies Distribution Matching Distillation (DMD) with backward simulation to close the remaining quality gap. On standard talking-head benchmarks, AURA 1.3B runs 23x faster than its own bidirectional teacher and reaches a quality comparable to TalkVerse at a lower resolution, and AURA 5B matches its TalkVerse teacher at 720p while running 17x faster. To support reproducibility and further research on streaming avatar generation, we release the weights of both models along with a dataset of 30K synthetic videos and their precomputed teacher trajectories.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26540v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26540v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.25523","arxiv":"2611.25523v1","baseId":"2611.25523","title":"Multi-View Relighting with Mergeable Radiance Fields","authors":["Yinan Wang","Rohan Kadkol","Hyunjin Yoo","Jean-François Lalonde","James J. Clark"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"We propose MergeableRF, a method for radiance field relighting through model parameter merging. Given multi-view images of a scene under known basis lights, our goal is to relight the scene by directly combining the parameters of radiance fields trained under these lights. This is motivated by the linearity of light transport: the image under a mixture of lights is the same weighted combination of the images under each light. However, combining the parameters of independently trained radiance fields does not produce the corresponding combination of images. To address this, we represent a scene with shared geometry and a separate appearance network for each basis light. We start these networks from the same initialization and introduce merge-aware training, which teaches their parameter combinations to reproduce the corresponding combinations of images. Given a target HDR environment map, we compute its basis-light coefficients and use them to merge the appearance networks into a single relit model. Our method supports both Neural Radiance Fields (NeRFs) and 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS), without material estimation, additional relighting networks, or target-specific optimization. Unlike image-space blending, it requires only one rendering pass per viewpoint. Experiments on synthetic Neural Gaffer objects and real OLATverse captures demonstrate relighting quality competitive with methods trained on the same multi-light observations.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.25523v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.25523v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.25934","arxiv":"2611.25934v1","baseId":"2611.25934","title":"AGSR: Aperture-Guided Super-Resolution for Gaussian Splatting","authors":["Ayman Alkhateeb","Hadi Amirpour","Christian Timmerer"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) enables real-time novel view synthesis, but Gaussian rasterization attenuates fine-scale scene structure through the projected covariance of each primitive. We show that contracting these projected covariances at inference time can mitigate the effective bandwidth limitation induced by finite Gaussian footprints, motivating a multi-aperture rendering framework for super-resolution. Based on this insight, we propose AGSR (Aperture-Guided Super-Resolution), a lightweight inference-time framework that progressively fuses multi-aperture renderings using uncertainty-guided feature aggregation and geometric conditioning from transmittance and depth cues. AGSR operates as a plug-in enhancement for pre-trained 3DGS models. Experiments on the Mip-NeRF 360 benchmark show that AGSR outperforms lightweight image-space super-resolution baselines, with gains increasing as the underlying Gaussian representation deteriorates. The results provide evidence that covariance contraction exposes bandwidth-sensitive structural cues attenuated by native Gaussian rasterization. AGSR achieves these improvements while maintaining real-time performance with up to 139k parameters.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.25934v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.25934v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.GR"]},{"id":"daily_2611.26456","arxiv":"2611.26456v1","baseId":"2611.26456","title":"Fixing the Last Mile: Failure-Aware Image Restoration with Region-Specific Guidance","authors":["Hongyu An","Fanghua Yu","Kainan Yan","Luc Van Gool","Chao Dong","Jinjin Gu"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Recent advances in image restoration have greatly improved global image fidelity, producing visually plausible results across most regions of an image. Nevertheless, even high-quality restorations often exhibit localized failures, including artifacts in semantically important regions such as faces and text, structural inconsistencies, and texture mismatches. Although these errors typically affect only a small portion of the image, they often dominate the overall perceptual quality of the result. In this work, we propose Failure-Aware Image Restoration (FAIR) to address the \"last-mile\" problem in image restoration. Rather than applying one uniform generative prior over the whole image, we condition a single-pass restoration model on both where local failures are likely to occur and what corrective guidance each region requires. This region-bound conditioning enables fine-grained and controllable restoration that improves semantic accuracy and stylistic consistency in critical regions while maintaining faithful, high-quality restoration elsewhere. Our method complements existing restoration pipelines by shifting the emphasis from global fidelity to local perceptual acceptability. Extensive experiments show that the proposed approach resolves common failure cases and improves perceptual quality, bridging the gap between technical accuracy and practical usability.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26456v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26456v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.25389","arxiv":"2611.25389v1","baseId":"2611.25389","title":"Zero to Four Views: Unified Panorama Generation and Preference RL for Unobserved Regions","authors":["Rui Hong","Xugang Ye","Jana Košecká","Kai Fan"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Panoramas can serve as shared scene backgrounds for multi-shot video generation, but most existing panorama generators take text alone or a single input image. We present a single model that generates a full equirectangular panorama from a text caption and K in {0,...,4} perspective conditioning views taken by a four-camera rig at one viewpoint. We fine-tune a pretrained text-to-image rectified-flow transformer in two stages. In the first stage, each conditioning view is appended to the token sequence together with the ID of its camera slot, and the number of views varies during training, so one set of weights serves every input setting. In the second stage, we improve the region not covered by any conditioning view. Supervised training computes its loss on noisy latents of the ground truth and never scores a panorama the model has generated and decoded, so we propose Uncovered-Region GRPO (UR-GRPO), a reinforcement-learning recipe that rewards the candidates a vision-language model prefers in pairwise comparisons on crops around this region. Because the slot ID alone does not tell the model which way the rig points, we also offer an optional variant that writes each view's camera direction into its position encoding, which lets the user decide where each view appears in the output. With one conditioning view, our model outperforms the previous state of the art, 360Anything, trained on the same data and evaluated with the same code, on Laval Indoor and gives mixed results on an indoor subset of SUN360; the second stage improves the results for every number of conditioning views.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.25389v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.25389v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.26629","arxiv":"2611.26629v1","baseId":"2611.26629","title":"Posterior Matching Distillation","authors":["Hidir Yesiltepe","Julius Berner","Arash Vahdat","Pinar Yanardag"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Diffusion distillation accelerates image and video generation by compressing a pretrained teacher's iterative sampling process into a few network evaluations. Distribution-based methods achieve this by matching the student's output distribution to the teacher's. This matching is often driven by reverse-KL objectives, whose mode-seeking bias favors dominant teacher modes at the expense of diversity across samples. We introduce Posterior Matching Distillation (PMD), a new member of the distribution-based distillation family that combines mode-covering posterior sampling with any-step generation. At zero population loss, PMD recovers the teacher's full posterior of clean images at each trained noise level. Its stochastic student supports any-step sampling budgets within the trained timestep grid without further training. To learn these posteriors, PMD matches exponentially reweighted averages of student samples conditioned on a fixed noisy state to corresponding teacher posterior means. Under Gaussian corruption, exponentially tilted posterior mean is a single teacher evaluation at a shifted input. We prove that, matching tilted means over an open neighborhood uniquely identifies the full posterior. PMD thus supervises the entire conditional distribution through a family of mean constraints, without any auxiliary student-score network. Experiments on SDXL and FLUX.2 [klein] 4B demonstrate that PMD achieves the best FID among 4-step methods on both models, while maintaining competitive or leading performance across prompt-alignment and perceptual-quality metrics.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26629v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26629v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.26040","arxiv":"2611.26040v2","baseId":"2611.26040","title":"Streaming-AAA: Streaming AAA Game Video Generation at High Resolution, High Frame Rate, and Low Latency","authors":["Zhihao Hu","Jianqi Ma","Jun Zhang","Jiayang Bai","Haifeng Shi","Yanxiang Zhuang"],"authorCount":9,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Controllable streaming video generation offers a promising route toward interactive game simulation, yet existing methods remain far from practical gameplay. They often fail to jointly achieve high visual fidelity, high frame rates, and low interaction latency, resulting in poor responsiveness and degraded user experience. We present Streaming-AAA, a new paradigm for controllable infinite generation of AAA-quality game videos. Our system generates 1080p gameplay videos at 43 FPS with 198 ms end-to-end latency interaction on merely NVIDIA A800 GPUs, while supporting long-horizon play without severe temporal degradation or collapse. Streaming-AAA is built upon three key designs. First, we redesign the VAE architecture for efficient high-resolution decoding, enabling real-time synthesis at 1080p. Second, we pretrain the diffusion model using diffusion forcing and significantly extend the temporal training horizon, allowing the model to capture long-term dependencies and maintain persistent memory. Third, we perform post-training distillation and memory compression to improve inference efficiency while retaining visual quality and temporal consistency. Experiments show that Streaming-AAA substantially improves visual fidelity, frame rate, latency, and long-term stability over prior controllable video generation approaches, enabling a significantly more playable experience for interactive AAA game video generation.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26040v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26040v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.26389","arxiv":"2611.26389v1","baseId":"2611.26389","title":"ViDecide: Teaching Video Diffusion Models to Reason and Plan Step by Step","authors":["Kaiwen Zhang","Yiming Gao","Zeqi Xiao","Xintao Wang","Pengfei Wan","Kun Gai"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Current leading video generation models can synthesize highly realistic videos, while still frequently violating physical and logical constraints in multi-step reasoning and planning, even after training with large-scale reasoning data or existing diffusion RL methods. We attribute these failures to the conventional video training paradigm, which treats a solution as a fixed-length clip rather than a chain of decisions. We present ViDecide, a decision-centric framework that trains video diffusion models to predict state transitions causally and decide when to stop, with a decision-focused training schedule. We further propose Corrective Fine-tuning (CFT), which corrects failed decisions by mapping their noise to the model's own successful continuations, achieving stable improvement without policy-gradient optimization. Across abstract games, planning tasks, and a new benchmark of multi-step robot planning, ViDecide achieves an average strict success rate of 83.8%, far above 23.3% for standard fine-tuning and 67.8% for the strongest prior method, and generalizes to larger puzzles and unseen layouts. Our framework therefore unlocks greater potential for video generation models as reliable reasoners and planners in the visual world. Project page: https://kevin-thu.github.io/ViDecide.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.26389v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.26389v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28146","arxiv":"2611.28146v1","baseId":"2611.28146","title":"Close the Loop on the Retrievability Gap","authors":["Guangyuan Dong","Wenhao Li","Zhen Tian","Jiaqi Zhang","Haoyu Wang","Junhao Xu"],"authorCount":10,"date":"2026-11-19","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"A frozen vision-language encoder now guides image restoration and later indexes what it produced. Over a fixed set of eighteen pansharpening systems on one WorldView-III test set, the fidelity index that ranks them, $Q2^n$ with a reference and HQNR without one, picks the less retrievable of two systems one time in three and one in two; the disagreement lies between the pixel-supervised and the language-conditioned family, and even the best system falls short of the reference, small objects first. We call this the retrievability gap and trace it to the area weighting of position-averaged losses. LEDGER closes the loop around the encoder: it indexes the input into a set of named concepts, generates, re-encodes the output alone with the same frozen encoder, and minimises a set-level soft-Jaccard loss on the coverage of that set, a label-free target available at test time, so that every present concept of a scale receives a gradient share independent of its area. Measured outside the loop by label-grounded probes, held-out retrievers, language-free evaluators, detectors and human raters, LEDGER matches or exceeds the strongest baselines in fidelity on four sensors, raises retrieval by seven to nine points within its vocabulary and two to five outside it, closes two thirds of the reduced-resolution gap to the reference, lifts extraction on raw SpaceNet imagery above the vendor product in each of four cities, and transfers to existing generators as a training-only loss at no inference cost.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28146v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28146v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI","cs.IR"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-18","count":12,"star5Count":2,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.24479","arxiv":"2611.24479v1","baseId":"2611.24479","title":"Every Query Routes Itself: Accelerating Video Diffusion Transformers with Per-Query Routed Sparse Attention","authors":["Zhen Huang","Shuwei Li","Ruizhe Yao","Xinrui Chen","Danyi Liu","Haoyuan Liang"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Self-attention over long token sequences dominates the inference cost of video diffusion transformers. Clustering-based sparse attention clusters both queries and keys and lets all queries in a cluster share one selection of key clusters. We show that this sharing limits accuracy: with key clusters and density fixed, attention grows more accurate as the query clusters are refined, and is most accurate when each query selects on its own, which needs no query clustering at all. We therefore propose Per-Query Routed Sparse Attention (PQRS), a training-free method that clusters only keys, lets each query select its own key clusters, and compensates for the unselected ones with their centroids. Per-query selections leave no query tile with a shared key list, so PQRS traverses key clusters instead of queries, buckets queries with identical selections into dense tasks, and merges their partial results in one pass under a fixed softmax offset carried from the previous denoising step. On four text-to-video and image-to-video models from the Wan and HunyuanVideo families, PQRS is the fastest of all the sparse attention methods we evaluate and the closest to the dense output, reaching $28.9$ dB PSNR on Wan2.1-T2V and $35.4$ dB on HunyuanVideo at $1.66$--$2.10\\times$ end-to-end speedup.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24479v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.24479v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG"]},{"id":"daily_2611.24941","arxiv":"2611.24941v1","baseId":"2611.24941","title":"UltraTex: Aligning Physical Knowledge for High-Fidelity PBR Texture Generation","authors":["Jingzhi Bao","Xiaohe Ma","Hong Li","Yuanming Hu","Xiaoguang Han"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":98,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["训练范式","打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Generating physically based rendering (PBR) texture maps for a 3D mesh from a single reference image is highly ambiguous, as the observed appearance entangles material identity, surface reflectance, and illumination. While current generative methods produce realistic albedo maps, they still struggle to infer accurate metallic and roughness attributes for relighting. We present UltraTex, a cascaded multi-view framework that moves beyond appearance matching by integrating physical knowledge into multi-view diffusion-based PBR generation. UltraTex first jointly predicts albedo and world-space shading normals to generate more detailed albedo maps, and then predicts metallic-roughness (MR) maps conditioned on the generated albedo, reference image, and material semantic priors. To inject material semantics, a lightweight adapter transforms hidden-layer features of a vision-language model (VLM) into intrinsic material representation tokens, providing part-level knowledge anchors for MR estimation. To model material-light interaction, photometric representation alignment guides intermediate diffusion features toward ground-truth material renderings under controlled lighting. This makes metallic and roughness variations observable through highlight intensity, specular sharpness, and local reflectance contrast, providing stronger guidance for fine-grained material prediction without explicit lighting supervision. Extensive experiments show that UltraTex generates high-quality textures and outperforms existing open-source and commercial methods in visual fidelity, material accuracy, and physical plausibility.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24941v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.24941v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28115","arxiv":"2611.28115v1","baseId":"2611.28115","title":"NOVA: Normalization-Aware Output-Guided Attention Acceleration for Video Diffusion Transformers","authors":["Wenxuan Miao","Haosong Liu","Aiyue Chen","Yiwu Yao","Zihan Liu","Anbang Wu"],"authorCount":10,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Video diffusion transformers (vDiTs) have become the main backbone for video generation. The quadratic cost of self-attention limits their efficiency at high resolutions and long durations. Existing sparse attention methods often rely on preserving high attention weights. However, these criteria do not fully capture the fidelity of the final value-weighted output, whose error depends jointly on changes to the attention numerator and the softmax denominator. We propose NOVA, a coarse-to-fine sparse attention framework that first approximates the full normalized output using size-weighted key/value centroids. Building on this estimate, we construct a residual proxy that accounts for coupled numerator-denominator errors by centering value contributions around the estimated output. This proxy guides the selection of query-key blocks for refinement under a predefined block budget for each query cluster. Selected blocks are evaluated through exact token-level computation, while the remaining blocks contribute through their centroids under a shared softmax normalization. On five vDiTs, NOVA achieves the best PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS among sparse attention methods, improving PSNR by up to 1.55\\,dB over the strongest baseline, while delivering up to 1.82$\\times$ end-to-end speedup on an H100 GPU.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28115v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28115v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.23674","arxiv":"2611.23674v1","baseId":"2611.23674","title":"Learning What to Degrade: A Recoverability-Aware Curriculum for Reliable Smart-Glasses Super-Resolution","authors":["Yaopeng Wang","Qingliang Wang","Yilun Jin","Xiujin Liu","Han Yue","Chen Xia"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Degradations in smart-glasses images obscure small text and fine-grained structures needed by vision-language models (VLMs) for visual assistance. We introduce an high-resolution (HR)-referenced semantic evaluation protocol that compares VLM outputs on restored images with those on corresponding HR references. Our evaluation reveals two failure modes: generative SR can synthesize misleading details, whereas restoration-oriented SR can leave critical evidence blurred or ambiguous. These findings motivate improving faithful detail recovery in restoration-oriented SR, where a key training challenge is to provide sufficiently difficult inputs while retaining recoverable visual information. We propose ReDiC (Recoverability-Difficulty Curriculum), which combines a model-independent recoverability proxy with model-relative restoration difficulty to adapt training degradation severity. ReDiC increases severity when inputs are recoverable and restoration is easy, maintains challenging but recoverable conditions, and reduces severity when estimated recoverability is low. It modifies only training degradation generation, leaving the SR architecture and reconstruction objective unchanged. Across NAFNet, SwinIR, and MambaIR, ReDiC improves average PSNR by approximately 2.5 dB and HR-referenced semantic scores by about 3 points over the corresponding baselines on synthetic benchmarks, with further gains confirmed by LLM-based and human evaluations on 200 real smart-glasses captures.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23674v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23674v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.23717","arxiv":"2611.23717v1","baseId":"2611.23717","title":"CoScan: Multi-Scale Content-Adaptive Space-Filling Scans for Causal State-Space Image Restoration","authors":["Wei Dong","Terry Ji","Yan Min","Shahram Shirani","Jun Chen","Han Zhou"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Selective state-space models (SSMs) have recently shown strong potential for efficient image restoration. A central difficulty, however, is that a 2D feature map typically needs to be linearized into a 1D token sequence for causal state-space updates, making restoration performance highly sensitive to the imposed scan order. Existing deterministic scans follow space-filling principles but remain fixed and content-agnostic, and can yield fragmented or semantically inconsistent contexts under degradations. We propose \\textbf{CoScan}, a \\textbf{co}ntent-adaptive \\textbf{scan}-order design for causal vision SSMs. CoScan learns image-dependent space-filling traversals via differentiable proxy supervision driven by \\textbf{scan-quality objectives} that \\textit{encourage short-range coherence and causal predictability, while preserving spatial adjacency}. To support hierarchical restoration backbones, we introduce an \\textbf{efficient multi-scale scan construction by coarsening a fine-level minimum spanning tree}, producing coherent scans across feature resolutions. In addition, a \\textbf{frequency-guided start selection strategy} is designed to \\textit{place informative structures early in the sequence prefix to strengthen causal context formation}. Plugged into existing Mamba-based backbones, CoScan yields consistent improvements across several restoration benchmarks with minimal overhead and shows promising results on high-level vision tasks. Code will be released at https://github.com/dongw22/CoScan","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23717v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23717v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.23889","arxiv":"2611.23889v1","baseId":"2611.23889","title":"Beyond Additive Composition: Interventional Modeling of State-Dependent Cross-Degradation Responses for Composite Image Restoration","authors":["Zongda Tang","Jie Feng","Wenda Zhao","Mingtao Feng","Yuping Liang","Ronghua Shang"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Composite image restoration aims to recover images corrupted by multiple coexisting degradations. Existing methods predominantly decompose composite corruptions into individual factors, relying on these isolated priors to guide restoration. However, coexisting degradations are inherently interdependent: the manifestation of one degradation dynamically shifts with the states of others, inducing non-additive interactions that factor-wise paradigms fail to capture. To bridge this gap, we formulate state-dependent cross-degradation responses and propose PROBE, an interventional framework that explicitly models how coexisting degradations influence one another. Specifically, we first construct unified degradation axes by aligning visual and textual degraded-clean offsets. Along these axes, a calibrated coordinate head estimates current degradation states, while bidirectional feature perturbations are injected to probe cross-coordinate responses under the current states. Contrasting these responses with corresponding intensity-matched single-degradation references quantifies intrinsic non-additive deviations, allowing the resulting response descriptors and state coordinates to jointly guide restoration. For comprehensive evaluation, we introduce SCD-15, a new benchmark covering all 15 nonempty combinations of four degradation factors at three severity levels. Experiments demonstrate that PROBE achieves state-of-the-art performance on the CDD-11, AWMM-100K, and SCD-15 composite restoration datasets using a compact model with modest GPU memory requirements.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23889v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23889v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.24023","arxiv":"2611.24023v1","baseId":"2611.24023","title":"STAR++: A Physical-Aware Benchmark and Framework for Astronomical Super-Resolution across Multiple Filters","authors":["Guohang Zhuang","Jiaze Li","Kuo-cheng Wu","Hao Du","Jinyang Huang","Xiang Zhang"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Astronomical super-resolution (ASR) aims to reconstruct high-resolution star fields from low-resolution telescope observations across multiple filters for astrophysical analysis. However, developing a physics-aware ASR framework faces two challenges: 1) Data view: no large-scale benchmark supports field-level ASR, especially on unseen filters. 2) Method view: Existing ASR methods often fail to preserve stellar flux across spatial resolutions. Moreover, ASR requires reconstructing high-frequency components, including accurate star locations and profiles, making it more ill-posed than natural-image SR. To tackle these problems, we introduce STAR++, a large-scale benchmark comprising approximately 1.7 million flux-consistent image pairs across 36 filters, derived from Hubble Space Telescope (HST) observations. STAR++ uses a flux-preserving data generation pipeline to ensure photometric consistency across resolutions and provides the Flux Error (FE) metric to evaluate ASR models from a physical perspective. To address method-level challenges, we propose a Flux-Invariant Super-Resolution Plus (FISR++) model that generalizes to unseen filters while accurately reconstructing flux-consistent high-resolution images. Beyond the flux-preserving modules, FISR++ introduces two novel components. Specifically, a Filter-Aware Adapter (FAA) incorporates filter metadata to adapt image representations to varying spectral and optical conditions. In addition, a Spatial Ambiguity Disentangle Loss (SADL) models spatial ambiguity as pixel-shift uncertainty, mitigating the smoothing of learned conditional mean reconstructions caused by this ambiguity. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FISR++ consistently outperforms existing ASR methods in reconstruction quality and flux accuracy, while generalizing to unseen filters, highlighting its effectiveness for photometrically faithful ASR.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24023v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.24023v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.24212","arxiv":"2611.24212v1","baseId":"2611.24212","title":"DetailScale-SR: Decoupling Restoration and Continuous Detail Control for Real-World Super-Resolution","authors":["Shengchuan Gao","Zhongjie Duan","Yingda Chen","Jingjing Chen"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Real-world image super-resolution often seeks the most detailed plausible reconstruction, although more detail is not always desirable. The same degraded image may require restrained restoration, faithful reconstruction, or enhanced visual detail according to user intent. We present DetailScale-SR, a preference-conditioned framework that continuously controls reconstructed detail for a fixed low-resolution input without altering the scene being restored. Rather than relying on post-processing or repeatedly rewriting prompts, it learns a continuous family of reconstructions while separating content recovery from detail preference. This enables a smooth transition from conservative restoration to richly textured results under consistent input and semantic constraints. Its controllable range also extends beyond conventional restoration: DetailScale-SR can enhance high-frequency structures and generate perceptually richer textures than the paired high-resolution reference. Text restoration is another distinct strength. For text-rich scenes, especially Chinese posters and signs, it recovers more legible and character-faithful text than prior generative SR methods under matched textual guidance. DetailScale-SR therefore unifies continuous detail control, beyond-reference enhancement, and reliable recovery of semantically important content. Our model is available at https://modelscope.cn/models/DiffSynth-Studio/Qwen-Image-2.1-SR","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24212v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.24212v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.24915","arxiv":"2611.24915v1","baseId":"2611.24915","title":"PrismRestore: A Compact Restoration Toolbox for Agentic Image Restoration","authors":["Zhaocheng Yu","Kui Jiang","Jing Cao","Yiang Chen","Xiaocheng Feng","Junjun Jiang"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Agentic image restoration composes operations to handle mixed degradations, yet its toolbox often requires storing multiple restoration networks. We propose PrismRestore, which constructs a compact bank of callable restoration actions from a single frozen all-in-one model. Our approach builds on task-dependent yet overlapping channel responses in the shared model. An offline task-prior search uses activation and gradient statistics to group channels with similar response profiles, then searches task-specific modulation coefficients over non-representative channels while leaving representative channels unmodulated. The resulting prior bank enables explicit action selection and composition without additional restoration backbones or task-specific adapter modules. A state-conditioned policy learns to compose these actions using Group Relative Policy Optimization. Its objective separately normalizes fidelity and perceptual feedback within groups of trajectories sampled for the same input, and combines terminal evaluation with return-to-go derived from stepwise quality changes. Experiments on synthetic composite-degradation and real-world multi-task benchmarks show improvements over the evaluated all-in-one baselines and restoration quality competitive with multi-model agents. PrismRestore achieves these results with a single restoration backbone and faster inference than the evaluated agentic baselines.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24915v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.24915v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28120","arxiv":"2611.28120v1","baseId":"2611.28120","title":"Separating Module Gain From Transport Plan Contribution in Audio-Visual Dynamic Facial Expression Recognition","authors":["Vu Nhat Minh Pham","Cong Khanh Nguyen","Minh Tuan Le","Van Thong Huynh"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["表情与人像"],"tags":["表情与人像"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【表情与人像】","abstract":"A cross-modal routing module can improve over its backbone while its transport plan accounts for no demonstrable part of that improvement. In ABVnet with DFEW, Sinkhorn cross-modal routing (SCR) exceeds the retrained baseline by 0.87 unweighted average recall (UAR) points, a resolved difference, yet sits 0.17 points above an individually trained, parameter-matched pooled variant, an unresolved difference that establishes no equivalence; under the development protocol the comparison with the baseline reverses. Separating the two needs comparisons a module-against-backbone test cannot supply: parameter-matched variants changing only the plan rule, and interventions replacing the plan without retraining. The evaluation covers MMA-DFER and ABVnet on DFEW and MAFW, and MMA-DFER on CREMA-D, with a single training run per configuration and paired fold intervals. ABVnet with DFEW motivated the follow-up after the broader results were available. Replacing the plan everywhere with a uniform one reduces UAR by 0.72, whereas permuting its rows gives an unresolved difference of -0.20 UAR points; permutation is less damaging by 0.51 UAR points, a resolved contrast. Replacing the plan with the product of its own marginals, preserving both marginals and removing only frame-specific mixtures, reduces UAR by 0.70. These reductions concern reliance at inference; attribution of the module gain to the transport plan remains unestablished. Under the 1.5 fold-dependence widening, the baseline gain remains resolved, whereas the replacement reductions and the paired contrast are unresolved.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28120v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28120v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","eess.IV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.24256","arxiv":"2611.24256v1","baseId":"2611.24256","title":"Spark-H3: Adaptive Block Sparse Attention for Video Diffusion Models","authors":["Zecheng Tang","Jiaye Fu","Youtian Lin","Xinyao Rao","Liuxuan Sun","Jian Zhang"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Long video sequences make attention a substantial cost in video diffusion models. Block sparse attention (BSA) reduces this cost by computing selected blocks exactly while compressing the remaining blocks. However, fixed token grouping can place tokens with dissimilar attention preferences in the same group, while uniform key averaging can underestimate the attention mass of compressed blocks. We introduce Spark-Attn, a training-free BSA method comprising Spark-Reblock and Spark-Reweight. Spark-Reblock recursively groups tokens by attention preference while preserving regular block shapes for efficient GPU execution. Spark-Reweight uses the global mean query as an anchor to construct attention-weighted key-value representations and logit biases for the compressed blocks, calibrating their attention mass. We instantiate Spark-Attn in MiniMax-H3 as Spark-H3. At 10% exact-block density, Spark-H3 delivers a 2.49$\\times$ DiT speedup and reaches 24.81 dB PSNR against the corresponding dense outputs on MiniMax-H3 (20 denoising steps, 345 frames, 1344$\\times$768). Increasing the density to 20% further improves PSNR to 26.76 dB while still achieving a 2.26$\\times$ DiT speedup. These results demonstrate that Spark-H3 achieves a state-of-the-art quality-efficiency trade-off for training-free BSA on video diffusion models.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24256v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.24256v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.24164","arxiv":"2611.24164v1","baseId":"2611.24164","title":"Gaussian Primitive Fusion for Any-Resolution Multimodal Images","authors":["Baoshuo Cai","Xinrui Jiang","Bing Cao"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-18","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Multimodal image fusion integrates complementary observations from different modalities into a unified representation. Existing methods typically estimate modality contributions on a common discrete grid. When source modalities have different native resolutions, constructing such a grid generally involves resampling, which may introduce artifacts and affect subsequent fusion decisions. To address this problem, we propose Gaussian Primitive Fusion (GPF), which reformulates heterogeneous-resolution fusion in a shared continuous Gaussian space. Instead of first converting source images to a common resolution, GPF directly fits their native-resolution observations with shared Gaussian geometry and modality-specific content representations, and learns modality contributions within the resulting continuous representation. Once optimized, the fused representation is rendered at the finest native resolution without resampling the sources. With heterogeneous-resolution inputs, GPF produces clearer target contours and finer textures, with less blurring and blocking artifacts than resample-then-fuse pipelines. Quantitatively, it achieves the best results on multiple infrared-visible benchmarks against state-of-the-art fusion methods equipped with interpolation or super-resolution frontends.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.24164v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.24164v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-17","count":13,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":4,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.23025","arxiv":"2611.23025v1","baseId":"2611.23025","title":"When a Larger Guidance Scale Repairs Text-to-Video Failures and When It Does Not","authors":["Taewon Kang","Matthias Zwicker"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":97,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"When a text-to-video diffusion model ignores part of a prompt, the usual first fix is to raise the classifier-free guidance scale. This paper is a measurement study of when that fix works. On Mochi, the same increase repairs failures on rare compositions (\"a snowy beach\") beyond what generating again does, does not repair negations (\"a highway with no vehicles\"), where it fixes fewer failures than generating again does, and changes nothing for events that must happen late in a clip (\"a sandcastle that a wave washes flat\"). Which of these happens can be known in advance, from the prompt and one inexpensive score. The score, which we call demand, measures how strongly the denoiser reacts to the part of the prompt that the failed video leaves out, and it is read from the first quarter of denoising, before any video exists. Our main finding is a condition: raising the scale is effective only when the prompt contains no negation and no event tied to a specific time, and when demand is sufficiently high, with recovery clearly higher above a fitted threshold than below it. Published corrections for the three failure types, used as fixed probes, behave as this account predicts. A short decision rule follows: before generating again, a user can tell whether the most common fix is worth trying, and what to use when it is not.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23025v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23025v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22495","arxiv":"2611.22495v1","baseId":"2611.22495","title":"Motion-Aware Reward Modeling for Instruction-Guided Video Editing","authors":["Li Li","Xiangbo Gao","Bo Ni","Samyadeep Basu","Viet Dac Lai","Jing Shi"],"authorCount":14,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Reward models for instruction-guided video editing can favor temporal consistency at the expense of motion. We study this failure, termed \\emph{motion collapse}, using controlled motion-suppression variants of real edits. On VE-Bench, the evaluated composite reward prefers a motion-suppressed candidate in $97\\%$ of cases. Learned video rewards and a VLM judge also struggle with mild suppression, while latent optimization and reward-guided fine-tuning exploit this weakness to increase reward as motion declines. A linear probe of our base reward backbone performs near chance on mild suppression, motivating an explicit inter-frame dynamics input. We propose a motion-aware reward model that combines an RGB frame-difference stream with preference training against motion-suppressed and fabricated-motion negatives. A distillation anchor preserves alignment with human quality judgments. On the VE-Bench motion ladder, our model reduces the rate of selecting motion-suppressed candidates to $0\\%$ while achieving a Spearman correlation of $0.57$ with human quality ratings. It also detects unseen suppression variants and preserves motion during reward-guided fine-tuning on two editing pipelines. These results support explicitly modeling motion in rewards for video editing.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22495v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22495v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.23054","arxiv":"2611.23054v1","baseId":"2611.23054","title":"ReTouchGS: Ultralight Render-free Aesthetic Color Enhancement and Transfer for 3D Gaussian Splatting","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Boyu Wei","Chengkun Yue","Xuanhua Yin","Yiyang Li","Haodong Chen"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["色彩迁移"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移】","abstract":"3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) enables photorealistic scene reconstruction. Existing 3DGS color transfer and interactive editing methods are guided by reference images and user-specified color adjustments. However, in many applications, users simply want aesthetically guided color enhancement that automatically makes the scene more visually appealing while maintaining consistent colors and tones across viewpoints. We present ReTouchGS, to our knowledge the first framework for reference-free aesthetic color enhancement of pretrained 3DGS models. It predicts a scene-level color lookup table (LUT) from the model and its associated camera parameters. ReTouchGS also supports reference-guided color transfer by fitting a scene-level LUT using one edited view of the same scene and its corresponding original render. A shared blend-aware color refinement network with only 44.7K parameters refines an analytic color update to realize either color mapping without rendering. It preserves geometry and delivers standard 3DGS assets in seconds. We also design the ReTouch3D benchmark to support the evaluation of aesthetic color enhancement and reference-guided color transfer across viewpoints. Experiments demonstrate high color accuracy and strong generalization to unseen scenes in both aesthetic color enhancement and reference-guided color transfer.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23054v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23054v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI","cs.GR"]},{"id":"daily_2611.23113","arxiv":"2611.23113v1","baseId":"2611.23113","title":"LUTBench: Benchmarking Text-Driven Photographic Look Transfer","authors":["Chuanzhi Xu","Boyu Wei","Chengkun Yue","Xuanhua Yin","Yiyang Li","Chupeng Liu"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["色彩迁移"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移】","abstract":"Photographic color grading shapes the tone and color of a photograph to achieve a desired look. Text-driven photographic look transfer lets users specify these adjustments in natural language, with the goal of reproducing the requested look across photographs while preserving their content. However, prior research on text-driven color grading uses different datasets, text instructions, target definitions, and metrics. These differences make it difficult to compare how faithfully the methods reproduce the same look. To compare methods under a shared protocol, we introduce LUTBench. We collect 91 three-dimensional lookup tables (3D LUTs) and define one photographic look per LUT with a specified input conversion. Applying these looks to 5,000 MIT-Adobe FiveK photographs produces 455,000 input-target image pairs with reproducible target images. For each look, we use templates to turn measured tone and color changes on fixed color samples into candidate clauses. We use training image measurements to verify applicable clauses before composing the text description. The evaluation measures target fidelity, tests sensitivity to predefined input conversions, and separates generalization to unseen looks from reproduction of seen looks on new photographs. Across 16 methods, stronger text alignment does not guarantee target fidelity. However, most supervised variants improve fidelity on both seen and unseen looks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23113v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23113v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22874","arxiv":"2611.22874v1","baseId":"2611.22874","title":"Harnessing Image Diffusion Prior for Photo-Realistic Video Restoration","authors":["Fanghua Yu","Hongyu An","Jinfan Hu","Xinqi Lin","Zhiyuan You","Jian Wang"],"authorCount":9,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":55,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Recent image generation models produce photo-realistic results with rich details, but transferring their powerful priors to video restoration remains difficult due to temporal inconsistency from stochastic detail synthesis. Existing video restoration methods improve temporal coherence but often sacrifice visual fidelity, leaving a gap between image-level quality and video-level stability. We propose a generative video restoration method that bridges this gap by enabling temporally consistent detail synthesis from image generation priors. Our method combines noise energy rebalancing to suppress structure-disturbing low-frequency stochasticity, motion-aligned noise warping to propagate details along object motion, a self-supervised denoising encoder to mitigate encoder-induced temporal uncertainty, and decoupled temporal attention to model cross-frame dependencies. Together, these components preserve fidelity, enhance fine-grained realism, and maintain temporal consistency. Extensive experiments show that our method substantially outperforms existing video restoration approaches in visual quality, detail richness, and temporal stability.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22874v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22874v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22222","arxiv":"2611.22222v1","baseId":"2611.22222","title":"ExplicitIR: Explicit Spatial Policy Fields for All-in-One Image Restoration","authors":["Dezhi Zhang","Shangrong Yang","Yudong Liang","Wenjian Wang"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"All-in-one image restoration aims to recover clean images blindly from inputs with various degradation types and levels using a single model. However, the adaptation policy in most existing methods remains implicitly encoded in feature representations, leaving the restoration decision neither explicitly represented nor independently controllable. Since real-world degradations vary spatially, applying uniform updates across the entire image leads to over-smoothing of clean areas and under-correction of localized degradations. To address these limitations, we present ExplicitIR, which represents the restoration policy as two explicit per-pixel fields: Budget, which determines where and how strongly to update, and Style, which selects between narrow and wide context windows. Specifically, ExplicitIR incorporates a Spatial Policy Estimator (SPE) to predict these fields, explicitly representing the restoration policy and capturing spatially-varying degradations, which are then applied by Policy-Adaptive Decoder Blocks (PADB) through adaptive fusion of narrow- and wide-window responses, enabling independent control and precise local modulation. This design enables spatially-adaptive restoration in a single forward pass, without requiring task labels, prompts, or test-time degradation estimators. Extensive experiments on three-task and five-task benchmarks demonstrate that ExplicitIR achieves performance competitive with the strongest all-in-one baselines.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22222v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22222v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22355","arxiv":"2611.22355v1","baseId":"2611.22355","title":"SandBAR: A Band-Asymmetric Architecture for Underwater Image Restoration with Removable Frequency Scaffolding","authors":["Yanzi Liu","Haoran Hou","Jiachi Tong","Shen Chen","Shuang Chen"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Light scattering, absorption and turbidity degrade underwater imagery through complementary frequency tendencies: large-scale, channel-dependent appearance distortions and local high-frequency detail loss. This band asymmetry motivates different forms of restoration for global colour and local structure. Our primary contribution is SandBAR, a novel unified band-asymmetric architecture for underwater image restoration. Rather than treating frequency processing as an isolated module, SandBAR organises the restoration pipeline around band asymmetry at the block, encoder and objective levels. Within this architecture, we introduce a novel band-asymmetric sandwich block with a reserved post-attention repair slot and the Split-band Parallel Asymmetric Repair Kernel (SPARK), a novel module that applies channel-aware large-support correction to the low band, which predominantly carries large-scale appearance distortions, and lightweight local refinement to the detail-bearing high band, thereby restoring global colour while preserving fine structure. To deliver the global cast to the depth at which the network represents global structure, a learnable low-frequency guidance path injects a low-pass view of the input into the deep encoder, and a three-scale spatial-frequency objective supervises the same axis throughout the decoder. Building on the reserved post-attention slot, we further introduce Post-attention Scaffold Training (PaST), a novel removable nonlinear scaffolding scheme that trains the permanent SandBAR graph under band-specific capacity and runs it at inference without that capacity. SandBAR reaches 29.89 / 28.92 / 22.77 dB on EUVP / LSUI / UIEB and establishes a new three-domain mean state of the art on the benchmark family, exceeding the strongest published result by approximately 1.0 dB. PaST adds no parameters or FLOPs at inference and preserves matched latency.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22355v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22355v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22655","arxiv":"2611.22655v2","baseId":"2611.22655","title":"Unified Residual Translation for Paired Image Restoration","authors":["Joyanta Jyoti Mondal","Mridul Banik","Ibne Farabi Shihab","Md. Shifatul Ahsan Apurba"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-24","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Paired image restoration spans changes in imaging contrast, sensor modality, and spatial resolution. These settings differ in their input channels and target grids, raising the question of how much of the prediction pipeline must be specialized to each task. We present the Unified Residual Translator (URT), which separates fixed channel and scale mapping from learned residual prediction. A deterministic reference uses channel averaging, replication, or bicubic interpolation to match the target dimensions, while a NAFNet encoder-decoder predicts an additive correction from the complete input. Separately trained instances share the prediction rule, $\\ell_1$ objective, and optimizer across MRI contrast synthesis, visible-to-infrared translation, SAR-to-optical translation, and real-camera super-resolution. Across six task and scale settings, URT achieves the highest PSNR and SSIM among the evaluated baselines on IXI, SEN1-2, and RealSR $\\times3$ and $\\times4$. It reaches 30.774dB on IXI and 13.478dB on SEN1-2, with luminance PSNR of 33.596, 28.742, and 27.342dB at the three RealSR scales. On LLVIP, it ranks second in PSNR and SSIM. A study of four spectral alternatives identifies complementary benefits of fitted references and bandwise residual gains. Together, these results support explicit dimensional alignment and learned residual correction as a common formulation for heterogeneous paired imaging tasks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22655v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22655v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22951","arxiv":"2611.22951v1","baseId":"2611.22951","title":"A high-dimensional spatial-physical coupling operator for generalizable super-resolution of three-dimensional turbulent flows","authors":["Zengyuan Zuo","Junjun Jiang","Fei Mu","Xubo Wang","Xianming Liu"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Accurate representation of turbulent flows requires simultaneously capturing multi-scale spatial structures and the complex, nonlinear coupling between distinct physical quantities (\\textit{e.g.}, velocity components and density). In practice, however, turbulent data are often constrained by low spatial resolution due to hardware limitations and computational costs. While current data-driven super-resolution methods have shown promise by learning mappings via convolutional neural networks (CNNs), they typically treat physical variables as independent channels. This simplified approach neglects intrinsic cross-variable correlations, causing models to struggle with high-fidelity reconstruction, particularly under extremely sparse conditions or in out-of-distribution regimes. To bridge this gap, we propose an architecture-agnostic spatial-physical coupling operator that extends the standard convolution into a joint spatial-variable dimension. Unlike conventional linear channel mixing, our framework constructs a unified spatial-physical representation within CNNs to explicitly capture local inter-variable interactions. By lifting features into a higher-dimensional manifold, this operator significantly enhances the model's representation capacity. Experiments demonstrate that this approach enables strong reconstruction performance and zero-shot generalization, offering a physics-aware inductive bias for super-resolution of three-dimensional turbulence. More broadly, this work suggests that high-dimensional operators offer a promising avenue for modeling intricate multi-scale turbulent flows.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22951v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22951v1","arxivCats":["physics.flu-dyn","cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22287","arxiv":"2611.22287v2","baseId":"2611.22287","title":"Decoding Without a Decoder: Direct Pixel Prediction from Diffusion Transformers","authors":["Hansheng Chen","Shengqu Cai","Jan Ackermann","Rayhan Zirvi","Gordon Wetzstein","Xinchen Yan"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":42,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Latent diffusion models rely on a decoder to map denoised latents to pixels, adding complexity and inference cost. We introduce Decoding Without a Decoder (DWD), which lets a finetuned diffusion transformer produce final pixels directly through a linear head. Unlike previous methods, where the denoising output leads to final pixels, DWD separates the two roles: the original denoising head continues to drive sampling, then the new DWD head predicts the pixels at the final step. We extend pretrained latent models with DWD efficiently by finetuning only the last few transformer blocks, training the DWD head with pixel quality losses while anchoring the denoising head to the pretrained model to preserve the sample trajectory. Across latent image and video generators, DWD removes the decoder while maintaining or even improving pixel quality. On Wan video generation, it visibly reduces detail distortion and flickering, lowering warping error by 23%. Remarkably, the same separation also improves pixel diffusion models that do not even use a decoder: a final-step DWD head produces substantially better texture detail in text-to-image generation, and improves ImageNet FID from 1.55 to 1.43, a new state of the art among pixel flow models.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22287v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22287v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22028","arxiv":"2611.22028v1","baseId":"2611.22028","title":"BATMoS: Budget-Aware Guidance Allocation for Efficient Video Diffusion","authors":["Zhixu Du","Kuo Yang","Jingwei Sun","Hai \"Helen\" Li","Yiran Chen"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"The effectiveness of a video diffusion model's inference budget depends on where computation is spent along the denoising trajectory. Across eight equal-budget inference configurations, VBench total scores span 4.80 points, highlighting the importance of explicit allocation design. Our key insight is that guidance evaluations are not equally valuable across diffusion time: retaining the sampling updates while selectively reusing guidance can preserve generation quality with fewer denoiser evaluations. We introduce BATMoS (Budget Aware Temporal Mixture of Strategies), which routes between fresh classifier-free guidance and cached guidance using a single pretrained denoiser. A magnitude-based temporal rule assigns guidance reuse to low-magnitude blocks and reserves fresh guidance for the remaining blocks, with a single integer controlling the computation budget. The resulting schedule requires no additional router training or per-budget threshold search. Evaluated on CogVideoX-2B with 4,720 videos per configuration across all sixteen official VBench dimensions, BATMoS achieves the highest total score among the eight allocation policies and comes within 0.10 points of full classifier-free guidance using 20\\% fewer branch evaluations. BATMoS also composes with feature reuse, reducing computation from 80 to 62 effective branch evaluations with a 0.11-point decrease in total score. These findings make temporal guidance allocation a practical mechanism for extracting more generation quality from a fixed inference budget.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22028v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22028v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22857","arxiv":"2611.22857v1","baseId":"2611.22857","title":"Beyond Co-Generation: Rethinking Jointness in Joint Audio-Video Generation","authors":["Jihoo Jung","Ji-Hoon Kim","Suyeon Lee","Gia Thinh Pham","Joon Son Chung"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Our aim is to advance the understanding of genuine audio-visual coordination in joint text-to-audio-video (T2AV) generation, with particular emphasis on semantic consistency. Evaluating audio-visual consistency in T2AV generation is nontrivial, as detailed text prompts obscure whether such consistency stems from genuine cross-modal coordination or merely from independent text following. To isolate the former, we introduce intrinsic jointness: the ability of audio and video to produce the same realization for details left underspecified by the prompt. Using semantically underspecified prompts (e.g., \"musical instrument playing\" rather than \"electric guitar playing\"), we investigate four recent models and reveal their limited intrinsic jointness. To address this limitation, we propose JointREPA, a simple representation alignment-based post-training method to facilitate cross-modal coordination. Since existing benchmarks use semantically detailed prompts, we introduce UNSAIDBench, a benchmark designed to evaluate intrinsic jointness under semantic underspecification. Experiments across two distinct models and three benchmarks, including UNSAIDBench, demonstrate that JointREPA consistently improves semantic consistency.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22857v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22857v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","eess.AS"]},{"id":"daily_2611.23417","arxiv":"2611.23417v2","baseId":"2611.23417","title":"Autoregressive View Model","authors":["Jensen","Zhou","Samuel Rota Bulo","Denis Rozumny","Duncan Zauss","Peter Hedman"],"authorCount":11,"date":"2026-11-17","updated":"2026-11-20","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"We present the Autoregressive View Model (AVM), which generates a scene as a stream of views without constructing an explicit 3D representation. Through a unified in-context interface, AVM conditions on images or videos to generate short clips along a requested camera path. Visual context grounds reconstruction and interpolation, while a learned prior enables extrapolation into unseen regions. AVM retrieves relevant views from inputs and generated history to form a bounded active context. We fine-tune a pretrained bidirectional video diffusion transformer with flow matching in an N-in-1-out formulation. We then tune a causal model on its own rollouts to improve photorealism, reduce denoising steps, and limit long-horizon drift. We scale training to approximately 8M scenes and video clips. We evaluate generation capacity and scene persistence across benchmarks for camera control, text control, visual quality, dynamics, sparse and 4D reconstruction, and long-horizon consistency. The results demonstrate that AVM adapts to diverse input and output settings through one in-context interface.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.23417v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.23417v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-16","count":12,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":2,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.20805","arxiv":"2611.20805v1","baseId":"2611.20805","title":"An Efficient Training Paradigm for Image Restoration via Improved Fréchet Loss","authors":["Shanyuan Jie","Wu Shi","Xinqi Lin","Kainan Yan","Zian Wang","Yingxue Pang"],"authorCount":9,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Diffusion-based image restoration achieves strong perceptual quality but typically relies on iterative sampling, leading to slow inference. Existing single-step methods often use diffusion distillation or adversarial training, complicating optimization. To simplify training, we propose FDIR, a framework for training single-pass generative restoration models through regression-style supervised optimization using directly computable objectives. To realize this paradigm, FDIR employs the Fréchet distance to match the feature distributions of restored and real images in frozen representation spaces, providing explicit distribution-level supervision. To better accommodate image restoration, we incorporate local patch features to capture fine-grained restoration discrepancies. Moreover, we introduce instance-adaptive statistics by combining the feature moments of each SR-HQ pair with synchronized historical statistics, allowing distribution matching to incorporate input-specific correspondence while retaining distribution-level context. FDIR optimizes only the LoRA parameters of the restoration backbone. Experiments demonstrate strong perceptual restoration quality and efficient training, suggesting a simpler paradigm for generative image restoration.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20805v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.20805v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21038","arxiv":"2611.21038v2","baseId":"2611.21038","title":"ScoutCache: Efficient Few-Step Autoregressive Video Diffusion via Conditional Readout and Selective Caching","authors":["Hao Tan","Jinghui Qin","Xuming Hu"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Few-step autoregressive video diffusion enables efficient long video generation, but inference still incurs substantial computational costs from repeated denoising and KV cache operations. Existing acceleration methods reduce parts of this overhead, while historical KV reading and cache construction remain costly across video chunks. To address these challenges, we propose ScoutCache, a training-free framework that jointly optimizes denoising, historical KV reading, and cache construction. We observe that shallow feature changes provide useful information for estimating clean latent updates, while historical KV dependence varies across Transformer layers and attention heads but remains relatively stable across video chunks. Based on these observations, we develop Conditional Denoising Readout (CDR) to estimate intermediate denoising updates from shallow feature changes and known noise variations. We further introduce Output Risk Guided Historical KV Reading (ORHR) to allocate historical reading budgets based on attention output perturbations, and Content and Coverage Guided Sparse Cache Construction (CCSC) to reduce cache construction costs by selecting informative spatial tokens while maintaining spatial coverage. Extensive experiments on Self-Forcing and LongLive using VBench and MovieGen demonstrate that ScoutCache achieves up to 2.28$\\times$ and 1.86$\\times$ speedups, respectively, while consistently improving generation quality without modifying pretrained model parameters.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21038v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21038v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.20629","arxiv":"2611.20629v1","baseId":"2611.20629","title":"Event-Guided Stereo Video Super-Resolution","authors":["Hanyu Xuan","Youtao Yao","Cheng Zhao","Kun Li","Xin Du","Fei Wang"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":55,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Stereo Video Super-Resolution (SVSR) aims to reconstruct high-resolution stereo videos by exploiting temporal cues across consecutive frames and complementary spatial cues between stereo views. However, existing methods struggle to recover high-frequency texture details under rapid motion and complex illumination, compromising reconstruction quality and stereo consistency. Event cameras provide valuable motion and texture cues with high temporal resolution and wide dynamic range, yet their potential for SVSR remains underexplored. To bridge this gap, we present the first investigation of event-guided SVSR and propose a novel framework. Specifically, we develop an Event-Guided Texture Enhancement (EGTE) module that combines bidirectional temporal feature propagation with event-guided texture refinement to restore high-frequency details in complex motion regions. Subsequently, we introduce a Cross-View Texture Interaction (CVTI) module to model cross-view spatial correspondences and aggregate cross-view complementary texture information. With this design, our method effectively integrates event-driven motion and texture information, inter-frame temporal dependencies, and cross-view spatial relationships to recover motion-affected details and maintain stereo consistency. Extensive experiments on both real-world and synthetic datasets demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms existing super-resolution methods in reconstruction quality and stereo consistency. Project Page: https://wzlwlm13.github.io/EvSVSR","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.20629v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.20629v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21890","arxiv":"2611.21890v1","baseId":"2611.21890","title":"Domain-Tuned Diffusion Super-Resolution for 4K Facial Asset Maps","authors":["Elly Akhoundi","Mathieu Lamarre","Thibault Goyallon","Patrick Anderson","Étienne Danvoye"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Physically based facial assets require 4K albedo and displacement maps for cinematic close-ups, yet generative pipelines commonly produce them at only 1K. Naively applying photographic super-resolution is a poor fit: facial UV maps have domain-specific statistics, and displacement is a scalar height field whose errors directly distort geometry. We present a domain-tuned diffusion upscaler for 4x (1024 to 4096) facial asset super-resolution. Starting from a bilinearly upsampled map, we adapt a frozen SDXL backbone per modality with lightweight LoRA modules, while a Tile ControlNet preserves input structure as the diffusion prior restores pore- and wrinkle-scale detail. On held-out maps, our method produces sharper, more faithful detail than bilinear and off-the-shelf GAN and diffusion super-resolvers, reducing albedo LPIPS from 0.21 to 0.08 while avoiding misplaced or excessive displacement relief. These results show that lightweight domain adaptation can produce render-ready 4K facial asset maps without a custom super-resolution architecture.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21890v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21890v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI","cs.GR"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21479","arxiv":"2611.21479v2","baseId":"2611.21479","title":"LynnReal-Hand: Unlocking World Interaction with Hand Control","authors":["Shaohao Rui","Chaoyang Zhang","Xiaofeng Mao","Weijie Ma"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-17","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":48,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Interactive video world models enable exploration through text and camera controls, yet direct object interaction requires finer control. Hands provide a natural interface, but translating continuous hand movements into coherent visual responses remains challenging because of complex hand-object dynamics and demanding real-time computation. To address these challenges, we introduce LynnReal-Hand, a hand-controlled video world model that unifies precise interaction modeling with efficient streaming generation. We curate 100K interaction clips spanning diverse real and synthetic environments and develop hand-aware supervision to improve fine-grained hand motions and object interactions. Building on this foundation, we learn a causal flow map for few-step generation and introduce region-aware score distillation that combines complementary video and image priors to refine interaction dynamics and visual appearance. To further reduce inference latency, we propose Ultra-Fast-VAE, a compact video decoder that preserves high reconstruction fidelity with substantially lower computational cost. Extensive evaluations on HandBench demonstrate superior hand-following accuracy and visual quality compared with existing approaches. Our full-sequence model achieves an FVD of 181.67 and a MANO Dice score of 93.02, while the three-step causal model maintains a MANO Dice score of 92.49. For real-time interaction, 540p streaming on a single H200 reaches 18.03 FPS with 741ms latency and an optimized peak GPU memory footprint of only 34.89GB. We will release the datasets, model weights, and the full training and inference code to support research on hand-controlled video world models.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21479v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21479v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21339","arxiv":"2611.21339v1","baseId":"2611.21339","title":"MovieRoll: A Systematic Framework for Rolling Out Long-Form Videos","authors":["Yunyang Ge","Weijie Kong","Ryan Xu","Yue Wu","Qi Tian","Jiangfeng Xiong"],"authorCount":12,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Streaming video generation must sustain quality beyond the training horizon while handling self-generated history and growing cache costs. To address these challenges, we present MovieRoll, a base-to-distilled framework that connects streaming fine-tuning, long-rollout distillation, and long-horizon inference. We first establish a robust multi-step base through Window-wise Teacher Forcing (WinTF), which jointly denoises chunks at progressive noise levels with bidirectional attention within each window and causal access to preceding history. To retain historical context efficiently, hierarchical KV-cache compression (HieraKV) exploits layer-wise attention patterns, retaining full history in shallow layers and sink--recent history in deeper layers. This reduces cache memory by up to 78% while improving generation quality. Building on this base, cumulative long tuning with distribution matching distillation (CuLT-DMD) holds parameters fixed throughout each long self-rollout and averages local gradients before updating. Each update therefore combines supervision across temporal positions while retaining only one local computation graph, enabling efficient long-rollout training of a few-step generator. To extend generation beyond the training horizon, KV recomputation bounds context for both models, while guidance annealing (AnnealCFG) reduces guidance toward the clean endpoint to stabilize MovieRoll-Base. Complementing these modeling advances, FBCA attention and PS-VAE decoding support efficient training and streaming output. On HeliosBench, MovieRoll-Base remains competitive at 30s after training on videos of up to 15s, and MovieRoll-Distilled achieves the highest total scores among the compared methods across single- and multi-event evaluations up to 60s, combining sustained naturalness with active motion.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21339v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21339v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21373","arxiv":"2611.21373v1","baseId":"2611.21373","title":"WeVisForensics: Self-Evolving Agent for Omni AI-Generated Video Detection","authors":["Yipeng Du","Yuxi Li","Ying Zhang","Chong Sun","Chen Li","Jing Lyu"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["强化学习"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["强化学习","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【强化学习】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Recent advances in video generation call for reliable detection grounded in explicit forensic evidence. However, current methods primarily depend on single-pass chain-of-thought reasoning that overlooks brief or localized artifacts, or on predefined multi-agent workflows that offer limited flexibility in selecting relevant inspections. Even with adaptive investigation, optimizing the investigation policy alone does not directly improve the forensic capabilities of fixed experts. We introduce WeVisForensics, an agentic framework in which a main investigator agent coordinates a self-evolving library of forensic inspector sub-agents spanning visual and audio cues. The investigator learns which artifact type to examine, over which window, and when to conclude, and these interactions provide shared feedback for both levels of agents. Our dual-timescale policy optimization strategy, DTPO, closes this loop through fast reinforcement learning of the investigator and slower refinement of the inspectors, guided by annotated artifact intervals, to report subtle artifacts they once dismissed. Furthermore, we curate ForenCue-30K, combining role-specific forensic annotations and interaction trajectories to supervise individual inspections and their coordination, with an accompanying reinforcement-learning pool. Experiments show that WeVisForensics averages 95.2% detection accuracy across in-domain, out-of-domain and agentic benchmarks, 9.0% above the strongest prior detector, while grounding its verdicts in localized evidence.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21373v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21373v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21559","arxiv":"2611.21559v1","baseId":"2611.21559","title":"Reference Forcing: Long Video Generation with Region-Guided Drift Correction","authors":["Sheng Zhao","Haitian Zheng","Weikai Lin","Junjie Zhao","Yuhao Zhu"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":47,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["少步蒸馏","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Long video generation should keep the subject and background consistent while supporting rich, natural motion. However, autoregressive models are often distilled with distribution matching distillation (DMD) over short windows, which does not train them to keep this consistency over long ranges. As a result, the subject and background can drift over time. Methods that manage the key-value (KV) cache retain or retrieve history to reduce drift. Yet this history may itself have drifted. We propose Reference Forcing, which uses the initial frames as an appearance reference. We first build a teacher that uses attention masks to force each region of a new frame to attend to the same region in the initial frames. The teacher finds these regions with off-the-shelf segmentation models. We then distill this behavior into a separate attention path of the student, which needs no external models at inference. We further introduce value correction, which corrects drifted values in the KV cache toward the matching values of drift-free initial frames. Experiments show that Reference Forcing achieves state-of-the-art subject and background consistency among long-video methods on different base models, and its advantage grows with video length. Ablations show both components are needed to gain consistency without losing motion. More visualizations and code are available at https://zhaosheng-thu.github.io/reference-force/","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21559v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21559v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21867","arxiv":"2611.21867v1","baseId":"2611.21867","title":"FaceRiff: Data-Efficient Unified Text-to-3D Face Asset Synthesis via Geometric Conditioning","authors":["Elly Akhoundi","Mathieu Lamarre","Thibault Goyallon","Patrick Anderson","Étienne Danvoye"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"High-resolution facial datasets with consistent UV-mapped geometry, albedo, and physically based displacement are scarce, yet such assets are essential for photorealistic digital-human rendering. Existing 3D face generation methods are often slow, single-modality, or require large specialized datasets. Meanwhile, large image diffusion models encode strong facial priors but do not natively produce lighting-independent textures, pore-scale displacement, or UV-aligned geometry. We present FaceRiff, a unified, data- and parameter-efficient framework for generating complete 3D face assets (mesh, albedo, and displacement) directly from text in a shared UV space. FaceRiff adapts a pretrained SDXL backbone with task-specific LoRA adapters and uses UV normal maps as geometric conditioning: per-instance normals guide albedo and displacement synthesis, while a dataset-mean normal provides a canonical spatial anchor for mesh generation. Using only 2,049 scans from approximately 350 identities and training on a single GPU, FaceRiff updates less than 3% of the backbone parameters per task and produces render-ready 3D face assets without per-instance optimization. Compared with FaceG2E and DreamFace, FaceRiff produces greater per-prompt diversity while preserving prompt alignment and fixed-topology UV consistency, and additionally generates lighting-independent albedo suitable for PBR relighting and pore-scale displacement absent from prior text-driven baselines. A blind study with 45 industry professionals shows that FaceRiff achieves substantially higher perceived realism than DreamFace, approaching that of real captures. Overall, FaceRiff demonstrates that pretrained diffusion priors can be adapted to unified 3D face asset synthesis under limited data.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21867v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21867v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI","cs.GR"]},{"id":"daily_2611.22005","arxiv":"2611.22005v1","baseId":"2611.22005","title":"Where Should the Lamp Go? Ambient-Aware Lamp Placement for Portraits","authors":["Yuyan Chen"],"authorCount":1,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Portraits shape first impressions, and lighting is an important factor. Prior work, such as lighting estimation and portrait relighting, edits lighting after capture but does not guide where to place a light before the shot. This leaves an open question: where should a light go to best light a face? Therefore, we propose LightMatch, a preference-grounded inverse lighting framework that disentangles the controllable light from ambient illumination and optimizes its placement. We first learn a Bradley-Terry quality model from pairwise human judgments. A perception head then regresses spherical-harmonic lighting from frozen self-supervised features via cross-attention. Finally, a placement solver isolates the light via spherical-harmonic linearity and optimizes its reachable position. On held-out environments, LightMatch attains 87.8% of the oracle improvement, outperforming the conventional solver that ignores ambient light by a relative 28.4% and the same pipeline with the widely used DPR estimator by a relative 70.5%. It reaches 73.2% on real OLAT faces of unseen identities and, on our real captures, cuts lamp-direction error by 37.7%.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.22005v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.22005v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.GR"]},{"id":"daily_2611.21114","arxiv":"2611.21114v1","baseId":"2611.21114","title":"VAE Rhythm: Temporal Decoding Traces for AI-Generated Video Detection","authors":["Ke Sun","Shuwei Li","Haiyang Mei","Mike Zheng Shou"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Visible artifacts are an unreliable cue for detecting generated videos, because newer generators learn to remove them. We instead detect a trace left by the generation pipeline itself. Most video generators decode latents with a 3D causal VAE that groups consecutive frames in time, so the fidelity of generated frames rises and falls periodically with their position in a group. We call this periodic trace VAE Rhythm. Real videos are captured frame by frame and do not have it. To reveal it without training, we propose a phase scan. The scan reconstructs a clip with an off-the-shelf VAE from successive start offsets and measures the periodicity of the reconstruction error across these offsets. We find VAE Rhythm in most of the 39 variants of 31 generators we test, including the closed-source Sora, Kling 3.0 and Seedance 2.0. We use the rhythm as a zero-shot score, as a plug-in for existing detectors and as a data engine that pairs real videos with their VAE reconstructions to train our detector CaDet. On DF26, the training-free rhythm score outperforms all 15 retrained baselines, and as a plug-in it improves all 19 tested detectors by 6.7% to 23.7% AUROC. CaDet achieves the best AUROC on AIGVDBench, DF26 and three 2026 closed-source models, and its variant trained without any generated video still exceeds 90% on AIGVDBench.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21114v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.21114v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.28085","arxiv":"2611.28085v1","baseId":"2611.28085","title":"UbiEnlight: User-Perception-Aware Real-Time Low-Light Video Enhancement on Mobile Devices and Smartglasses","authors":["Minfan Wang","Sicong Liu","Teng Li","Zimu Zhou","Sixun He","Bin Guo"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-16","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":41,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":[],"tags":["训练范式","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Low-light video capture is now common on smartphones and wearables, yet dynamic illumination coupled with mobile/werable user motion causes noise, blur, and flicker that make videos hard to perceive and use, especially for users with night blindness and near-eye displays. Prior work enhances frames with post-hoc temporal constraints or optimizes videos directly; however,low-light and motion degradations are spatiotemporally coupled, so post-hoc consistency after per-frame restoration often leaves flicker and motion artifacts. We present UbiEnlight, the first diffusion-transformer-based on-device system for real-time, temporally stable low-light video enhancement. UbiEnlight builds on two insights: (1) illumination and structure separate more robustly in the frequency domain, enabling a spectrum-guided diffusion transformer that injects Fourier amplitude/phase priors to correct illumination while anchoring structure without optical flow; and (2) user perceptual tolerance varies by context, motivating a perception-aware controller that adapts sampling depth, attention reuse, and frame caching at runtime. We implement UbiEnlight on smartphones and smartglasses, and evaluate it against state-of-the-art baselines. UbiEnlight improves temporal stability by up to 54.76% under dynamic/extreme conditions while sustaining real-time on-device performance.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.28085v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.28085v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-13","count":2,"star5Count":0,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.17957","arxiv":"2611.17957v1","baseId":"2611.17957","title":"MacroGS: Macro-Pixel Shared Gaussian Primitives for Arbitrary-Scale Light Field Super-Resolutio","authors":["Zeqiang Wei","Angulia Yang","Min Xu"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-13","updated":"2026-11-13","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Arbitrary-scale light field spatial super-resolution reconstructs high-resolution views at any requested scale from low-resolution multi-view observations. Gaussian primitives enable continuous reconstruction, but generating separate primitive sets for each view redundantly encodes shared scene structure across views and causes the primitive count grow linearly with the number of views. To avoid such redundancy, we propose MacroGS, a feed-forward decoder that jointly predicts a single shared Gaussian set per macro pixel rather than generating separate sets for each view. Disparity-dependent replacement and angle-conditioned appearance allow each set to be reused across all output views, keeping the predicted primitive count independent of the number of views at each requested scale. The decoder iteratively predicts per-primitive disparities, retrieves the corresponding multi-view features, and updates the primitive states. Gaussian splatting and residual refinement then reconstruct the output light field. Despite being learned without geometric supervision, the predicted disparities strongly correlate with underlying scene geometry. Experiments across five benchmarks demonstrate that MacroGS integrates effectively with diverse light field encoders, improving reconstruction quality at fixed scales while achieving leading performance across most evaluated arbitrary-scale settings. The decoder also achieves speedups of approximately $2$-$10\\times$ over state-of-the-art implicit light field decoders and $10$-$18\\times$ over the view-wise Gaussian baseline.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17957v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.17957v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.18006","arxiv":"2611.18006v1","baseId":"2611.18006","title":"RecurIR: Learning When and How to Revise Restoration Policies via Recursive Self-Improvement","authors":["Hu Gao","Haoran Zhang","Yulong Chen","Lizhuang Ma"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-13","updated":"2026-11-13","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Agent-based image restoration has achieved promising performance by coordinating restoration tools to handle heterogeneous and coupled degradations. However, existing agents often improve their decisions through accumulated successful and failed executions, without explicitly identifying the conditions under which learned restoration strategies remain effective. Consequently, changes in degradation severity, spatial coverage, or interactions may invalidate previous decisions, while providing limited guidance on how these decisions should be revised. In this paper, we propose \\textbf{RecurIR}, a recursive self-improvement framework that enables restoration agents to learn when and how to revise their decision rules. Specifically, we introduce a policy-hypothesis discovery mechanism to identify testable, condition-dependent rules through matched tool executions. We then design a rule-falsification-driven task evolution mechanism to generate targeted degradation variations, examine the validity of learned rules, and identify their applicability boundaries. Based on the resulting evidence, we develop a change-conditioned policy revision mechanism that learns how variations in degradation conditions should modify tool selection, execution order, operation strength, and processing regions. We further incorporate independent verification to retain effective revisions and use the resulting feedback to guide subsequent hypothesis discovery and task evolution. Through this recursive process, RecurIR progressively refines its restoration policies as degradation conditions change. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed framework.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18006v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18006v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-12","count":4,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.16269","arxiv":"2611.16269v2","baseId":"2611.16269","title":"FlowIR: Depth-driven Rectified Flow for Resolving Thermal Ambiguity in Infrared Super-Resolution","authors":["Xingyue Zhu","Qinyun Mei","Zhexi Min","Yang Zou","Xingyuan Li","Zhiying Jiang"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-12","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Distinguishing different heat sources in low-resolution infrared (IR) images is inherently difficult, as multiple thermal emitters often collapse into ambiguous and overlapping responses. Existing super-resolution methods hallucinate structures from such ambiguous 2D evidence, leading to thermal drift, boundary merging, and unreliable restoration under infrared degradations. We propose FlowIR, a depth-driven rectified flow framework that resolves thermal ambiguity by injecting 3D geometric cues into the generation process. Pseudo-3D depth lifting and depth-guided 3D convolutions disentangle overlapping foreground objects, while cluster-wise inter-attention suppresses cross-source feature contamination. A Thermal-Geometry Consistency Loss further enforces geometric alignment during refinement. Built upon a lightweight rectified flow backbone, FlowIR restores IR images in only a few steps, producing sharply separated, degradation-robust, and geometrically faithful high-resolution results.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16269v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16269v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.16620","arxiv":"2611.16620v1","baseId":"2611.16620","title":"Improved CNN-Transformer Network for Neutron Image Super-Resolution","authors":["Yuheng Chu","Hongli Li","Tian Zhang"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-12","updated":"2026-11-12","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"The spatial resolution of neutron images is of great importance for material defect diagnosis, structural characterization, and nondestructive testing. However, owing to limitations in neutron beam quality, scintillator conversion efficiency and light scattering, detector pixel size, and noise levels, conventional neutron images often suffer from speckle noise, blurred edges, and missing details, making them inadequate for high-precision analysis. To address these issues, an improved super-resolution model combining convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and Transformers is proposed. First, to overcome the difficulty of obtaining high-quality and clear training images for neutron imaging, a pretrained image restoration model is fine-tuned using real neutron images and then employed to enhance existing neutron images, thereby providing high-quality reference images for subsequent super-resolution model training. Second, an improved CRAFT-based super-resolution network is developed by incorporating a hybrid prior-guided channel fusion module (HPCF), a mean-standard deviation dual-statistics squeeze-and-excitation block (MS-SEBlock), and a partial convolution feed-forward network (PCFN), with the aim of improving the reconstruction of important information, such as textures and fine details, in neutron images. Simulation results demonstrate that, compared with bicubic interpolation, SRCNN, SRGAN-VGG, and other methods, the proposed network achieves superior performance in both full-reference metrics, including PSNR and SSIM, and no-reference metrics, including AGM and SMD. The visual quality and structural fidelity of the reconstructed images are also significantly improved. This study provides a new approach to improving neutron image quality.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16620v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16620v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.16650","arxiv":"2611.16650v2","baseId":"2611.16650","title":"Learning the Missing Derivative: Neural Multiple-Scattering Operators for Inverse Volume Rendering","authors":["Sipeng Chen","Shibo Li"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-12","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Reconstructing optically thick media from photographs requires modeling multiple scattering and its response to changes in extinction. Frozen-source tomography discards this derivative; differentiable path tracing estimates it through expensive, noisy sampling. We instead predict the multiple-scattering field with a neural operator and differentiate through it during inverse rendering, while computing transmittance and single scattering directly. A further challenge arises during optimization: an operator that predicts the field accurately on held-out media can still produce poor derivatives at the density states visited by the optimizer. We address this by fine-tuning on states generated during inversions driven by the operator, using Monte Carlo scattering-field supervision. On held-out optically thick clouds, our method improves relighting over frozen-source tomography at equal time and outperforms differentiable path tracing given substantially more time. Training on inversion trajectories also improves derivative alignment at visited states and reduces optimization drift.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16650v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16650v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.16724","arxiv":"2611.16724v1","baseId":"2611.16724","title":"What Limits Thermal Infrared Drone Detection? A Cross-Dataset Benchmark Analysis","authors":["Tamara R. Lenhard","Jan Kels","Tobias Koch"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-11-12","updated":"2026-11-12","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["色彩迁移"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移】","abstract":"Thermal infrared (IR) imaging enables drone detection with reduced dependence on ambient illumination, supporting continuous surveillance beyond the limitations of visible-spectrum sensing. However, substantial and often undocumented variability in camera systems, processing pipelines, and annotation practices across public IR drone datasets complicates the assessment of detector performance and cross-domain generalization. To examine these sources of variability and their implications for drone detection, we systematically characterize existing IR datasets and establish an artifact taxonomy reflecting their origins across the acquisition and processing pipeline, encompassing optical and sensor effects, tone mapping and image enhancement, and recording-related artifacts. Focusing on long-wave IR data, we investigate the impact of training data composition and model characteristics on YOLO-based detection performance, generalization, and condition-dependent failure modes. Our results demonstrate that detection performance is governed more strongly by training data composition than by YOLO generation or model capacity, while cross-dataset generalization exhibits pronounced asymmetries. Even the best-performing configuration fails to detect over 40\\% of drones, with elevated miss rates for very small targets, cluttered urban backgrounds, and thermal crossover, highlighting the shortcomings of existing datasets.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16724v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.16724v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-11","count":4,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.15457","arxiv":"2611.15457v1","baseId":"2611.15457","title":"WaveFlow-UIE: A Single-Pipeline Wavelet-Domain Flow Model for Efficient Underwater Image Restoration","authors":["Abdulaziz Alfaraj","Mohammed Al Naser","Hassan Al Nasser","Muzammil Behzad"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-11","updated":"2026-11-11","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Underwater image restoration remains challenging because color distortion, scattering, and detail loss affect low- and high-frequency image components differently. Recent diffusion-based methods have shown strong restoration quality, but often rely on multi-stage pipelines and costly iterative refinement. We present WaveFlow-UIE, a single-pipeline wavelet-domain flow model that directly learns to transform degraded underwater wavelet representations into clean ones. Our method applies a discrete wavelet transform, estimates lightweight physics-inspired degradation cues including transmission and ambient light, and conditions a velocity network trained with flow matching in wavelet space. The training objective combines flow-matching, frequency-weighted reconstruction, perceptual, color-consistency, and physics-consistency losses. Across UIEB, LSUI, UFO-120, and EUVP, WaveFlow-UIE achieves a strong quality-efficiency tradeoff, with especially competitive results on perceptual metrics. Relative to a strong wavelet-based diffusion baseline, the proposed model substantially reduces inference cost while maintaining or improving restoration quality on several paired benchmarks. We show that reducing the inference process from five flow steps to a single step preserves nearly all of the restoration quality while providing an additional speedup of about $4\\times$ on UIEB at $256\\times256$ resolution. This single-step result suggests that a single-pass wavelet-domain model is an effective alternative to heavier diffusion-style refinement.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15457v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.15457v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.15146","arxiv":"2611.15146v2","baseId":"2611.15146","title":"Reframing Video Inverse Tone Mapping as Blind Posterior Inference via Latent Luminance Supervision","authors":["Hanwool Sul","Chang-Hoon Jeong","Kisung Shin","Bomseok Park","Donghoon Cha","Hansl von Kwon"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-11","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"视频","stars":4,"score":59,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移"],"tags":["色彩迁移","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"High-dynamic-range (HDR) displays are now common, but most video content, such as archival footage, broadcast material, and the output of generative video models, exists only in standard dynamic range (SDR). The need is sharpest for generated video, which has no HDR master at all and is graded alongside live-action and visual-effects material finished in HDR. Converting SDR to HDR means undoing a tone-mapping operation that was never recorded, and clipping destroys the radiance wherever it acts, so HDR recovery is a blind inverse problem. Methods built on generative video priors fill clipped regions with plausible structure, but their reconstructions are systematically biased: in every public method we test, saturated content leans too bright or too dark, and the direction of the lean moves with the tone operator behind the input. We call this failure the luminance error, measure both its magnitude and its sign, and make it the focus of this work. Reframing ITM as posterior inference under an unknown operator locates the failure in two places and yields two corrections. A fixed affine functional of the video-diffusion latent reads log-luminance without decoding; on that coordinate an asymmetric loss selects an expectile of the posterior, applied only where clipping leaves luminance unconstrained. We also synthesize training SDR through an explicit family of tone operators, so the model marginalizes over what the observation cannot show. Trained this way, our luminance error stays flatter across operators and smaller on average than any public method, and the full system achieves the best perceptual quality on all three benchmarks.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15146v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.15146v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.15449","arxiv":"2611.15449v1","baseId":"2611.15449","title":"Reading the Unreadable: Text-Aware Image Super-Resolution Needs Reasoning","authors":["Jaeseong Lee","Jinwoo Kim","Jinho Jeong","Seon Joo Kim"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-11","updated":"2026-11-11","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Text-aware image super-resolution (TAISR) aims to recover high-resolution images from low-resolution inputs while preserving textual content. Existing methods treat this as a visual restoration problem, yet severely degraded text often cannot be read from local visual evidence alone. Humans instead read it by reasoning over image context, logical patterns, and prior knowledge. This raises a fundamental question: Does TAISR need reasoning? To answer this, we introduce two diagnostic tools. The first, ReasonText, is a human-curated benchmark that separates reasoning-required text from locally recoverable text. The second, Ground-Truth Text-Crop Accuracy (GTTCA), is a recognition-based metric that measures text restoration quality without text detection errors. Evaluating twelve recent super-resolution (SR) models, we find a consistent and substantial performance drop on reasoning-required text, revealing a systematic blind spot. Two observations point to a remedy. One is that text-conditioned SR models restore text substantially better when given ground-truth text. The other is that multimodal large language models (MLLMs) can read degraded text in low-resolution images through reasoning. We therefore propose Reasoning Transfer via Captioning (RTC), a training-free plug-in that uses an external MLLM to infer the degraded text and feeds the resulting caption to a text-conditioned SR model. RTC consistently improves all six text-conditioned SR models we evaluate, especially on reasoning-required text. Surprisingly, a real-world SR model with RTC can even outperform the TAISR model with RTC, suggesting that reasoning should be a core design axis for future TAISR.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15449v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.15449v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.15514","arxiv":"2611.15514v1","baseId":"2611.15514","title":"Real Pairs Are Not Enough: Physically Anchored Supervision for Volumetric CT Super-Resolution","authors":["Haoran Liu","Zipei Yan","Mingzhe Liu","Jizhou Li","Guibin Zan"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-11","updated":"2026-11-11","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Volumetric CT super-resolution (SR) models are commonly trained on synthetic or physically acquired pairs of low-resolution (LR) and high-resolution (HR) volumes. However, synthetic pairs miss real acquisition effects, whereas registered physical pairs retain inter-scan noise, artifact, and microstructure differences---causing direct voxelwise regression, and even naive synthetic-real mixing, to oversmooth fine porous structures. We introduce the Physically-anchored Implicit Network (PIN) with Real-Pair Dual-Anchor learning, which decouples physical-pair supervision into two complementary roles. The HR anchor teaches fine structure from registered HR through pair-conditioned spectral replay, while the LR anchor constrains the prediction from acquired LR via affine-calibrated low-pass projection. Together with exact synthetic supervision in one shared volumetric implicit model, PIN learns from both physical scans without forcing the acquired-LR reconstruction to match registered HR voxel by voxel. Inference requires only acquired LR. Across 30 external baseline configurations on real CT, PIN achieves the lowest full-volume LPIPS, reduces average symmetric surface distance by 42.3-49.5% and bone-volume-fraction error by 52.2-53.4% relative to the three strongest baselines for each metric, and lowers the erased reference-pore volume fraction from 37.3-72.9% to 10.4%. Code, pretrained models, and evaluation scripts will be publicly available.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15514v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.15514v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","eess.IV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-10","count":3,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.13995","arxiv":"2611.13995v1","baseId":"2611.13995","title":"Lipschitz-Controlled Flow Matching for Robust Image Restoration","authors":["Sedjro Salomon Hotegni","Konstantin Sonntag","Paul-Christian Bürkner","Sebastian Peitz"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-10","updated":"2026-11-10","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Flow-matching models can produce high-quality image restorations, but their learned dynamics may amplify small input perturbations and make reconstructions unstable. We introduce LipsFlower to reduce this amplification. We derive stability bounds for Flow-from-$Y$, which initializes the ordinary differential equation from a noise-perturbed observation, as well as for observation-conditioned posterior sampling, which conditions the velocity field on the observation throughout integration. Based on this analysis, we develop LipHDiT, a Lipschitz-aware hourglass diffusion transformer that serves as the vector-field architecture of LipsFlower. LipHDiT incorporates stabilized approximate spectral normalization, bounded modulation, and convex residual mixing. We also use adversarial finite-radius gain regularization at sampled interpolation states to penalize excessive sensitivity to the state and, for conditional sampling, the observation. We test both formulations on blind face restoration, measuring adversarial robustness and the perceptual quality of the reconstructions. LipsFlower reduces perturbation amplification while keeping restoration quality competitive with the evaluated baselines. These results suggest that Lipschitz-aware flow matching can improve the stability of generative image restoration.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13995v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13995v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.14608","arxiv":"2611.14608v1","baseId":"2611.14608","title":"FreeDeshadow: Shadow Removal for User-Specified Objects With Multimodal Prompts","authors":["Zhiliang Wu","Jihao Gu","Kun Li","Hanyu Xuan","Hehe Fan","Meng Wang"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-10","updated":"2026-11-10","modality":"图像","stars":4,"score":56,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Existing shadow removal methods either perform global shadow removal or rely on precise shadow masks to guide restoration. Such paradigms limit their ability to selectively remove the shadow cast by a user-specified object in practical applications. To this end, we introduce an object-aware controllable shadow removal task, which allows users to specify a target object through a simple prompt and removes only its cast shadow while preserving other content. However, object-shadow asymmetry makes it challenging to establish accurate correspondence between the specified object and its spatially separated cast shadow and to confine restoration to the target shadow region. To address these challenges, we propose FreeDeshadow, a unified controllable shadow removal framework that supports both visual and textual prompts. It consists of an object-aware generator and a shadow-aware local refiner. The former interprets the user prompt and produces a high-quality shadow-free proposal, while the latter establishes the correspondence between the object and its cast shadow and constrains the generated changes to the target shadow region. To support training and evaluation, we further construct PCSRD+, a large-scale dataset containing 154,571 quadruples at 1024$\\times$1024 resolution and covering six types of user prompts. The visual prompts include a dot, a line, and an object mask, while the textual prompts describe the target object using attributes, spatial relations, or states. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FreeDeshadow accurately removes the shadow of the target object under both visual and textual prompts while effectively preserving non-target regions. It also outperforms existing methods in shadow removal performance and controllability. Project Page: https://wzlwlm13.github.io/FreeDeshadow","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14608v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.14608v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.13677","arxiv":"2611.13677v1","baseId":"2611.13677","title":"Deformable Texture Maps: Compiling Dynamic Microgeometry into Neural Textures","authors":["Antonio Arian Silaghi","Zhen Chen","Danny Kaufman","Élie Michel","Jean-Marc Thiery","Jorge Lopez-Moreno"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-10","updated":"2026-11-10","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Deformable material surfaces, such as cloth, owe their appearance to fine-scale geometries that restructure under deformation. Even when coarse simulation models efficiently recover this microstructure, explicitly instantiating and rendering it remains expensive. We introduce Deformable Texture Maps (DTM), a representation that compiles deformation-driven microgeometry into a continuous and compact neural texture. Our model maps texture coordinates and local deformations to intrinsic maps describing opacity, surface normals, depth, and material identity. This captures the occlusion and visibility changes generated by deformation. At runtime, these maps provide a common texture-space representation of deformation-driven fine-scale structure that can be evaluated directly on coarse deforming surfaces. Since our representation encodes geometric attributes rather than radiance, lighting and material response remain decoupled from the learned model. This allows us to apply relighting, as well as shading and material editing without retraining. We further introduce a persistent texture-space rendering strategy that caches these intrinsic maps and selectively updates them as deformation changes. This effectively decouples neural evaluation from frame rendering and enables their reuse across frames and conventional rendering effects. We demonstrate our approach on yarn-based textiles, where non-linear material stretch and shear induce changes in yarn geometry, spacing and occlusion. We build our model by, first, sweeping tileable yarn patches through deformation space, then, rendering the detailed geometry into a canonical texture-space, and finally, compressing the resulting maps into a single neural representation. Our resulting model can be evaluated directly on arbitrary coarse garment meshes and integrated with conventional shading pipelines, keeping appearance authorable at render time.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13677v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13677v1","arxivCats":["cs.GR"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-09","count":3,"star5Count":0,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.12025","arxiv":"2611.12025v1","baseId":"2611.12025","title":"Single Image Super-Resolution via Semantic Prompt and Cross-Modal Training on Content Area","authors":["Xiao Tan"],"authorCount":1,"date":"2026-11-09","updated":"2026-11-09","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":55,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Enhancing image quality through super-resolution is crucial in image and video processing. While contemporary deep learning models, such as CNNs and Transformers, excel in pixel-level enhancements, they often overlook the rich semantic context within images. To address this, we introduce a novel approach that synergizes pixel intensity with semantic information for superior detail recovery. Our method involves extracting semantic data, including textual descriptors and spatial positioning, and encoding this information. We also present a Cross-Modal Feature Fusion module for generating semantic feature maps via multimodal training, and a Prompt Traction Head to augment the backbone network with semantic insights, thereby refining image details. Our empirical results across various datasets validate the superiority of our approach.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12025v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12025v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.11831","arxiv":"2611.11831v1","baseId":"2611.11831","title":"End-to-End Joint Training of Super-Resolution and Compression Artifact Removal for Industrial Machine Vision","authors":["Dang BuiDinh","Sang NguyenQuang","Anh NguyenPhuong","Wen-Hsiao Peng","Xiem HoangVan"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-09","updated":"2026-11-09","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"In industrial machine vision, images captured by edge devices are often downsampled and compressed to reduce bandwidth, introducing resolution loss and compression artifacts that severely degrade the performance of downstream tasks, such as defect detection. Existing quality enhancement methods typically apply super-resolution (SR) or compression artifact removal (CAR) independently or in a sequential manner and are largely optimized for human visual fidelity rather than machine perception. This paper proposes an end-to-end quality enhancement framework that integrates SR and CAR with a unified training strategy that optimizes both human-perception and machine-perception objectives. We investigate fixed-weight and uncertainty-based dynamic-weight loss functions to balance reconstruction quality and machine vision task performance. Extensive experiments on PCB Defect, DeepPCB, and NEU-DET datasets show that the proposed jointly trained model generally outperforms standalone and separately trained SR-CAR pipelines. In particular, under severe JPEG compression, our method improves defect detection performance from 0.461 to 0.887 mAP@50 on PCB Defect over sequential SR-CAR baselines, with only a slight reduction in PSNR. These results demonstrate that joint optimization is critical for compression-aware industrial machine vision systems.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11831v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11831v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.13010","arxiv":"2611.13010v1","baseId":"2611.13010","title":"Preserving Cultural Records: A Large-Scale Benchmark and Visual-Prior Routing Network for Ancient Book Image Restoration","authors":["Shumin Fan","Tianyu Song","Hongming Chen","Xiaodong Zhou","Xiang Chen"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-09","updated":"2026-11-09","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Ancient books preserve invaluable cultural heritage, yet their images often suffer from complex degradations caused by long-term ageing, environmental exposure, and physical damage. Existing ancient book image restoration benchmarks are limited in scale and rely on simplified degradation simulations, which cannot fully represent real-world degradation patterns and restrict the generalization ability of restoration models. To address these challenges, we introduce AncBench, a large-scale synthetic paired benchmark containing 100K aligned clean-degraded image pairs. AncBench models realistic composite degradations through a comprehensive degradation pipeline that considers material ageing, paper texture variation, blur, staining, water-induced geometric distortion, and local occlusion. Furthermore, we propose AncRestore, a degradation-aware visual-prior routing network for ancient book image restoration. AncRestore first learns degradation representations from degraded images and then dynamically selects and integrates suitable visual priors according to different degradation characteristics. Guided by these degradation cues, a dynamic routing mechanism combines expert-wise and region-wise routing strategies adaptively adjusts the contribution of these priors across different regions, enabling the model to recover both global appearance and local text details. Extensive experiments show that AncRestore effectively suppresses composite degradations while preserving text structures and enhancing restoration quality against other methods, highlighting its application potential for intelligent ancient book image restoration in cultural heritage preservation.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13010v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.13010v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-08","count":2,"star5Count":0,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.11210","arxiv":"2611.11210v1","baseId":"2611.11210","title":"DynOLAT: OLAT Video Prediction for Controllable Dynamic Full-Body Relighting","authors":["Taoming Liu","Yinji ShenTu","Guangzhao He","Yudong Jin","Yukang Ye","Siyu Zhang"],"authorCount":10,"date":"2026-11-08","updated":"2026-11-08","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":60,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Accurate lighting response is essential for realistic dynamic human relighting, because shadows, highlights, and color changes should follow the target illumination in both direction and relative strength. Current learning-based relighting methods can generate visually plausible videos, but they often exhibit imprecise lighting responses: shadows and highlights may drift away from the target direction, and the output brightness may not faithfully follow the relative intensities in the target environment, making the result closer to appearance harmonization than controllable relighting. To address this issue, we propose DynOLAT, a generalizable OLAT video prediction framework for dynamic full-body humans. Instead of directly synthesizing a single relit appearance, our method predicts temporally consistent One-Light-At-a-Time (OLAT) basis videos from flat-lit inputs, and then linearly combines the predicted bases according to the target environment map. This OLAT-based formulation makes the directional response explicit in the predicted bases, while the relative lighting strength is determined by the linear weights derived from the environment map. We further introduce a dynamic capture pipeline and the first large-scale dynamic full-body OLAT dataset, comprising over 24 million frames from 224 identity-outfit combinations, where different outfits of the same person define separate combinations. Experiments show that DynOLAT responds more faithfully to variations in target lighting direction and relative intensity than the tested baselines, enabling controllable video relighting while preserving fine appearance details and temporal coherence. The dataset and processing tools will be publicly released to foster future research.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11210v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11210v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.11139","arxiv":"2611.11139v2","baseId":"2611.11139","title":"Graphene: A Lightweight Transformer with Ising Mean-Field Refinement for Image Super-Resolution","authors":["Seungki Hong","Taekwang Jang"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-08","updated":"2026-11-18","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"This paper proposes Graphene, a lightweight image super-resolution architecture with two complementary feature refinement mechanisms: Ising-model-inspired local feature refinement using an Ising Refinement Block (IRB) and stage-history-based feature refinement using a History Attention (HistAttn) module. The IRB reuses attention output features and pre-softmax attention logits to construct signed pairwise token couplings within each local window, applies unrolled mean-field updates, and refines features through a gated residual correction. These signed couplings allow alignment and opposition between latent spins to modulate local feature refinement. HistAttn attends over the current stage output and earlier representations at the same token position and adaptively blends the current stage output with a history-weighted feature aggregate without adding spatial pairwise attention. On Urban100 at $\\times4$, Graphene and Graphene-light improve over IET and IET-light by 0.24 dB and 0.38 dB, respectively, with 34.3%/13.7% fewer parameters, 44.8%/44.4% fewer FLOPs, 83.9%/95.9% lower latency, and 63.7%/92.0% lower peak memory. Real-world SR comparisons further show that Graphene recovers fine details and preserves textures across diverse degradations. Code and reference checkpoints are available at https://github.com/skethz/Graphene","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11139v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.11139v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-07","count":2,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.10485","arxiv":"2611.10485v2","baseId":"2611.10485","title":"Decoupling Direction and Magnitude: Language-Steered Flow Matching for Super-Resolution in the Dark","authors":["Jiaxin Gao","Ziyu Yue","Yaohua Liu","Danchen Cui","Zhihui Zhao","Zhixun Su"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-07","updated":"2026-11-13","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Image super-resolution in the dark is fundamentally challenged by extreme spatial heterogeneity: severely underexposed and noisy regions demand aggressive generative enhancement to reconstruct missing details, while relatively well-exposed areas require conservative updates to prevent hallucinated textures. Standard diffusion and flow-matching models, however, rely on globally uniform integration trajectories, making them sub-optimal for such spatially varying degradations. In this paper, we propose DeLang-SR (Degradation-Language steered Super-Resolution), a one-step flow-matching framework that explicitly decouples the restoration direction and magnitude. Rather than treating low-light corruption as arbitrary latent features, DeLang-SR translates measurable physical statistics into structured degradation language, harnessing the semantic prior of pretrained text-to-image models. The degradation-language intent, complemented by image-specific visual tokens, steers the velocity field (direction) toward restoration-relevant manifolds. Simultaneously, a continuous spatial update-strength field acts as a locally varying Euler step-size field (magnitude), applying stronger integration steps in heavily degraded shadows while enforcing conservative updates in reliable regions. Experiments on paired and real dark images, together with ablations and diagnostic analyses, show that DeLang-SR provides an effective and interpretable one-step route to super-resolution in the dark. Code and pretrained models are available at https://github.com/moriyaya/DeLang-SR.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10485v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10485v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.10653","arxiv":"2611.10653v2","baseId":"2611.10653","title":"FG-DSA: Frequency-Guided Depthwise Separable Attention for Efficient Dense Prediction","authors":["Yiyao Yang"],"authorCount":1,"date":"2026-11-07","updated":"2026-11-15","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Dense prediction and generative modeling remain challenging in resource-constrained environments, particularly because repeated downsampling can lead to the loss of spatial details and less accurate object boundaries. Although attention mechanisms have been widely adopted to address semantic ambiguity, preserving high-frequency information while maintaining computational efficiency remains an important challenge. To address this issue, we propose Frequency-Guided Depthwise Separable Attention for Efficient Dense Prediction (FG-DSA), a framework that integrates frequency-domain information into lightweight vision architectures for dense visual understanding and generation. Specifically, FG-DSA uses a 2D Fast Fourier Transform (FFT)-based high-pass filtering module to guide spatial attention, aiming to retain fine-grained features and improve object-boundary representation. Experimental results show that FG-DSA can improve boundary-related performance in semantic segmentation under the evaluated setting. Beyond discriminative tasks, we also examine its application in a generative U-Net for single-step image restoration. The results suggest that incorporating FG-DSA can enhance reconstruction quality and help preserve high-frequency details.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10653v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.10653v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-06","count":2,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.09387","arxiv":"2611.09387v2","baseId":"2611.09387","title":"A Bridge from Paste to Place: Physics-Guided One-Step Image Composition via Object-Scene Interaction","authors":["Hongzhen Li","Youwei Pan","Yingfang Zhu","Sen Zhu","Fengjie Zhu","Leilei Cao"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-06","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏"],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","打光与光影"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏】 + 项目方向【打光与光影】","abstract":"Realistic image composition makes a pasted object appear naturally present in a scene while preserving the pasted layout and fine foreground and background details. Reference-based insertion methods preserve overall object identity but can alter pose and fine details, often at substantial computational cost. Foreground harmonization and shadow generation address individual aspects of this task, but neither alone ensures natural integration. An efficient, unified solution remains an open challenge, and large-scale, high-quality datasets and benchmarks for this task are lacking. To address these challenges, we introduce Paste2Place, an end-to-end, physics-guided flow-based model that learns a direct latent bridge from pasted inputs to realistic composites, guided by image-space corrections analytically derived from predicted physical components of object and scene appearance changes. To support training and evaluation, we introduce P2P-Data with 86K manually reviewed synthetic and real-scene training pairs, including dense physical annotations from our Blender rendering and validation pipeline for the synthetic subset. P2P-Bench combines paired compositions and manually pasted inputs from collected images into 1.5K examples for evaluating composition under user-chosen placements. Extensive qualitative and quantitative experiments demonstrate that Paste2Place achieves substantial advantages in image composition quality and inference speed across diverse scenarios.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09387v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09387v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.09845","arxiv":"2611.09845v1","baseId":"2611.09845","title":"AdaptivePnP: Fast Auto-Tuned Plug-and-Play for Deconvolution","authors":["Haley Duba-Sullivan","Emma J. Reid","Charles A. Bouman","Gregery T. Buzzard"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-06","updated":"2026-11-06","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Plug-and-play (PnP) methods reconstruct images by alternating a data-fitting step with an image denoiser. Their performance depends on two parameters: the step size $γ$ and the measurement noise level $σ$. In practice, $γ$ is often tuned by grid search over validation images and must be retuned whenever the problem setting changes, and $σ$ is assumed known or estimated separately. We propose AdaptivePnP, a PnP framework that jointly estimates $σ$ and $γ$ at every iteration from the data-fitting residual. To do so, we apply the PnP virtual model to the data-fitting proximal map and show that the residual is zero-mean Gaussian with covariance parameterized by $γ$ and $σ$. However, when one parameter is much larger than the other, the residual carries little information about the smaller one and using the maximum likelihood (ML) estimator can degrade the PnP iterations. We therefore introduce the identifiable maximum likelihood (IML) estimator, which restricts the ML estimator to an identifiable window in which the normalized Cramér--Rao bound of every parameter is bounded by a user-specified tolerance. For the virtual model, this window is an interval of ratios $ρ= γ/σ$, so computing the IML estimate reduces to a one-dimensional search over $ρ$. We apply AdaptivePnP to two standard PnP methods, yielding algorithms that require neither a tuned $γ$ nor a known $σ$. On image deblurring and super-resolution, AdaptivePnP matches or outperforms PnP baselines tuned with the true $σ$. Code is available at https://github.com/hdubasullivan/AdaptivePnP.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09845v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.09845v1","arxivCats":["eess.IV","cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-05","count":2,"star5Count":0,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.08393","arxiv":"2611.08393v1","baseId":"2611.08393","title":"Adaptive Low-Light Enhancement for Road Anomaly Detection: A Multi-Architecture Benchmark and Edge Deployment Study","authors":["Obed Honour Eje","Oluwatosin Kolade","Glory Bagai","Taliha Abiodun Folorunso"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-11-05","updated":"2026-11-05","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Road anomaly detection systems on autonomous vehicles must function reliably under degraded visual conditions including darkness, rain, and Harmattan dust haze. This paper benchmarks four object detection architectures, YOLOv8n, YOLOv12n, RT-DETR, and Faster R-CNN, under physically grounded lighting and weather degradation, and shows that uniform application of the SCI low-light enhancement network is actively harmful at dusk-level lighting (-16.1% to -30.7% mAP@50). We propose a lightweight, training-free luminance-adaptive router that selects between no enhancement, moderate SCI, and denoising plus strong SCI based on per-frame Rec. 601 luminance, recovering most of the dusk penalty while preserving low-light gains, and we validate the full pipeline on NVIDIA Jetson Nano edge hardware.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08393v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.08393v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.07665","arxiv":"2611.07665v1","baseId":"2611.07665","title":"CoGenRec: A Unified Generative Framework for Complementary Vision Reconstruction","authors":["Yapeng Meng","Yuguo Chen","Xiangru Chen","Shaojun Xu","Xuezhen Wang","Lijian Wang"],"authorCount":10,"date":"2026-11-05","updated":"2026-11-05","modality":"视频","stars":3,"score":43,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Capturing scenes with extreme motion and high dynamic range is fundamentally constrained by trade-offs among temporal resolution, spatial detail, signal-to-noise ratio, and dynamic range under practical bandwidth, power, and quantum-efficiency limits. Conventional frame-based cameras therefore suffer from temporal undersampling, exposure-induced motion blur, and sensor saturation. The Complementary Vision Sensor (CVS) addresses these limitations by decomposing visual information across two complementary pathways, a spatially dense, temporally sparse, long-exposure RGB pathway and a temporally dense, spatially sparse, short-exposure patiotemporal difference pathway. Their complementary measurements provide appearance, structural, and motion cues across a wide dynamic range that enable reconstruction beyond conventional imaging. We present CoGenRec, a complementarity-driven generative reconstruction framework that unifies video deblurring, high-temporal-resolution frame interpolation, and HDR fusion. CoGenRec encodes complementary measurements into multi-scale spatiotemporal features using exposure-aware appearance modeling and bidirectional temporal propagation. These features constrain a video diffusion model and are injected into the VAE decoder to recover fine structures lost during latent-space compression, combining the scene fidelity of physical measurements with the appearance recovery capabilities of learned video priors. On SportsSloMo-CVS, CoGenRec improves PSNR over the strongest general-purpose baseline by 4.40 dB for deblurring, 3.89 dB for frame interpolation, and 5.04 dB for HDR fusion, respectively. It also achieves strong perceptual quality on 129 unseen scenes captured with real CVS. These results establish a unified approach to advance high-quality reconstruction under challenging motion and illumination conditions. Project page: https://cogenrec.github.io/.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07665v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.07665v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-04","count":4,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":2,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.06422","arxiv":"2611.06422v1","baseId":"2611.06422","title":"Unaligned Image Guided Denoising via Cross-modal Conditional Flow Matching","authors":["Runmin Zhang","Linshan Li","Si-Yuan Cao","Zhu Yu","Lingyu Zhu","Huaqi Zhang"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-04","updated":"2026-11-04","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"This work presents UGD-FM, a novel unaligned image guided denoising framework based on cross-modal conditional flow matching. Unlike previous approaches that assume spatially aligned inputs or only handle small-baseline rectified pairs, we address a more challenging setting where severe target noise and large-range two-dimensional cross-modal misalignment coexist. Specifically, we formulate guided denoising as a progressive flow matching process, allowing image restoration and aligned guidance aggregation to mutually reinforce each other. To support efficient few-step inference, we introduce a stage-focused and inference-consistent training strategy that focuses on task-critical denoising stages, and mitigates the training-inference mismatch. We further propose adaptive sparse guidance propagation for efficient and reliable large-range matching, regularized by perceptual and geometric auxiliary losses. Experiments demonstrate that UGD-FM achieves state-of-the-art denoising performance with only one or two inference steps, and is compatible with modern single-image restoration backbones.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06422v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06422v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.06355","arxiv":"2611.06355v1","baseId":"2611.06355","title":"FlashRelight: Portrait Video Relighting with Dynamic Lighting","authors":["Jiahui Sheng","Le Li","Limin Lin","Jiahao Li","Wansen Feng","Hong Gu"],"authorCount":9,"date":"2026-11-04","updated":"2026-11-04","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":60,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["打光与光影"],"tags":["打光与光影","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【打光与光影】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Portrait video relighting aims to modify the illumination of a portrait video while preserving photorealism and temporal stability. The task becomes particularly challenging under dynamic lighting, where illumination direction, intensity, and color vary over time. To tackle this problem, we introduce FlashRelight, a novel two-stage framework for dynamic portrait video relighting. FlashRelight first suppresses the source illumination through delighting, and then synthesizes the target dynamic lighting on an illumination-neutral portrait video. FlashRelight supports three complementary control modalities: envmap sequences for explicit frame-level lighting control, dynamic lighting text for semantic illumination editing, and lighting reference videos for visual lighting transfer. Training follows a low-cost hybrid strategy that combines static one-light-at-a-time (OLAT) data with generated dynamic-lighting portrait videos, covering both controlled illumination variation and realistic portrait motion. Extensive experiments show that FlashRelight achieves temporally coherent and photorealistic dynamic relighting while enabling flexible cross-video lighting transfer. Code is available at https://github.com/vivoCameraResearch/FlashRelight","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06355v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06355v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.06870","arxiv":"2611.06870v1","baseId":"2611.06870","title":"PixelSpline: Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution via Endpoint-Anchored Shared Pixel Trajectories","authors":["Wanqi Yuan","Nianyi Li"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-04","updated":"2026-11-04","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":55,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Existing continuous space-time video super-resolution methods often infer motion from only two endpoint frames and model the two motion directions independently, making them vulnerable to ambiguous correspondence and inconsistent trajectories. We introduce a continuous STVSR framework that combines long-context encoding, continuous HR feature lifting, and an endpoint-anchored PixelSpline. Long-range observations provide more reliable endpoint representations, while continuous upsampling constructs detailed appearance features directly on the requested HR grid. PixelSpline explicitly models each pixel's motion as a single continuous trajectory shared by both endpoints. Using this trajectory, our method warps endpoint colors into a coarse intermediate frame and aligns the HR features for residual reconstruction. Experiments across spatial and temporal upsampling settings demonstrate that the proposed framework offers a favorable quality--efficiency trade-off.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06870v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06870v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.06042","arxiv":"2611.06042v1","baseId":"2611.06042","title":"UniRestorer++: Scalable Universal Image Restoration with Multi-Granularity Degradation Modeling","authors":["Jingbo Lin","Zhilu Zhang","Fan Li","Renjing Pei","Wenbo Li","Hang Xu"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-11-04","updated":"2026-11-04","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Universal image restoration aims to handle diverse degradations within a single model, but a reliable scaling strategy remains underexplored. Increasing backbone depth and width can lead to performance saturation, while degradation-aware specialization depends on accurate degradation estimation and is vulnerable to errors on out-of-distribution and real-world inputs. We propose UniRestorer++, a scalable framework based on multi-granularity degradation modeling. It progressively refines degradation groups and introduces group-specific experts, coupling capacity growth with specialization. Degradation-estimation-based routing selects a root-to-leaf path, while granularity-estimation-based routing chooses a level along that path to balance specialization and robustness. Spatially dense degradation representation maps further enable region-aware routing for spatially non-uniform corruptions. We develop CDD-50, a benchmark covering 50 degradation categories from single to penta-degradation. Experiments show approximately log-linear PSNR trends over the evaluated model-size range and stronger scaling gains on out-of-distribution and real-world data than vanilla MoE scaling. UniRestorer++ achieves state-of-the-art performance across benchmarks and demonstrates zero-shot generalization and few-shot transfer on real-world images. Code: https://github.com/mrluin/UniRestorer_plusplus","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06042v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06042v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-03","count":3,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.04092","arxiv":"2611.04092v1","baseId":"2611.04092","title":"CHROMA-MILL: Joint Low-Light Enhancement and White Balance under Colored Illumination","authors":["Pol Riubrogent-Comas","David Serrano-Lozano","Maria Pilligua","Michael S. Brown","Ramon Baldrich","Javier Vazquez-Corral"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-11-03","updated":"2026-11-03","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["色彩迁移","画质修复"],"tags":["色彩迁移","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【色彩迁移 · 画质修复】","abstract":"Capturing an image in a low-light environment produces a weak, noisy sensor signal. Low-light image enhancement (LLIE) aims to restore it. Auto white balance (AWB) is also required for every image, and the same noisy signal degrades its ability to infer the scene illuminant. LLIE is typically performed first, followed by AWB, but this strategy remains largely unexamined because existing low-light datasets span a narrow range of illuminants and lack illuminant ground truth for evaluating AWB. We address this with CHROMA-MILL, a RAW dataset of 50 scenes captured with a DSLR and smartphone under four calibrated illuminants at eleven intensity levels. Because light is additive, we blend these captures in the linear RAW domain to synthesize physically valid illuminant mixtures with exact chromaticity ground truth. Benchmarking on this data, we find joint correction is best, and introduce CTI-Retinexformer, which supervises an illumination map, a chromaticity prediction, and an illumination-invariant scene embedding, outperforming the best cascade by 0.7 dB PSNR$_L$ and 1.1 $ΔE_{00}$.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04092v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.04092v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.18690","arxiv":"2611.18690v2","baseId":"2611.18690","title":"Perceptual Weight Space Programming for Diffusion Super-Resolution","authors":["Chenzhi Zheng","Zhuoran Zheng"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-03","updated":"2026-11-19","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Diffusion models for image super-resolution can recover scene content while producing distracting artifacts or unbalanced colors. We study whether a large language model (LLM) can improve perceptual quality by editing a small subset of existing U-Net weights without a clean reference image. The proposed framework provides the editor with architecture code, selected weights, activation summaries, and sampling previews. It permits up to 1,020 candidate trials while limiting accepted changes to three layers and fewer than 0.1\\% of model parameters. The evaluation protocol uses ResShift, SinSR, and SUPIR and compares GPT-6 Astra with a specified Qwen3-VL model under matched access. Feedback metrics guide editing, while separate quality metrics and blinded human comparisons evaluate the final weights. We also report an executed color calibration experiment using real photographs and two small residual networks. This experiment demonstrates correction of a fixed color shift and reveals that an improving feedback score can accompany worse performance on unseen scenes. A separate lightweight super-resolution experiment isolates modest changes in luminance detail while preserving color. The findings motivate restricted updates and independent evaluation of perceptual weight editing.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18690v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.18690v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]},{"id":"daily_2611.04268","arxiv":"2611.04268v1","baseId":"2611.04268","title":"Weight-Space Programming for Improving Image-Restoration Diffusion Models: Technical Conjectures and a Recursive Improvement Loop","authors":["Zhuoran Zheng","Doubao"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-03","updated":"2026-11-03","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Can an LLM directly edit the weights of a diffusion-based image-restoration model to \\emph{improve} restoration quality---without gradients, paired data, or fine-tuning? Recent experiments show frontier LLMs can fix structural bugs by modifying as little as \\(0.016\\%\\) of parameters, but cannot instill new capabilities. We ask whether weight-space programming (WSP) can move from \\emph{debugging} to \\emph{improvement} in the precise-regression setting of image restoration (deblurring, super-resolution, denoising, inpainting). Restoration diffusion UNets present a middle ground: low-level signal-processing layers are concentrated enough for targeted weight-direction edits, while high-level semantic layers remain distributed and resistant to sparse modification. We propose three technical conjectures: (C1) an LLM can localize the layer behind a quality gap (over-smoothing, color shift, hallucinated texture) within a 200 forward-pass budget; (C2) editing a small set of identified weight directions (e.g., scaling high-frequency filter norms, correcting output-bias channels) can measurably improve PSNR and perceptual quality; (C3) a recursive loop---diagnose \\(\\rightarrow\\) edit \\(\\rightarrow\\) evaluate \\(\\rightarrow\\) re-diagnose---can compound gains across rounds, forming a minimal recursive self-improvement (RSI) loop for vision. We analyze the required AI capability (arguing targeted edits need not superintelligence, while general capability generation does) and detail the challenges of precise pixel-level regression. We provide a PyTorch-style deployment pipeline and outline a benchmark. This manuscript is conjectural, intended to open a new subtopic for image restoration.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04268v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.04268v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-11-02","count":5,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.03393","arxiv":"2611.03393v2","baseId":"2611.03393","title":"TaoMate-FVSR: Audio-Guided Facial Video Restoration","authors":["Xinyang Li","Zeran Xu","Jingna Sun","Qijun Gan","Chenwei Zhang","Meiguang Jin"],"authorCount":7,"date":"2026-11-02","updated":"2026-11-03","modality":"视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【视频】","abstract":"Low-quality facial videos suffer from missing fine details, while AI-generated videos may additionally contain implausible teeth and lip movements that do not match the speech. Restoring the mouth region is particularly challenging: enhancing existing details can preserve structural errors, whereas regenerating them risks changing facial identity and articulation. We propose an audio-guided facial video restoration framework that reconstructs unreliable mouth details and aligns lip movements with the speech while preserving facial identity and temporal consistency. Built on a video diffusion transformer, our framework mixes input video latents with noise to retain useful source information while allowing corrupted details to be regenerated. Audio guides lip movements, reference images constrain facial appearance, and previously generated frames promote temporal consistency. We train the model in two stages, first learning visual restoration from degraded images and videos, then learning audio-guided lip correction from degraded dubbed videos. The resulting model supports both restoration with the original audio and visual dubbing with replacement audio. On talking-head restoration benchmarks, our method achieves the lowest LPIPS and DISTS and the best lip synchronization scores among the evaluated restoration baselines. Qualitative results on AI-generated videos further show improvements in facial detail and mouth-region appearance, including clearer tooth boundaries and lip contours.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03393v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03393v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.02786","arxiv":"2611.02786v1","baseId":"2611.02786","title":"Local-Global Joint Modeling for Point Cloud Super-Resolution Guided by Multi-Scale Spatial Priors","authors":["Wang Yang","Wenyi Ge"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-11-02","updated":"2026-11-02","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Range-image-based point cloud super-resolution provides an effective way to reconstruct sparse outdoor point clouds by projecting low-resolution LiDAR scans into a regular 2D representation. However, current methods still face three challenges. First, range images exhibit pronounced vertical nonuniformity, with sparse observations in distant regions and denser observations in nearby regions. Some cross-scale fusion mechanisms do not account for the varying reliability of features at different vertical positions, allowing invalid values and noise from distant sparse regions to propagate into the decoder. Second, a single convolutional or sequence-modeling architecture often struggles to preserve both local high-frequency geometric details and global long-range dependencies, resulting in over-smoothed reconstructions. Third, smooth transition depths near foreground-background boundaries can produce spike artifacts when the 2D range image is back-projected into 3D. To address these issues, we propose a point cloud super-resolution method based on multi-scale spatial priors and joint local-global modeling. The design comprises a spatially gated fusion module, a multi-granularity local-global cooperative perception module, and 2D median-residual denoising. Experiments on KITTI-360 and nuScenes demonstrate effective reconstruction, with IoU scores of 0.4703 and 0.3593, respectively, supporting the feasibility of the proposed method for range-image super-resolution.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02786v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.02786v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.02862","arxiv":"2611.02862v1","baseId":"2611.02862","title":"Performance evaluation of super-resolution techniques for characterization and phenotyping of commodity crops using multispectral imagery","authors":["Abhiram Karukayil","João F. C. Mota","Mateo Vásquez-Guevara","Tito Arevalo-Ramirez","Fernando Auat Cheein"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-02","updated":"2026-11-02","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Multispectral imagery provides spectral information beyond the visible range for vegetation monitoring, but it typically suffers from limited spatial resolution, which can obscure fine crop structures and affect vegetation-index (VI) estimates.} This study evaluates whether single-image super-resolution (SISR) methods developed for red, green, and blue (RGB) imagery can enhance the spatial resolution of red, green, and near-infrared (RGN) crop images while preserving spectral fidelity. Eight pretrained methods are applied directly to RGN images without fine-tuning: bicubic and nearest-neighbor interpolation, Real-ESRGAN, HAT, HiT-SR, SRFormer, ResShift, and StableSR. Reconstruction at $4\\times$ and $16\\times$ is evaluated for whole images and manually segmented leaf regions. Reconstruction quality is assessed using mean absolute error (MAE), peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), structural similarity index (SSIM), spectral angle mapper (SAM), and erreur relative globale adimensionnelle de synthèse (ERGAS). Eight VIs, computed from reference and super-resolved images, are evaluated using MAE, PSNR, and SSIM. Transformer-based methods \\modification{significantly} outperform GAN- and diffusion-based approaches in both evaluation settings \\modification{($p < 0.001$)}, with \\modification{HAT, HiT-SR, and SRFormer forming the highest-performing statistical tier}. These findings indicate that RGB-trained SISR can enhance spatial detail in RGN crop imagery while maintaining spectral consistency and VI reliability for high-resolution crop phenotyping.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02862v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.02862v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.03390","arxiv":"2611.03390v2","baseId":"2611.03390","title":"Progressive Multi-Scale Super-Resolution via Cascaded Bicubic Anchors and Inter-Scale Mutual Refinement","authors":["Chun-An Lin","Yu-Chia Chang","Chi-Mao Fan","Yen-Chieh Ouyang","Tsung-Jung Liu"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-02","updated":"2026-11-16","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Super-resolution networks are trained for one magnification factor, so several output resolutions require several models. We propose PMS-SR, a progressive single-input multiple-output network that produces x2, x4, and x8 results in one forward pass, with a Transformer backbone on the low-resolution grid. In Stage 1, each scale is predicted as a residual over the detached bicubic upsampling of the previous scale's prediction. With Dirac and zero initialization, training starts exactly from the bicubic cascade, and each head adds only the band the previous scale lacks. In Stage 2, inter-scale mutual refinement couples the scales in both directions through space-to-depth feedback, deformable alignment, and per-pixel fusion, so x8 features refine the x2 output. Trained on DF2K, the 7.95M-parameter model achieves a higher PSNR than every compared multi-scale method at x4 and x8 on all five standard benchmarks. At x8, it outperforms the x8-specific DRN-L on all of them, leads all compared methods on Urban100, and is within 0.13 dB of the best result elsewhere. Against three single-scale SwinIR-M models, PMS-SR uses 22% of the parameters, runs 2.9x faster on BSD100 inputs, and its outputs agree 6.7-12.4 dB more closely. Code and pretrained models will be released at https://github.com/julian135707/PMS-SR upon acceptance.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03390v2","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03390v2","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG","eess.IV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.03120","arxiv":"2611.03120v1","baseId":"2611.03120","title":"KAUSTics-Net: Diffusion-Based Through-Water UAV Restoration with a Semi-Synthetic Dataset","authors":["Dario Scilla","Omar A. Lopez","Kasper Johansen","Brian O. Nieuwenhuis","Matthew F. McCabe"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-11-02","updated":"2026-11-02","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Accurate mapping of shallow-water environments using unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) imagery is challenged by water-induced distortions, including refraction, caustics, and wavelength-dependent attenuation. The scarcity of paired distorted and reference imagery further limits the development of supervised restoration methods. To address this challenge, we introduce KAUSTics, a semi-synthetic dataset combining 1,280 reference patches derived from 32 high-resolution UAV images with 12,800 degraded observations generated through Blender-based water-surface rendering and empirical color and blur augmentations. We further propose KAUSTics-Net, a pixel-space conditional diffusion framework integrating a multi-scale conditional encoder, feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM), cross-attention, and a composite reconstruction objective to restore shallow-water imagery. We evaluated the framework against eight image-restoration methods, complemented by architectural ablations and cross-dataset experiments. On KAUSTics, KAUSTics-Net achieved a mean peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) of 31.715 +/- 0.439 dB, structural similarity index (SSIM) of 0.849 +/- 0.002, and learned perceptual image patch similarity (LPIPS) of 0.145 +/- 0.007 across three training seeds. On the independent Sea-Undistort dataset, sampler-aware fine-tuning improved restoration fidelity, reaching 19.469 +/- 0.332 dB PSNR and 0.554 +/- 0.005 SSIM under combined water-surface distortions, although perceptual trade-offs remained. Additional experiments on real UAV imagery examined feature preservation and segmentation behavior. These findings demonstrate the potential of semi-synthetic training and conditional diffusion for shallow-water image restoration while highlighting the importance of evaluating both reconstruction fidelity and downstream remote-sensing utility.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03120v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.03120v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-10-31","count":2,"star5Count":0,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2611.00523","arxiv":"2611.00523v1","baseId":"2611.00523","title":"SASFormer: Stage-Aware Selective Attention Transformer for All-in-One Blind Image Restoration","authors":["Xiangpeng Tian","Mingyang Li","Fajing Liu","Xiaofeng Wang","Xiaohai He","Chao Ren"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-10-31","updated":"2026-10-31","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"All-in-one image restoration aims to recover high-quality images from multiple degradation types using a single unified model. Recent methods improve unified restoration with hybrid attention, prompt learning, or routing mechanisms, but generally focus on enhancing degradation adaptivity while employing similar feature modeling strategies across network stages. Consequently, the distinct representation requirements of encoding and decoding under diverse degradations remain insufficiently explored. In this paper, we propose SASFormer, a Stage-Aware Selective Attention Transformer for all-in-one blind image restoration. SASFormer follows a stage-aware design principle: the encoder uses established hybrid channel-spatial attention to build robust content-preserving and degradation-aware representations, while the decoder employs adaptive selective attention for restoration-oriented reconstruction. To address the stage-specific requirements of decoder reconstruction, we develop Adaptive Selective Channel Self-Attention (ASCSA) and Adaptive Selective Spatial Self-Attention (ASSSA), enabling the decoder to dynamically identify restoration-relevant channel responses and spatial contexts. Specifically, ASCSA performs adaptive query-channel selection and attention masking, while ASSSA adaptively integrates spatial attention across multiple window sizes. Compact implicit prompts further modulate the selection process without requiring explicit degradation labels. Extensive experiments on 3-, 5-, and 11-degradation settings demonstrate that SASFormer achieves strong restoration performance with favorable parameter efficiency. Moreover, stage-wise representation analysis and ablation studies provide empirical support for the proposed stage-aware design and the effectiveness of decoder-side adaptive selection.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00523v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00523v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.00963","arxiv":"2611.00963v1","baseId":"2611.00963","title":"4K Image Super-Resolution on Mobile NPUs: Mobile AI 2026 Challenge Report","authors":["Andrey Ignatov","Georgy Perevozchikov","Radu Timofte","Fubin Ren","Shihao Wang","Liwen Zhang"],"authorCount":46,"date":"2026-10-31","updated":"2026-10-31","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Image super-resolution is widely used on mobile devices for upscaling various types of image data. This creates a need for efficient deep learning-based models that are not only performant but can also be deployed on neural processing units (NPUs) present in the majority of mobile and IoT chipsets. In this Mobile AI challenge, we address this problem and propose the participants to design fast solutions that can upsample images from Full HD to 4K resolution while running on popular mobile NPUs from Qualcomm and MediaTek. For this, the participants were provided with the DIV2K dataset and trained models to do an efficient 3X image upscaling. The competition had two tracks: in the first track, the models had to be fully quantized (INT8), and the runtime of all models was evaluated on the Qualcomm Snapdragon 8 Elite Gen 5 NPU. In the second track, the participants were developing floating-point solutions that were evaluated on the MediaTek Dimensity 9500 NPU. Top solutions proposed in this challenge are able to reconstruct 4K images under 5 milliseconds while reaching a PSNR score of 30 dB. A comprehensive description of the models developed in the challenge is provided in this paper.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00963v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00963v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.AI"]}]},{"date":"2026-10-30","count":3,"star5Count":0,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2610.41258","arxiv":"2610.41258v1","baseId":"2610.41258","title":"PBSR: Pseudo-Burst Super-Resolution via Zero-Shot Novel View Synthesis","authors":["Yuki Kondo","Norimichi Ukita"],"authorCount":2,"date":"2026-10-30","updated":"2026-10-30","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Burst super-resolution (BSR) achieves higher reconstruction fidelity than single-image super-resolution (SISR) by aggregating sub-pixel information from multiple frames. However, it requires a burst of frames captured under near-static conditions, and such frames are unavailable for single photographs or archival images. We propose Pseudo-Burst Super-Resolution (PBSR), which brings BSR-style reconstruction to the single-image input setting by synthesizing pseudo-burst frames via zero-shot novel view synthesis (NVS). PBSR first generates pseudo-burst frames with realistic parallax using a viewpoint sampling strategy calibrated to the pixel-shift distribution of real burst datasets. The frames are then processed by a BSR backbone equipped with a geometry-aware alignment module that reuses the NVS camera parameters and estimated depth. While the NVS stage is zero-shot, the backbone is fine-tuned with an artifact-aware adaptation so that it can exploit the synthesized frames despite their generative artifacts. Experiments on the BurstSR dataset show that PBSR consistently improves in PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS over the single-frame baseline with the same reconstruction backbone.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41258v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41258v1","arxivCats":["eess.IV","cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2611.00192","arxiv":"2611.00192v1","baseId":"2611.00192","title":"VC-1K: A Diverse Video Test Set for Real-Time Communication","authors":["Yu-Chen Sun","Jie Dong","Sergey Sablin","Hassene Tmar","Sriram Srinivasan"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-10-30","updated":"2026-10-30","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"We introduce Video Conferencing 1000 (VC-1K), an open-source test set comprising approximately 1,000 video sequences specifically designed for evaluating video processing and encoding pipelines in real-time communication (RTC) scenarios. Unlike existing test sets that primarily rely on studio-produced or screen content, VC-1K consists of user-generated video call content captured on consumer mobile devices under a wide variety of conditions. The dataset predominantly features talking-head sequences with distinct faces, but also includes a small subset of outward-facing, rear-camera videos without faces to reflect additional common RTC use cases. It spans a wide range of real-world variables including device type, lighting conditions, motion levels, indoor/outdoor environments, and demographic diversity. All video sequences are accompanied by synchronous audio to facilitate multimodal research. We detail the dataset's design methodology and statistical distribution, and demonstrate its utility through a comparative encoder evaluation of OpenH264, SVT-AV1, and libaom under constant bitrate (CBR) encoding at resolutions typical of RTC deployments. Furthermore, two super-resolution approaches, RealESRGAN and GFPGAN, are evaluated on VC-1K, highlighting the dataset as a useful benchmark for pre- and post-processing research. VC-1K is intended to serve as a publicly available dataset for the video RTC research community.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.00192v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.00192v1","arxivCats":["eess.IV","cs.CV","cs.MM"]},{"id":"daily_2610.41501","arxiv":"2610.41501v1","baseId":"2610.41501","title":"VoxLution: Evolving Voxels for High-Fidelity 3D Scene Generation","authors":["Daoyi Gao","Xinyi Zhang","Alexey Bokhovkin","Lei Li","Angela Dai"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-10-30","updated":"2026-10-30","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":40,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"High-resolution 3D indoor scene generation is crucial for immersive applications such as virtual reality, spatial computing, and robotics simulation. We introduce VoxLution, a coarse-to-fine transformer-based approach that generates high-fidelity indoor scenes with rich details, overcoming the overly smooth geometry typical of existing generative models. First, we generate a low-resolution spatial layout; then, based on this structural information, we progressively refine the scene geometry locally in chunks. Key to our approach is formulating 3D super-resolution as a sequence modeling problem over multi-resolution sparse voxel grids. To achieve this, we introduce a training-free, resolution-invariant tokenization scheme that compactly encodes the geometric information of eight octants into a single decimal number. We formulate the transformer sequence to include multiple resolutions, allowing the coarse layout to guide the generation of fine local details. To ensure coherence across local chunks, we incorporate adjacent chunk boundary information directly into the transformer sequencing. VoxLution achieves scene generation at a finer voxel resolution than existing baselines, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance on standard benchmarks with 59.6% lower FPD and 51.1% lower FID on 3D-FRONT, and 70.5% lower FPD and 73.8% lower FID on Scannet++.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.41501v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.41501v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-10-29","count":3,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":0,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2610.39517","arxiv":"2610.39517v1","baseId":"2610.39517","title":"FFAI: Flow Matching with Feedback Training and Adaptive Initialization for CT Reconstruction","authors":["Jigang Duan","Heran Wang","Genwei Ma","Xing Zhao"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-10-29","updated":"2026-10-29","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Although low-dose, sparse-view, and limited-angle CT involve distinct degradation mechanisms, all three pose reconstruction challenges due to limited measurement information and can therefore be addressed within a unified framework. In iterative image restoration with flow matching, prediction errors may cause inference states to deviate from the prescribed training path, while a fixed initialization may be unsuitable for inputs with varying degradation severity. We propose a unified conditional flow matching method that conditions on the initial reconstruction and combines feedback-aware training with degradation-adaptive initialization. During training, a teacher network simulates inference updates to generate shifted states, from which a student network learns to recover high-quality reference images, improving its ability to handle prediction errors. During inference, the starting time is selected based on the noise level or extent of missing projection data, and the noise added to the initial state is adjusted accordingly to adapt the starting point to the input. Experiments on public benchmark datasets show that the proposed method achieves better quantitative results than the compared methods across all three reconstruction tasks, effectively suppressing noise and artifacts while preserving anatomical structures and details.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39517v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39517v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2610.39617","arxiv":"2610.39617v1","baseId":"2610.39617","title":"UniTele: Learning Device-Conditioned Degradations for Unified Smartphone Telephoto Super-Resolution","authors":["Yuchen Zhang","Aojie Li","Wei Cheng","Xianfang Zeng","Runxing Liu","haixin hao"],"authorCount":8,"date":"2026-10-29","updated":"2026-10-29","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Super-telephoto imaging on mobile phones suffers from complex, device-dependent degradations that are poorly captured by conventional handcrafted pipelines. Collecting large-scale aligned training pairs across different phones and diverse scenes, however, is both costly and difficult to scale. We therefore study whether device-specific degradations can be learned from only a small number of physical captures and transferred for training a unified super-resolution (SR) model. Our key idea is to factor degradation learning into two learnable components: one captures the device-specific imaging characteristics under well-resolved conditions, while the other captures the additional information loss and artifacts that arise in super-telephoto imaging. We build a paired benchmark across three phone brands (Huawei, Apple, and vivo) by photographing the same content at two display sizes and zoom settings. The larger displayed image is captured at the phone's maximum optical zoom setting, while the smaller image is photographed directly at the phone's highest native zoom setting through the phone's native imaging pipeline. The benchmark contains 519 training and 60 held-out pairs. We use the benchmark to learn device-specific degradation, then apply it to external images to synthesize thousands of additional pairs to train our UniTele SR model. Relative to the pretrained Qwen and FLUX backbones before adaptation, the full UniTele training recipe substantially improves paired restoration fidelity and perceptual similarity on the held-out benchmark and yields markedly more stable and visually faithful restorations.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39617v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39617v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2610.39972","arxiv":"2610.39972v1","baseId":"2610.39972","title":"Blind Video Denoising for Vocal Tract rtMRI using a Self-Supervised Ensemble","authors":["Julian Andres Hernandez-Potes","Tomás Arias-Vergara","Daiqi Liu","Andreas Maier","Paula Andrea Perez-Toro"],"authorCount":5,"date":"2026-10-29","updated":"2026-10-29","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Real-time magnetic resonance imaging of the vocal tract makes speech articulation directly observable, but the trade-off between temporal resolution and signal quality introduces substantial noise and obscures fine articulatory detail. Since clean references cannot be acquired for a moving vocal tract, supervised denoising approaches are not applicable in this setting. We propose a self-supervised denoising framework that combines spatiotemporal super-resolution learning with pseudo-supervision derived from a variance-stabilized non-local means filter. A dual-model fusion strategy balances noise suppression and preservation of high-frequency articulatory structure. Experiments on the USC-75 multi-speaker dataset under natural noise conditions show that the ensemble raises the signal-to-noise ratio by 11.9 dB on unseen subjects while attaining more high-frequency articulatory content in the frames. Among tuned implementations of anisotropic diffusion, block-matching filtering, non-local means and pre-smoothed non-local means, every configuration that achieves comparable noise suppression does so at the cost of high-frequency content or of structural fidelity. Under severe added Gaussian, Rayleigh and Rician noise, peak signal-to-noise ratio improves by 7.6 to 11.8 dB and structural similarity roughly triples. An ablation shows that the variance-stabilized pseudo-supervision is an essential element for denoising, and that the effect is attributable to the hard thresholding contained in the stabilization transform.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39972v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39972v1","arxivCats":["eess.IV","cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-10-28","count":4,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2610.39001","arxiv":"2610.39001v1","baseId":"2610.39001","title":"AlbumVSR: Identity-Preserving Face Video Super-Resolution from a Personal Photo Album","authors":["Nianyao Du","Xijun Wang","Stanley H. Chan"],"authorCount":3,"date":"2026-10-28","updated":"2026-10-28","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复","表情与人像"],"tags":["少步蒸馏","训练范式","画质修复","表情与人像","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【少步蒸馏 · 训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复 · 表情与人像】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Severe blur, downsampling, noise, and compression can erase the identity cues needed to faithfully restore a person's face. We therefore use photographs of the same person as an external source of identity. AlbumVSR conditions a frozen, one-step streaming diffusion transformer on a personal album. We use four MagFace/CLIP set tokens to encode the album, and 64 texture tokens which come from a fused UV atlas rendered at the current window's estimated face pose. These tokens are appended to the transformer's cached cross-attention context. On an identity-disjoint VoxCeleb2 benchmark with heavy synthetic degradation, AlbumVSR raises ArcFace similarity to the ground truth from 0.586 to 0.670 and face PSNR from 24.98 to 26.71 dB. On the 91 test identities, its mean identity similarity is higher than that of all 14 baselines.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.39001v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.39001v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","eess.IV"]},{"id":"daily_2610.38499","arxiv":"2610.38499v1","baseId":"2610.38499","title":"Residual Motion Splatting for Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution","authors":["Vincent Herfeld","Eva Coupet{é}","Julien Mairal","Jean Ponce"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-10-28","updated":"2026-10-28","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":55,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Continuous space-time video super-resolution produces a high-resolution, highframe-rate video from a low-resolution, low-frame-rate one at arbitrary spatial scales and temporal instants. Classical spatial multi-frame super-resolution has shown that detail beyond a single frame's sampling limit can be recovered by aligning complementary observations with sub-pixel precision and accumulating them on a finer grid. Existing learning-based methods largely depart from this principle: some align and fuse frames only at low resolution before regressing a continuous high-resolution representation, while others interpolate each frame to the output resolution before splatting. In both cases, the per-frame samples are resampled before fusion, so their sub-pixel positions are lost before frames are combined. Restoring this principle in a learned continuous model raises two challenges: estimating sub-pixel motion at unobserved times, and handling holes and occlusions when splatting a coarse grid onto a finer one. We address the first by predicting per-pixel continuous trajectories that refine linear optical-flow paths with smooth residual motion, using features integrated over the full video sequence. We address the second by directly splatting consecutive low-resolution features in a learned latent space onto a high-resolution grid at their predicted sub-pixel positions with predicted visibility weights, before a decoder resolves remaining ambiguities. This makes temporal interpolation and multi-frame superresolution a single operation. Our method improves both reconstruction fidelity and perceptual quality on multiple standard benchmarks over the state of the art, while running up to an order of magnitude faster.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38499v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.38499v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2610.38121","arxiv":"2610.38121v1","baseId":"2610.38121","title":"What Does LOL Measure? Hidden Label Shifts in Low-Light Enhancement Benchmarks","authors":["Adityan Thirumani Srinivasan"],"authorCount":1,"date":"2026-10-28","updated":"2026-10-28","modality":"图像","stars":3,"score":51,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"LOL-v1 and LOLv2-Real are the standard benchmarks for supervised low-light image enhancement, and several leading methods ship hand-set test-time adjustments with their evaluation scripts. We audit both benchmarks with 18 public checkpoints of eight methods under one metric implementation that reproduces every published number to within 0.09 dB, and find that their test labels differ from their training labels. (i) The 15 LOL-v1 test labels are rendered differently: the same captures appear in LOLv2 with labels of identical luminance but 1.48x lower saturation, and LOL-v1's own training labels match the LOLv2 rendering; we confirm this on the official release. (ii) The LOLv2-Real test set is the contiguous block of images 690-789, whose labels are 14% darker than the training labels (Cliff's delta = -0.45, p < 1e-12) with no comparable shift in the input statistics we examined; methods trained without any labels score up to 5.5 dB higher on test than on train. (iii) All 15 LOL-v1 test inputs reappear in LOLv2-Real, 12 in its training set. The released test-time settings are consistent with compensating for these shifts. Separating what can be calibrated on training data from what only test-set tuning finds, the test-only residual has median 0.00 dB on LOLv2-Syn, which has no shift, but 0.37 dB on LOL-v1 and 0.92 dB on LOLv2-Real (up to 3.9 dB). Under the shift, test-set checkpoint selection inflates a from-scratch model by 0.9-2.0 dB, and removing the released settings changes which method ranks first. We give concrete reporting recommendations.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38121v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.38121v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV","cs.LG","eess.IV"]},{"id":"daily_2610.38507","arxiv":"2610.38507v1","baseId":"2610.38507","title":"UnMixMamba: Unmixing Guided State Space Subpixel Mapping of Crops from MODIS Time Series","authors":["Motasem Alkayid","Yimin Zhu","Naser El-Sheimy","Lincoln Linlin Xu"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-10-28","updated":"2026-10-28","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Subpixel mapping (SPM) of crop types from \\SI{250}{m} Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) time series to field-scale Agriculture and Agri-Food Canada (AAFC) labels supports acreage estimation and agricultural monitoring. Three difficulties remain: (i)~spectral-temporal coupling and mixed pixels that break direct coarse-to-fine classifiers; (ii)~intra-class endmember variability that a single spectrum per crop cannot capture; and (iii)~uniform refinement on the high-resolution (HR) grid, which treats every subpixel the same, so most of the effort goes to easy pixels while errors remain in the minority class. UnMixMamba addresses them as follows. For~(i), a temporal-spectral-spatial decoupled (TSSD) factorization estimates simplex fractions and drives AAFC classification from the same mixture field, instead of mapping low-resolution (LR) observations directly to HR logits. For~(ii), \\(\\times 8\\) abundance super-resolution and a learnable per-crop variant endmember bank reconstruct the MODIS cube at LR and HR under a linear mixing model. For~(iii), the predicted crop fractions decide where a fixed Mamba token budget is spent, so HR refinement focuses on the minority class instead of the full grid. On Alberta 2015 under a fixed spatial-block protocol, UnMixMamba reaches OA 0.8397, mIoU 0.6640, AA 0.7837, and Kappa 0.7715, ahead of ST-DRes, ESPCN, FineCrop, AgriFM, and classical SPM baselines. The task is closed-set four-crop mapping inside a known agricultural mask.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38507v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.38507v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]},{"date":"2026-10-27","count":3,"star5Count":1,"star4Count":1,"papers":[{"id":"daily_2610.37802","arxiv":"2610.37802v1","baseId":"2610.37802","title":"Flow Matching for Fluctuation-Based Fluorescence Super-Resolution","authors":["Alberto Cozzani","Giacomo Meanti","Laure Blanc-Féraud","Luca Calatroni"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-10-27","updated":"2026-10-27","modality":"图像","stars":5,"score":100,"coreTags":["训练范式"],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["训练范式","画质修复"],"reason":"命中 通用核心【训练范式】 + 项目方向【画质修复】","abstract":"Fluctuation-based super-resolution techniques are an appealing alternative to single-molecule and structured-illumination microscopy, as they adapt to standard acquisition settings and exploit the independent stochastic fluctuations of fluorophores across a sequence of frames. However, most existing approaches lack an explicit model of the acquisition process and provide only relative intensity estimates, limiting quantitative analyses. In this work, we combine model-based and generative approaches to exploit the full stochastic information in the sequence, proposing a reconstruction framework built on flow matching and a double-Poisson forward model accounting for blinking, blur, under-sampling and noise. The method alternates two steps: a flow matching model first learns the distribution of the acquired data; then, the sample and the background are jointly estimated by minimising a Kullback-Leibler divergence between observed and simulated distributions via regularised proximal gradient steps. On simulated and real Argolight data, the method resolves lines separated by 120 nm, outperforming SACD and eSRRF.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37802v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.37802v1","arxivCats":["eess.IV","math.OC"]},{"id":"daily_2610.37290","arxiv":"2610.37290v1","baseId":"2610.37290","title":"Taming Noise Initialization for Zero-shot Video Restoration with Image Diffusion Priors","authors":["Chong Wang","Zixuan Fu","Hao Cheng","Siyuan Yang","Alex C. Kot","Bihan Wen"],"authorCount":6,"date":"2026-10-27","updated":"2026-10-27","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":4,"score":55,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"Recent advances in diffusion models have achieved remarkable success in restoring high-quality images in a zero-shot manner. Unfortunately, extending this success to video restoration is challenging due to the substantial computational resources required to pre-train video diffusion models. A rising trend seeks to address video restoration by utilizing a pre-trained image diffusion model, initiating restoration from either frame-wise independent or identical Gaussian noise. Yet, these approaches often fall short, as such simple noise initiations fail to capture and utilize the inherent temporal correlations within degraded video frames. In this work, we propose taming noise initialization for zero-shot video restoration, which preserves the intrinsic temporal correlations by generating initial noises directly using DDIM inversion on the corrupted video frames themselves. Building on this, we introduce an adaptive noise blending strategy that suppresses degradation patterns encoded during DDIM inversion while gradually steering the noise toward a Gaussian distribution. To further emphasize temporal consistency, we propose measurement-guided trajectory attention to better align features across frames. Extensive experiments on multiple video restoration tasks demonstrate that our method outperforms existing zero-shot approaches, consistently achieving superior results on the DAVIS, REDS4, and Set8 datasets.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37290v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.37290v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]},{"id":"daily_2610.36781","arxiv":"2610.36781v1","baseId":"2610.36781","title":"CVBL-CCTV-Face Dataset for Real-World Face Enhancement and Recognition in Surveillance","authors":["Manish Das","Ajay Kumar Yadav","Shiv Ram Dubey","Satish Kumar Singh"],"authorCount":4,"date":"2026-10-27","updated":"2026-10-27","modality":"图像 & 视频","stars":3,"score":44,"coreTags":[],"domainTags":["画质修复"],"tags":["画质修复","视频方向"],"reason":"命中 项目方向【画质修复】 + 模态【图像 & 视频】","abstract":"In real-world surveillance settings, facial captures from closed-circuit television systems suffer from degradations such as low resolution, motion blur, noise, and occlusions, which undermine dependable face recognition. In this study, we propose a framework for super-resolution and identity recognition, aimed at surveillance applications. For this purpose, we present the CVBL-CCTV-Face Dataset which is a dataset of real-world images taken in an unpaired manner where each pair consists of a low-quality (LQ) image of a face captured by a 8MP IP Dome CCTV camera together with a high-quality (HQ) reference image that is taken in controlled settings. In order to cope with the various problems posed by such datasets, we construct a modular system that involves face detection, deep learning-based super-resolution of low-quality face images, and identity recognition through L2-normalized embeddings extracted by an embedding model. Enhancement models are trained and tested according to three different setups, and the system is deployed for identity verification in a real-time unconstrained environment.","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36781v1","pdf":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.36781v1","arxivCats":["cs.CV"]}]}]};
